## Estimating domain model: Assembly, Estimate Item, Cost Component

The CSI Estimate workflow (`app/core/estimates/`, tools covered in "MCP tool catalog"
below) mirrors how a human estimator actually builds a bid — not a flat list of priced
takeoff rows. Three concepts, smallest to largest:

- **Cost Component** (`EstimateCostComponent`, `app/models/estimate.py`) — the true
  atom. `cost_type` is one of `material | labor | equipment | subcontract | other`: a
  quantity, a unit, a rate, nothing more. A component can never stand alone on a bid —
  "500 SF of gypsum board" or "40 labor-hours" means nothing without the installed work
  it belongs to. `scope` is `direct` (nested under one `estimate_item_id`) or `project`
  (a project-wide cost with no `estimate_item_id`, written only via `write_project_cost`).
- **Estimate Item** (`EstimateItem`) — the smallest thing that IS a sellable, priceable
  unit of real installed work: one CSI-coded crew task ("Hang and finish 5/8" GWB, Wall
  Type A, 2nd floor corridor"), never a bare material, bare labor hours, or a category
  header. It carries `csi_code`/`csi_title`/`quantity`/`unit` and rolls up its nested
  Cost Components' prices into a total — it never gets its own separate price. This is
  the unit `propose_estimate_structure` writes and `get_estimate_structure` reads back.
- **Assembly** — a named system grouping several Estimate Items together (e.g. "Wall
  Type A" = framing + board + finish + insulation), for the estimator's mental model
  only — its total is just the sum of its Estimate Items, it's never priced directly,
  within the `EstimateItem`/`EstimateCostComponent` graph itself: `EstimateItem` has no
  `assembly` column, and nothing groups multiple `EstimateItem` rows by a shared
  assembly identity inside `app/core/estimates/`. The only `assembly` field inside that
  graph is a free-text string on `TakeoffLineItem`, populated from the takeoff CSV's
  `assembly`/`category`/`system`/`wall_type` column (`app/core/ingestion/parsers.py`'s
  `_ASSEMBLY_HEADERS`) and surfaced read-only via `EstimateSourceRowOut` for context —
  treat *that* as a reasoning aid, not a queryable grouping.

  **Correction — a real Assembly recipe library now exists as a separate system,
  contradicting older language in this section that called Assembly "not a first-class
  entity" without qualification.** `app/models/assembly.py` (`Assembly` →
  `AssemblyVersion` → `AssemblyVersionItem` → `AssemblyVersionComponent`, mirroring the
  same mutable-identity/immutable-published-version split as price-book Material) plus
  full CRUD/publish/retire/component-mutation service (`app/core/assemblies/service.py`)
  and REST API (`app/api/assemblies.py`: `POST/GET/PATCH /api/assemblies`, `.../versions`,
  `.../versions/{id}/publish`, `.../versions/{id}/retire`, `.../versions/{id}/components`,
  plus hybrid ILIKE+`pg_trgm` search via `GET /api/assemblies?q=`) are implemented and
  documented in full in `docs/assemblies.md`, whose own header states status:
  "implemented and verified on the Neon dev branch `pricebook-dev`... not yet migrated
  to production, no MCP tools yet." So as of this pass: **creation, publishing, and
  searching are built**; only **recipe expansion is still genuinely unbuilt** — nothing
  in `app/core/assemblies/` applies/instantiates a published `AssemblyVersion` into
  actual `EstimateItem`/`EstimateCostComponent` rows, and it isn't wired into any
  MCP tool yet, so the chat agent cannot use it. `app/models/estimate.py`'s
  `estimate_version_assembly_instances` table already reserves a lineage FK toward this
  system for when that expansion step lands. **Also unresolved, not verified this
  pass**: `Assembly`/`AssemblyVersion` carry a nullable `org_id` but no `user_id` column
  at all — unclear whether this is deliberate (a shared, company-wide recipe library,
  the same posture price-book `Material` had before the `3698854` cross-tenant leak fix
  scoped it — see "Authentication and multi-tenancy" below) or an oversight that
  reintroduces that exact bug in a newer system. Check before trusting `app/api/
  assemblies.py` against multi-tenant data.

Hierarchy:
```
Assembly ("Wall Type A")                    -- grouping label, never priced itself
 └─ Estimate Item ("Hang 5/8" GWB, Wall A")  -- the atomic sellable unit, ONE price
     ├─ Cost Component: material  (cost_type=material)
     ├─ Cost Component: labor     (cost_type=labor)
     └─ Cost Component: equipment/subcontract/other, if needed
```

### Additive V2 estimate core

`app/core/estimates/` and `app/models/estimate.py` contain a backend-only V2
foundation built alongside V1. Existing projects default to `estimate_schema_version=1`
and are never migrated implicitly. V2 exposes immutable whole-estimate versions as the
business concept, but persists each version as a complete lightweight manifest over
project-scoped logical identities and copy-on-write item/component revisions. Unchanged
revisions are shared across commits and cloned scenarios. V2 also provides relational
locations/dimensions, joint allocation slices, explicit labor bases, selected pricing
calculations, waste state, typed changesets, and metric-grain reporting. Units are
**not** a V2-owned concept: `estimate_v2.py` used to define its own `Unit`/`UnitAlias`
mapped to the `units` table, which collided with price-book's own `Unit`/`UnitAlias`
(`app/models/units.py`, the live, callers-having version) also mapped to that same
table — that duplicate mapping broke `app.main` import outright. V2 now imports
price-book's `Unit` directly (`app/core/estimates/calculations.py`'s
`convert_quantity`, `.../service.py`'s unit resolution, both via
`app.core.price_book.units.resolve_unit`) rather than owning a parallel controlled-unit
system. There are intentionally no V2 REST routes, MCP tools, agent workflows, price-book
adapters, frontend consumers, or existing-project backfill.

**How an Estimate Item actually gets derived** — the reasoning `propose_estimate_structure`
expects before it's called, one takeoff row at a time, three questions in order:

1. **Where is this?** Locate the takeoff quantity on the drawings — which wall, room,
   floor — using one batched `retrieve_project_evidence` request, then
   `inspect_document_page` for a verified, page-level read only when indexed evidence is
   insufficient. This is what tells you the assembly/wall-type context.
2. **What does it require?** Retrieve the spec evidence and a bounded structural window
   (`retrieve_project_evidence` then `get_document_context`) — layers, fire rating,
   finish level — to determine
   which real work activities are actually needed (framing? one layer or two? a finish
   coat?).
3. **Is this a real, standalone piece of installed work?** If the takeoff row plus
   drawing location plus spec requirement together describe one crew-doable task with a
   confirmed quantity, it becomes an Estimate Item (`origin=imported_qto` or `inferred`,
   `quantity_status=exact`/`derived`). If the location or requirement can't be confirmed,
   it does **not** get guessed into an Estimate Item — `propose_estimate_structure`'s own
   docstring says to "leave unsupported rows unmapped for review": an unresolved source
   row, never a fabricated one. A row left this way can be left implicitly unmapped, or
   explicitly declared via `EstimateDraftIn.unresolved_source_rows`
   (`EstimateSourceUnresolvedIn`, `app/core/estimates/types.py`) — a `takeoff_line_item_id`
   plus a required `reason` string. Either way the row surfaces as an `unassigned_source_row`
   verification issue (`issue_type="unassigned_source_row"`, see "User verification queue"),
   but an explicit `unresolved_source_rows` entry replaces that issue's generic fallback
   text ("This imported takeoff row is not assigned...") with the agent's own stated reason
   — the more useful signal for whoever triages that queue.

**A takeoff row's quantity does not have to go entirely to one Estimate Item.** The same
material can serve different work in different locations (500 SF of board: 300 SF to
Wall Type A, 150 to Wall Type B, 50 to Wall Type C) — `EstimateSourceMappingIn.quantity`
(`app/core/estimates/types.py`) lets a `row_type=material` mapping claim a portion of the
source row rather than always the full amount (omit it to consume the row's full
quantity, backward compatible). The quantities mapped to one source row across a whole
proposal can never sum to more than that row's total quantity — over-allocation rejects
the entire proposal (`app/core/estimates/service.py::_expand_source_row_mappings`). Only
`material` mappings carry this quantity semantic; `installed_work`/`allowance` mappings
still cannot repeat the same source row within one proposal, since there's no split
rationale for them. The raw `source_takeoff_line_item_ids` list field (as opposed to
`source_row_mappings`) remains a quantity-blind, unenforced loophole — multiple
items/components can still reference the same row through it with no accounting; that's
accepted, not fixed.

**`quantity_basis_type="qto"` means an exact, unchanged copy, not just "has some
relation to a takeoff row."** `estimates/service.py::_validate_direct_qto_quantity_copies`
re-fetches every `qto`-tagged item/component/project-cost's linked `TakeoffLineItem` and
rejects the whole proposal if the declared quantity or unit differs from that row's own
quantity/unit at all — an item that applies any conversion or factor on top of the QTO
number must be tagged `derived` (with the formula stated in its basis) instead, not `qto`.
`_validate_quantity_basis_types` also now requires a `qto`-tagged item/component to link
to **exactly one** source row (not merely "at least one"), and requires every
agent-authored **labor** component to carry a non-empty `basis` naming the operation and
quantity source, even when its quantity is `0`/`unknown` — labor can no longer go
undocumented the way it previously could.

Provenance is tracked two different ways, matching the two kinds of evidence above:
`EstimateSourceLink` ties an Item/Component back to the exact `takeoff_line_item_id` it
came from; drawing/spec evidence lives on `evidence: list[EvidenceInput]` (the same shape
`write_priced_line` uses), both on `EstimateCostComponent` (required whenever
`origin=inferred` — a validator on `EstimateComponentIn` rejects an inferred material
with no `basis`/`evidence`) and on `EstimateItem` itself. An agent-authored item satisfies
this either with a document-evidence entry, **or** with at least one QTO source link plus
a non-empty `rationale` — `_expand_source_row_mappings` rejects the whole proposal only
when an item has neither (a loosening from this rule's earlier all-items-need-evidence
form: a QTO-linked item with a stated rationale no longer strictly needs a drawing/spec
citation too). User-edited items are not evidence-checked, matching how inferred-material
evidence is already scoped. Either way the evidence's
`source_table` must be `document_nodes`, never a takeoff row id — a takeoff row is
provenance (`EstimateSourceLink`), not evidence for "where is this / what does it
require." A targeted page inspection is persisted as a non-verbatim `document_nodes`
observation and cites that stable node id.

Only after an Estimate Item and its components exist does pricing happen —
`write_estimate_component_price` fills in a component's rate. This is a second, parallel
pricing-write path alongside the pre-existing per-takeoff-line `write_priced_line` — both
independently promote into `historical_prices` today (see "Historical data source seam"
and "Open follow-ups"); that duplication is a known gap, not a designed-in feature.

The three-question derivation walk above is the reasoning freeform chat is expected to
follow when the model itself judges a message calls for it. The `categorize_estimate`
chat skill follows a considerably more elaborate, mandatory version of the same
reasoning — an explicit multi-system allocation sweep, a labor-coverage sweep, and a
bounded, agent-directed research tool rather than the three questions read literally; see
"Document research workflow" and "Estimate categorization skill" below for how that's
triggered and enforced.

## What this repo is

Backend only. A separate frontend repo owns the real UI — never scaffold product frontend
code here. This repo owns:

- The product's own REST API (FastAPI) that the frontend calls.
- The MCP server exposing tools/resources/prompts that an LLM agent uses to do the
  actual pricing work.
- All business logic and DB access.

## Architecture rules — do not deviate without asking

- **The agent never gets raw SQL or ORM access.** Every DB read/write the agent can do
  is a specific, named MCP tool with a typed schema and a tight, disambiguating
  docstring. This matters a lot: a previous version of this agent got confused about
  which tool to use, so tool descriptions must be precise and mutually exclusive —
  state what a tool is *not* for when there's a similarly-named neighbor.
- **Every priced line item gets a `source` field**: `historical_match | rfq_quote |
  agent_estimate | user_input`. This is an audit trail — don't skip it on any write
  path that prices a line item.
- **RFQs are async.** Creating one writes an editable `draft` row with selected line
  items, recipients, email copy, and vendor response links. Sending later moves the draft
  to `pending` and queues an email job, never inline in the request/response cycle. The
  FastAPI lifespan starts the background worker by default for local/single-process runs;
  deployments can disable that and run `app.workers.jobs` separately. A webhook/inbox
  handler later marks the row `fulfilled` with the quoted price. This is a real job/state
  table, not an afterthought — model retries and failure states explicitly.
- **The MCP server is mounted inside the same FastAPI app** (Streamable HTTP transport
  at `/mcp`), not run as a separate service, using the official Python MCP SDK's
  `FastMCP`. See `app/mcp/server.py` and the lifespan wiring in `app/main.py` — the
  FastMCP session manager must be started via the FastAPI app's lifespan or the mount
  will hang.
- **Business logic lives in `app/core/`.** Both the REST routes (`app/api/`) and the
  MCP tool handlers (`app/mcp/`) call into `app/core/` for pricing, matching, and RFQ
  orchestration. Never duplicate logic between the two callers — if a REST route and
  an MCP tool need the same behavior, factor it into `app/core/` and have both call it.
- **Every user-owned table's service function takes a required `user_id` and filters or
  checks it before any read/mutate.** This app is multi-tenant per-user; see
  "Authentication and multi-tenancy" below for the mechanism and the real cross-tenant
  leak this rule exists to prevent from recurring.

## Authentication and multi-tenancy

Real per-user multi-tenancy, added wholesale in a prior pass and hardened by
`3698854` ("Fix cross-tenant data leaks across projects, vendor contacts, memory, and
price book") on this branch. Flat per-user ownership — there is no company/org/tenant
entity above `User` today (`Assembly`/`AssemblyVersion` carry an unused nullable
`org_id`, see "Estimating domain model" above, but nothing else does).

- **JWT access + rotating refresh token.** `app/core/auth_jwt.py`:
  `create_access_token(user_id)` signs `{sub, iat, exp}` with `settings.
  jwt_secret_key`/`jwt_algorithm`; access tokens are stateless, short-lived
  (`jwt_access_token_ttl_seconds`), bearer-only (`fastapi.security.HTTPBearer`).
  `app/core/auth_refresh.py`: refresh tokens are opaque `secrets.token_urlsafe(32)`
  strings, stored only as sha256 hashes (`refresh_tokens.token_hash`), delivered via an
  httpOnly cookie scoped to `/api/auth`. Rotation is reuse-detection: every refresh
  token belongs to a `family_id` chaining back to one login, and presenting an
  already-rotated token revokes the *whole family* — standard refresh-token-theft
  defense, not just "rotate on use."
- **Password auth** (`app/core/auth_password.py`) uses Argon2
  (`argon2.PasswordHasher`, default params). `verify_password` fails closed on both
  `VerificationError` (wrong password) and `InvalidHash` (covers a seed migration's
  placeholder hash) — a malformed hash is never allowed to raise, only to fail like a
  wrong password. `app/api/auth.py` returns one identical generic 401 for "no such
  user" and "wrong password" to prevent email enumeration; lookup is
  case-insensitive via a functional unique index on `lower(email)`.
- **`require_project_ownership`** (`app/core/auth_deps.py`) is the FastAPI dependency
  guarding essentially every project-scoped router (`agent.py`, `artifacts.py`,
  `documents.py`, `document_annotations.py`, `estimates.py`, `side_chat.py`, `jobs.py`,
  `rfqs.py`, `projects.py`, `spreadsheets.py`, `verifications.py`, and more). It depends
  on `get_current_user` (bearer token → `User` row, sets the `current_user_id`
  ContextVar), then checks `Project.id == project_id AND Project.user_id == user.id`,
  404ing identically whether the project doesn't exist or belongs to someone else — no
  IDOR-revealing 403. Its own docstring frames it as a defense-in-depth convenience
  wrapper for once Postgres RLS is fully authoritative, not the only line of defense.
- **Postgres RLS is wired in but not yet the sole enforcement mechanism.** `app/db.py`'s
  SQLAlchemy `after_begin` listener runs `select set_config('app.user_id', :user_id,
  true)` per transaction, sourced from the `current_user_id` ContextVar
  (`app/core/telemetry_context.py`). Runtime app traffic is meant to connect via
  `settings.app_database_url` — a low-privileged, non-`BYPASSRLS` role — but falls back
  to `database_url` (the table-owning role Alembic also uses) until `APP_DATABASE_URL`
  is explicitly configured, so **enabling RLS enforcement for real is a config-only
  cutover step that has not necessarily happened yet** — check which URL is actually set
  before assuming the database itself is rejecting cross-tenant rows. Two narrow,
  named escape hatches from the ContextVar-based scoping exist and are load-bearing, not
  incidental: `is_job_coordinator` (the job worker's cross-tenant claim query — a queue
  claim must see every tenant's due jobs) and `is_magic_link_lookup` (`app/api/
  rfq_responses.py`'s vendor-facing token lookup, which runs before any `current_user_id`
  exists since a vendor never logs in). `auth_deps.py` also force-commits `get_current_
  user`'s own read-only lookup to start a fresh transaction, because the session's
  connection warm-up otherwise autobegins a transaction *before* `current_user_id` is
  set, which RLS would then never see.
- **The tenancy root is `Project.user_id`**, and per its own model comment every other
  project-scoped table's `user_id` is meant to be derived from that root, not set
  independently by application code. It — and most other tables' `user_id` — is still
  **nullable**, with an explicit comment that it becomes `NOT NULL` only after a
  tenancy-backfill migration that has not run yet.
- **The `3698854` leak, as the concrete standing example of the rule above**: four
  service layers queried or mutated rows with no `user_id` filter at all — `list_
  projects` (returned every user's projects), `app/core/vendors/` (vendor-contact
  list/get/update/delete had no ownership check), `app/core/memory/service.py` (agent
  memory list/patch/forget were unfiltered, `write_memory` never set `user_id` on
  create, and two prompt-injection paths mixed every tenant's saved facts into the
  system prompt), and `app/core/price_book/` (materials/labor search and
  identity-resolution-during-import could silently match or mutate another tenant's
  existing row). The fix pattern everywhere was the same: require `user_id`, filter list
  queries by it, and check `row.user_id == user_id` before any read/mutate — returning
  not-found, not 403, on mismatch (mirroring `require_project_ownership`'s own posture).
  MCP tools get `user_id` from `require_current_user_id()` off the same `current_user_id`
  ContextVar, since they have no FastAPI dependency injection to pull it from a header.

## Admin operator console (secret-path, password-gated)

`app/api/admin.py` + `app/core/admin/service.py` (added 2026-09-02): a JSON-only
operator surface, deliberately outside JWT user auth — no User row, no bearer token.
Two gates, both required: routes exist only under `/api/admin/{ADMIN_URL_SECRET}`,
and every route requires the `X-Admin-Password` header (`require_admin`,
constant-time compare over sha256 digests). Fails closed everywhere: with either
setting unset, `app/api/__init__.py` never includes the router at all and
`require_admin` 404s as a backstop; a wrong password is also a 404 (never 401) so a
probe can't confirm the path exists; `include_in_schema=False` keeps the prefix out
of `/openapi.json`. Endpoints: `POST /users` (create account, generated-or-chosen
password, 409 on case-insensitive duplicate), `GET /users` (list), `GET
/users/{id}/activity` (summary counts + recent runs/messages/jobs/projects).
The activity read is the RLS-sensitive part: it opens a **fresh** session inside
`telemetry_context.user_id_scope(target_user_id)` — an injected `get_session()`
session would silently return zero rows, since its transaction autobegins before the
route body runs and the `after_begin` RLS hook fires once per transaction. The whole
activity report (counts, cost sum, and all four recent-item feeds) is assembled
server-side in ONE json_build_object/json_agg SQL statement (`_ACTIVITY_QUERY`) —
the original one-query-per-piece version cost ~12 sequential Neon round trips and
measured 17s wall-clock vs ~1.2s now; round trips, not row volume, were the entire
cost, so don't split it back into ORM queries. (Side effect: activity-feed
timestamps serialize as `+00:00`, not `Z`.)
`scripts/create_user.py` now delegates to the same `create_user` service function.
Never expose any of this as MCP tools. Frontend contract:
`docs/admin-console-frontend-handoff.md`. Verified live against the shared DB
(auth-layer 404s, create/duplicate/login round-trip, RLS-scoped activity returning
real per-user counts); probe rows deleted after.

## Tech stack

- Python, managed with `uv` — use `uv add`/`uv remove` for deps, never pip/poetry/conda.
- FastAPI + Uvicorn.
- MCP Python SDK (`mcp` package, `FastMCP`).
- Postgres via SQLAlchemy async + `asyncpg`, migrations via Alembic. Connection string
  comes from the `DATABASE_URL` env var (see `app/config.py`).
- Pydantic / pydantic-settings for schemas and config.
- pytest for tests, ruff for lint/format.
- `aioboto3` for Cloudflare R2 file storage (R2's S3-compatible API) — see "File storage"
  below.
- Anthropic Python SDK (`anthropic`) for the product chat agent (see "Product chat
  agent" below) and, as a mode-gated fallback, for takeoff column mapping during
  ingestion (see "Takeoff column mapping" below). OpenAI Python SDK (`openai`) is also
  wired up as a fully equivalent alternative chat agent provider (`AGENT_LLM_PROVIDER=
  openai`, `app/core/agent/openai_provider.py`), and a third value `openai-codex`
  (`app/core/agent/codex_provider.py`) runs that same OpenAI loop over a ChatGPT
  subscription transport — takeoff column mapping itself stays Claude-only regardless
  of this setting.

## Repo structure

```
app/
  api/          FastAPI routers. Thin — parse request, call app/core/, return response.
    agent.py    GET/POST /api/projects/{id}/agent/messages — the product chat endpoint.
                POST accepts optional mentioned_document_ids (from the @-mention picker,
                see documents.py's mention-suggestions endpoint below); ids are validated
                against the project (400 on a foreign/unknown id, before any LLM call or
                DB write), stored as document_mention content blocks on the user
                AgentMessage (body stays exactly what the user typed), and injected into
                the LLM conversation as a filename + project_document_id context suffix —
                on the current turn and on history replay alike (orchestrator.py's
                _with_mention_context). AgentMessageOut carries tool_calls/queued_jobs;
                GET .../agent/runs/current is the live-progress poll/discovery endpoint,
                GET .../agent/runs/{run_id}/events/stream is the SSE live-progress
                stream, and POST .../agent/messages/start is the start/complete-split
                entrypoint that returns a run_id before the turn finishes — see "Live
                tool-call visibility and queued-job tracking" and "Agent run streaming
                and the three chat surfaces" below.
    jobs.py     Two read-only status routers, because a job's owner is not always a
                project. GET /api/projects/{id}/jobs/{job_id} is the project-scoped one
                (pending/running/succeeded/failed, result, error), scoped via
                job.payload["project_id"] since it predates Job.project_id existing.
                GET /api/jobs/{job_id} is the user-scoped one, for work on user-owned
                data no project contains (the price book) — it checks job.user_id ==
                user.id explicitly rather than trusting RLS, 404s (never 403s) on
                anything unreachable, restricts itself to USER_POLLABLE_JOB_TYPES so it
                can't hand out other features' result blobs, and reports
                progress_completed/progress_total only while a job is actually running
                (progress is written outside the job's transaction, so a failed attempt
                leaves a stale count for rows that were rolled back). Neither router
                queues anything; every job is created by the core service owning its
                workflow.
    projects.py GET /api/projects lists; GET /api/projects/summaries (added `8716aea`)
                computes every home-page card's fields (readiness, takeoff/doc/
                verification counts, priced/unpriced, estimate-generation and
                quote-import states) for ALL of a user's projects in a fixed handful of
                set-based queries via app/core/project/summaries.py — built because one
                GET .../overview per card starved the connection pool (each request held
                one pooled connection while waiting to check out a second; the overview
                now runs sequentially on the caller's one session). Field paths mirror
                the ProjectOverview shape so card logic works on either payload; the
                per-project overview remains the detail read.
                GET .../overview reads (includes readiness, see
                "Mandatory project files and readiness" below); DELETE /api/projects/{id}
                permanently removes a project: every row it owns (takeoff lines,
                priced_line_items, RFQs, quote_submissions/vendor_quote_line_items,
                quote_material_groups, chat/audit history, project_spreadsheets) plus its
                uploaded files in Blob storage. Vendor contacts and historical_prices are
                company-wide, not project-owned, so they're kept — a deleted priced line
                just clears historical_prices' optional back-link to it. See
                app/core/project/service.py::delete_project. POST /api/projects is
                multipart, not JSON: name plus a required takeoff_file/spec_file/
                drawing_file, created atomically in one transaction (see "Mandatory
                project files and readiness" below) — there is no name-only creation path.
    documents.py GET project document endpoints (list/search/outline/section/chunk) —
                thin wrappers around app/core/project/document_catalog.py, the same
                functions the MCP document tools call (see MCP tool catalog) — plus
                POST for document upload, which calls
                app/core/ingestion/ingest_project_document (deleted — no function of
                that name exists today) instead.
                Also GET .../documents/mention-suggestions?q= — the chat @-mention
                typeahead (filename substring match, newest first). Deliberately separate
                from the plain list endpoint: it skips presigned blob URLs and chunk
                counts because it's hit on every keystroke of a mention query.
    spreadsheets.py PATCH/POST endpoints under /api/projects/{id}/spreadsheets/quote-comparison
                for the estimator quote-comparison grid — see "Quote comparison spreadsheet" below.
                Its grouping-review-suggestion endpoints are now compatibility wrappers over
                the generic verification queue (see "User verification queue" below).
    verifications.py GET /api/projects/{id}/verifications and POST .../{id}/resolve — the one
                generic review surface for every verification kind. Thin over
                app/core/verification/service.py.
    auth.py     POST .../login, .../refresh, .../logout — issues the JWT access token +
                httpOnly refresh cookie described in "Authentication and multi-tenancy"
                above. Not project-scoped. There is deliberately NO register endpoint —
                accounts are created only via scripts/create_user.py or the admin
                console (both through app/core/admin/service.py::create_user).
    admin.py    The secret-path, password-gated operator console — see "Admin operator
                console" above. Mounted conditionally in app/api/__init__.py only when
                ADMIN_URL_SECRET and ADMIN_PASSWORD are both set.
    side_chat.py POST .../side-chat/messages/start + GET .../runs/{id}/events[/stream] —
                a project-scoped, read-only chat surface distinct from the main agent;
                see "Agent run streaming and the three chat surfaces" below. Its
                start/stream/complete SSE implementation is committed and live.
    user_wide_agent.py Cross-project, user-scoped chat surface (not under /api/projects/
                {id}) — see "Agent run streaming and the three chat surfaces" below.
    assemblies.py Full CRUD/publish/retire/search REST API for the Assembly recipe
                library — see "Estimating domain model"'s correction above and
                docs/assemblies.md. Not yet MCP-agent-facing.
    document_annotations.py REST backing for the highlight_document_region/
                list_document_annotations MCP tools (§ MCP tool catalog) — delegates to
                app/core/project/document_annotations.py, not app/core/drawings/ (which
                is an empty leftover directory from the deleted drawing-indexing
                pipeline, not a live module — do not add code to it without checking
                git history first).
    gmail.py    GET .../connection, POST .../oauth/start, GET .../oauth/callback,
                DELETE .../connection — per-user Gmail OAuth (gmail.send scope) so RFQ
                emails send from the sub's own Gmail account. See app/core/gmail/.
    units.py    GET/POST /api/units — user-facing custom-unit *creation* (name + aliases,
                auto-generated collision-free code), distinct from
                app/core/price_book/units.py's resolve_unit, which is unit *resolution*
                against the existing catalog, not creation.
    rfq_responses.py GET/POST /api/rfq-responses/{token} — the vendor-facing magic-link
                surface (no auth; vendors never log in), backed by the
                is_magic_link_lookup RLS escape hatch (see "Authentication and
                multi-tenancy" above).
    artifacts.py, estimates.py, price_book.py, vendor_contacts.py, health.py — REST
                surfaces over their same-named app/core/ packages below; vendor_contacts.py
                is the REST CRUD for app/core/vendors/, not a separate vendor system.
  mcp/          MCP server setup (FastMCP instance), mounted at /mcp. `ping` lives in
                server.py itself, outside tools/, and is excluded from what the chat
                agent sees (see "Live MCP tool registry" above).
    tools/      The @mcp.tool()-decorated functions, one file per MCP tool catalog
                section: artifacts.py, document_annotations.py, estimates.py,
                evidence.py, memory.py, pricing.py, project_context.py, project_setup.py,
                rfq.py, user_interaction.py, user_wide.py, vendor_quote.py,
                verification.py — see "Live MCP tool registry" above for the exact
                tool-name mapping; this list is the current, correct one (the older
                "project.py, pricing.py, rfq.py" grouping this line used to name no
                longer matches the real files). Import app.mcp.tools from server.py
                registers them; keep that import.
  core/         Shared business logic. Only layer that REST routes and MCP tools both call into.
    admin/      Operator console service (create/list users, one-query per-user
                activity report) — see "Admin operator console" above. Never MCP-facing.
    agent/      The manual bridging loop between an LLM API and the MCP tools for the
                main project chat surface — see "Product chat agent" and "Agent run
                streaming and the three chat surfaces" below. orchestrator.py is the
                entrypoint (now split start_message_run/complete_message_run, see the
                streaming section), provider.py is the LLM seam (run_agentic_turn takes
                excluded_tool_names/allowed_tool_names/include_server_tools and an
                optional max_tool_turns override; run_structured_turn is the separate
                tool-free structured-output call shape), claude_provider.py/
                openai_provider.py/codex_provider.py the three implementations
                (factory.py resolves AGENT_LLM_PROVIDER to one of them), tools.py
                bridges to the
                FastMCP registry in-process (execute_tool also marks/clears each run's
                live current_tool_name — see "Live tool-call visibility" below — and
                emits the SSE events described in "Agent run streaming" below;
                display_name_for_tool cleans a raw tool name for the frontend). skills.py
                holds fixed, mandatory directive text + per-skill tool allow/exclude
                lists for structured skill invocations (categorize_estimate,
                price_estimate, project_setup_orient, project_setup_resolve — see
                "Estimate categorization skill" and "Thin mandatory project setup"
                below). skill_router.py detects when a freeform chat message means a
                skill even though the frontend's "/" picker wasn't used — see "Skill
                auto-detection" below. events.py/streaming.py/run_steps.py/
                cancellation.py back the SSE run-event architecture (AgentRunEvent,
                TERMINAL_EVENT_TYPES, the start/complete split) — see "Agent run
                streaming" below. images.py handles inline image attachments in a chat
                turn. side_chat.py is its own surface, not part of the main
                orchestrator loop — see the streaming section.
                **Deleted, do not reintroduce as a dependency**: pricing_batch.py (the
                v1 per-Estimate-Item pricing subagent runner) and document_research/
                (the bounded research_project_documents pipeline) no longer exist as
                files — the MCP tools they backed (price_estimate_items,
                research_project_documents) are gone from the registry too (see "Live
                MCP tool registry" above); Stage 5's interactive set_component_price
                flow and evidence.py's retrieve_project_evidence replaced them
                respectively.
    project_setup/ Backs the identify_scope skill's project_setup.orient/.resolve turns
                — see "Thin mandatory project setup" below for the current tool set and
                lifecycle.
    user_wide_agent/ The cross-project, user-scoped chat surface's own orchestrator
                (start_message_run/complete_message_run, mirroring app/core/agent/'s
                split) — see "Agent run streaming and the three chat surfaces" below.
    document_pipeline/ document.prepare / document.enrich.text / document.enrich.visual
                / document.enrich.finalize — the versioned extraction/structure/
                embedding/publication pipeline described at the top of this file under
                "Current document ingestion and retrieval foundation."
    retrieval/  Backs retrieve_project_evidence/get_document_context/
                inspect_document_page: fusion.py implements weighted reciprocal-rank
                fusion across entity/BM25/dense/visual lanes plus sibling-diversity
                selection; providers.py wraps the Voyage embed/rerank calls;
                repository.py is the Postgres-FTS-baseline / Lakebase-BM25-accelerator
                split described at the top of this file.
    assemblies/ Assembly recipe library service (create/publish/retire/search a
                versioned Assembly → AssemblyVersion → AssemblyVersionComponent graph)
                — see "Estimating domain model"'s correction above and
                docs/assemblies.md. REST-only; not yet MCP-agent-facing, and does not
                yet expand a published assembly into actual Estimate content.
    gmail/      Per-user Gmail OAuth (authorization-URL construction, HMAC-signed TTL'd
                CSRF state param, token exchange/storage) used to send RFQ emails from
                the sub's own Gmail account. See app/api/gmail.py above.
    vendors/    Vendor contact CRUD, shared by app/api/vendor_contacts.py (REST) and
                app/core/rfq/service.py's list_vendor_contacts (RFQ/MCP path) — the one
                implementation both callers delegate into, not a competing module.
                Unrelated to vendor_bids/ (quote-document extraction/grouping) beyond
                the shared "vendor" naming.
    source_identity.py A tiny module capturing SOURCE_IDENTITY (git branch + commit,
                from RIALTO_GIT_BRANCH/RIALTO_GIT_COMMIT env vars or a git subprocess
                fallback) at import time, for stamping which build is actually running
                into logs/health output.
    db_telemetry.py, llm_telemetry.py, telemetry_context.py, telemetry_report.py
                Two independent, previously-undocumented telemetry systems.
                db_telemetry.py is per-HTTP-request Postgres query/pool-checkout timing,
                wired as FastAPI middleware, logged only (no table, no endpoint).
                llm_telemetry.py wraps every LLM API call and persists a normalized
                LLMCall row per call (provider/model/tokens/estimated cost/a fixed
                Operation enum), keyed to telemetry_context.py's current_run_id/
                current_span_id/current_user_id ContextVars (the last of which app/db.py
                also reads for RLS — see "Authentication and multi-tenancy" above).
                telemetry_report.py builds read-only reports over persisted
                LLMCall/ToolCall/AgentRun rows, exposed via GET /api/agent/runs/{id}/
                telemetry and GET /api/projects/{id}/telemetry (the latter explicitly
                commented dev/internal-only).
    project/    Read takeoff line items and project overview/readiness aggregation (see
                below) — document retrieval itself now lives in retrieval/ and
                document_pipeline/ above, not here; this package predates that split and
                its own docstrings may still describe the older, now-retired
                get_document_outline/search_project_documents-era tools (see "Live MCP
                tool registry" above).
                summaries.py is the batched all-projects home-page read behind
                GET /api/projects/summaries (see projects.py above) — a fixed handful
                of set-based queries, deliberately serving only the fields the cards
                render; the per-project overview stays the detail read.
                overview.py aggregates scope/readiness/pricing/RFQ/quote/document counts
                into one GET /api/projects/{id}/overview payload for a project dashboard —
                read-only, composes the other app/core/* services rather than querying
                tables directly. readiness.py derives (never stores) whether active
                document uploads are still processing and whether searchable evidence
                exists — see "Project ingestion readiness" below.
    ingestion/  Direct browser-to-R2 multipart upload orchestration plus deterministic
                takeoff parsing helpers. Upload initiation records an immutable document
                and session; completion verifies object size and queues document.prepare.
                The versioned document pipeline owns extraction, structure, embeddings,
                and atomic publication. column_mapping.py holds the LLM-assisted fallback
                used when deterministic takeoff-header matching fails -- not agent-facing,
                purely an ingestion-time concern.
    pricing/    Historical-price scoring/matching, the HistoricalDataProvider seam
                (provider.py -- EstimateHistoricalProvider/RialtoHistoricalProvider/
                SyntheticHistoricalProvider, see "Historical data source seam" below),
                write_priced_line (the one function that persists a price to the
                legacy priced_line_items table + its evidence -- no longer an agent
                tool, but still called directly by the spreadsheet Apply-Pricing
                legacy branch and the priced_line_proposal verification resolver).
    estimates/  CSI-organized Estimate structure: EstimateItem (installed-work, CSI-coded)
                nesting EstimateCostComponent (material/labor/equipment/subcontract/other)
                plus project-wide costs, append-only EstimateComponentPrice history, and
                EstimateSourceLink/EstimateSourceExclusion takeoff-row provenance -- see
                "Estimating domain model" above. Also owns get_estimate_items (scoped
                multi-item read), update_estimate_item (targeted correction, the one
                write allowed on an approved Estimate), search_historical_estimate_items
                (cross-project item lookup by CSI code), and get_estimate_pricing_status
                (component pricing coverage). Independently promotes into
                historical_prices in parallel with pricing/'s write_priced_line, not yet
                reconciled -- see Open follow-ups.
    rfq/        RFQ draft creation/editing, vendor response links, send-job enqueueing, vendor lookup.
    vendor_bids/ Quote-document extraction, LLM-only quote-material grouping, persisted
                material correction/review, unit normalization, and deterministic
                takeoff-line matching. The package name remains for compatibility; its
                internal API uses quote-native names. See "Vendor quote ingestion and
                grouping" below.
    spreadsheets/ Builds the quote-comparison spreadsheet workbook (project/vendor-quote
                columns + estimator-editable overlay state), vendor consolidation, and
                Apply Pricing. Material-group mutations live in vendor_bids/, not here.
    verification/ Generic user-verification queue for low-confidence pipeline/agent output —
                see "User verification queue" below. registry.py + kinds.py map each kind to
                a payload schema, decisions, and a resolver that dispatches into the owning
                domain package; service.py owns queue semantics (raise/list/resolve/supersede)
                and never contains business logic.
    jobs/       service.py's JOB_HANDLERS dict is the one place a job_type string maps to
                its handler function. Current document work is document.prepare, the
                document.enrich.text/visual/finalize fan-out, and vendor_quote.extract;
                setup, RFQ, quote regroup, and price_book.unit_gaps.resolve
                jobs share the same worker contract. claim_next_job/
                perform_job/record_success/record_failure/record_deferred are the worker's
                only contract with a Job row; handlers never commit (app/workers/jobs.py
                does, per job, after the handler returns). JobDeferred is a typed escape
                hatch for "reschedule me without penalizing retry count, another job needs
                to finish first" (quote_material.regroup's extraction-pending wait is the
                precedent).
                Every handler is a thin adapter here that delegates into its owning
                domain package; adding a JOB_HANDLERS key is the only wiring a new
                non-document job type needs, since DEFAULT_JOB_TYPES is derived by
                subtracting the document queues. Note what that implies operationally:
                the default queue runs only in the web process at concurrency 1, so a
                long-running job head-of-line-blocks every other default-queue job
                (rfq.send included) and is hard-cancelled at jobs_default_timeout_seconds
                (1200s), which rolls the whole attempt back. A handler must also never
                raise on user input -- record_failure discards every write, keeps only a
                bare error string, and retries input that will fail identically; put
                user-input failures in the returned result dict and let the job succeed.
                progress.py's set_job_progress is the one live-progress marker (its own
                session, its own commit, lease-fenced); a RUNNING job that doesn't
                checkpoint within queue_health_stale_running_seconds (300s) trips a
                stale_running incident.
    storage/    Cloudflare R2 client (aioboto3 against R2's S3-compatible API).
    audit.py    run_logged_tool — every MCP tool call goes through this. Owns the session,
                the commit/rollback, and the tool_calls log row. Business logic in the
                app/core/* service modules never commits or logs itself. Also holds
                current_run_id, the ContextVar the agent orchestrator uses to thread an
                AgentRun id into tool_calls/evidence_refs — see "Product chat agent" below.
                set_current_tool is the one other thing that writes here (agent_runs'
                live-progress columns only, never tool_calls) — see "Live tool-call
                visibility and queued-job tracking" below.
  models/       SQLAlchemy models, one file per domain area (project, pricing, rfq, quote,
                job, audit, chat, spreadsheet, verification, estimate, assembly, memory,
                side_chat, gmail, units, user, project_setup). app/models/base.py holds
                Base plus shared mixins. audit.py now also holds AgentRunEvent (the SSE
                event log — see "Agent run streaming" below) and LLMCall (see
                db_telemetry.py/llm_telemetry.py above). user.py backs "Authentication
                and multi-tenancy" above.
  config.py     Settings via pydantic-settings, reads from .env.
  db.py         Async engine/session + to_asyncpg_url (Neon URL normalization, see below).
  main.py       FastAPI app instance; mounts API routers and the MCP app; owns the lifespan
                that starts/stops the MCP session manager and background job worker.
tests/          pytest tests. Only pure-logic units (scoring, URL normalization) run here --
                DB-backed flows were verified manually against a throwaway local Postgres
                and the real DB, see "How this was verified" below.
alembic/        Migrations. env.py pulls DATABASE_URL from app.config.settings, normalizes it
                through app.db.to_asyncpg_url, and targets all models under app.models.
```

## MCP tool catalog

**Read "Live MCP tool registry (authoritative)" near the top of this file first.** Most
of the tool names below (`propose_estimate_structure`, `get_estimate_structure`,
`get_estimate_items`, `update_estimate_item`, `start_over_estimate`,
`write_estimate_component_price`, `write_project_cost`,
`search_historical_material_price`, `search_historical_labor_rate`,
`search_historical_estimate_items`, `get_estimate_pricing_status`,
`price_estimate_items`, `get_project_document_summaries`,
`list_document_understanding_cards`, `search_document_understanding`,
`research_project_documents`, `inspect_drawing_sheet`, `search_project_documents`,
`read_project_document_section`, `read_project_document_chunk`, `get_document_outline`)
no longer exist in the live registry — this section was written for a v1 tool surface
that has since been deleted, not merely superseded. It's left in place because the
design reasoning behind each retired tool's *behavior* (what counts as evidence, why
material/labor search are separate, why historical tiers are additive, etc.) mostly
still applies to whatever replaced it, but do not attempt to call any tool named below
without first checking it against the real registry list above.

The tools are organized around the four pricing `source` values, the CSI Estimate
workflow, and read-only artifact discovery/display tools, plus
read-only artifact discovery/display tools for chat artifacts and the two
user-verification-queue tools (see "User verification queue" below):

- `list_project_artifacts(project_id)` — read-only artifact discovery for the product
  chat response. It returns the currently available DB-backed Material Takeoff, Estimate,
  RFQ, Quote Comparison, and Document `AgentArtifactRef`s for a project, so the agent can
  find an existing artifact id before calling `display_artifact`. It does not read full
  artifact rows for reasoning and does not create or mutate pricing/RFQ state. A document
  only appears here once it has at least one non-superseded annotation — an uploaded file
  with nothing highlighted on it yet doesn't flood this list (see `display_artifact`'s
  `kind="document"` note below for the unrestricted, explicit-id path).
- `display_artifact(project_id, kind, rfq_id, document_id)` — read-only presentation intent
  for the product chat response. It validates that a DB-backed Material Takeoff, Estimate,
  RFQ, Quote Comparison, or Document artifact exists and returns its `AgentArtifactRef`; it
  does not read full artifact rows for reasoning and does not create or mutate pricing/RFQ
  state. **There is no separate "pricing" artifact kind** — retired along with the legacy
  per-takeoff-line pricing surface (see "Estimate artifact absorbs the legacy pricing
  artifact" below); `kind="estimate"` covers a project both before anything is priced
  (shows the unpriced CSI structure as soon as it's proposed) and after (reflects prices
  as `write_estimate_component_price`/`update_estimate_item`/the pricing batch write
  them), so there was never a state only the old pricing artifact could represent.
  `kind="document"` requires `document_id` and opens that document's review panel; unlike
  the list path above, an explicit call works even before any annotation exists.
- `highlight_document_region(project_id, document_id, node_id, page_number, quad, note)` —
  creates a highlight the user sees in the document review panel and the agent can link
  back to in chat prose (see "Identifiers in chat prose, and clickable Estimate row
  links" below for the same `workbook_ref`-style mechanism applied to documents). Prefer
  `node_id` (resolved through the same evidence path as citations, exact precision) over
  a raw `quad`; omitting both and supplying only `note` derives an approximate region via
  a fresh vision read of that page. Not for citing document text in prose — that's
  `retrieve_project_evidence`/`get_document_context`, which this tool never touches.
- `list_document_annotations(project_id, document_id)` — read-only: every highlight on
  one document, the user's and the agent's own prior ones, so the agent knows what's
  already flagged before adding another.
- `get_takeoff_line_items(project_id)` — read-only normalized takeoff scope.
- `get_estimate_structure(project_id)` — reads the active CSI-organized Estimate,
  including installed-work items, direct components, project costs, latest append-only
  prices, derived totals, and source-row coverage split into `unassigned_source_rows`
  (zero material quantity placed anywhere) and `partially_allocated_source_rows`
  (some but not all of a row's quantity placed — `remaining_quantity` says how much is
  still unaccounted for). See "Estimating domain model" above for why coverage isn't
  binary now that a material row can be split across items. If you already know which
  item ids you need, use `get_estimate_items` instead — lighter, no coverage/totals
  overhead.
- `get_estimate_items(project_id, estimate_item_ids)` — read-only, scoped to specific
  items: their info, components (materials/labor/etc. with latest prices if priced),
  and evidence, in the order requested. Raises by name on any requested id that doesn't
  belong to the project's active Estimate rather than silently dropping it. This is
  what a `price_estimate_items` subagent (below) calls to read only its assigned item.
- `begin_estimate_proposal(project_id, mode)` / `get_estimate_proposal(...)` /
  `patch_estimate_proposal(...)` / `finalize_estimate_proposal(...)` — the managed
  product-chat categorization path. It opens or resumes an internal, versioned proposal
  workspace, saves complete item replacements under stable proposal-local keys, returns
  structured validation after every patch, and exposes an Estimate artifact only after
  finalization. `mode=replace` snapshots the active Estimate without changing it;
  finalization archives the old revision and creates the new one in the same transaction.
  Managed schemas use run-local QTO/fact aliases while persistence receives authoritative
  UUID/evidence values. Drafts are internal and resumable, not user-visible artifacts.
- `propose_estimate_structure(project_id, proposal, research_dispositions)` — compatibility
  single-shot tool for external MCP clients; atomically
  writes one complete proposed Estimate revision. Direct costs must be nested under
  installed-work items; project-wide costs remain separate. Unknown source rows,
  malformed CSI values, orphan components, invalid labor inputs, a material split summing
  to more than its source row's quantity, a `qto`-tagged quantity that isn't an exact
  copy of its one linked source row, an agent-authored labor component with no `basis`,
  and an Estimate Item with neither evidence nor a QTO link plus rationale all reject the
  complete proposal — see "Estimating domain model" above for the split-quantity,
  quantity-basis, and evidence rules. `research_dispositions` is a required (possibly
  empty) list of `EstimateResearchDispositionIn` — one entry per
  `research_project_documents` question this proposal's reasoning drew on, logged as a
  tool-call audit trail only, never stored as Estimate content (see "Estimate
  categorization skill" below). Agent overwrite is blocked after estimator edits or
  approval, unless the user has just explicitly asked for a full restart — see
  `start_over_estimate` below. Not a piecemeal correction tool — use `update_estimate_item`
  to edit one existing item/component in place.
- `start_over_estimate(project_id)` — compatibility tool for external MCP clients and the
  existing REST start-over behavior. It archives the active Estimate (`status="archived"`,
  excluded from `get_active_estimate`) so the *next* `propose_estimate_structure` call
  creates a fresh `ProjectEstimate` row with `user_modified_at=None`, rather than
  hitting the same-row conflict check. This is the only way the agent can lift the
  "approved Estimates are locked" restriction below — the docstring requires the tool
  only be called when the user explicitly asks, in that exact message, to
  restart/redo/scrap the whole thing; an ambiguous request or a single wrong item
  should go through `update_estimate_item` instead, which needs no restart at all.
  Nothing is deleted (the archived draft and any prices already written under it stay
  in the database), but between this call and the next successful
  `propose_estimate_structure`, the project briefly has no active Estimate — the same
  transient state the pre-existing REST `POST .../estimate/start-over` endpoint already
  produces. Product chat never receives this tool; full rebuilds use the safe staged
  replacement workflow above. **Not yet verified against a live Postgres** — the
  create-vs-reuse logic in `save_estimate_draft` (`app/core/estimates/service.py`) was
  confirmed by reading the code (`get_active_estimate` filters out `archived` rows, so
  a fresh row gets created on the next proposal), not by running the flow against a
  real database; follow the "How this was verified" convention before trusting it in
  production.
- `update_estimate_item(project_id, estimate_item_id, updates)` — the one exception to
  "approved Estimates are locked" that needs no restart at all: corrects fields (name,
  CSI code, a component's name/quantity/basis/evidence, etc.) and/or appends a
  component price on one *existing* item in place — never creates or deletes an item,
  component, price, or source link. Every field but a component's `id` is optional and
  means "leave unchanged" when omitted; pass `""` to explicitly clear a nullable text
  field. Every call permanently marks the estimate user-modified, which then blocks
  `propose_estimate_structure`/`write_project_cost` on it until `start_over_estimate`
  runs. Not `propose_estimate_structure` (that replaces the complete structure) and not
  `write_estimate_component_price` (that only appends a price, nothing else) — use this
  specifically for "the user pointed out something wrong, fix it," not for the initial
  proposal and not for a full restart (`start_over_estimate` is for that).
- `write_estimate_component_price(project_id, component_id, price)` — appends the selected
  component price. Labor supports productivity-based pricing; all methods preserve basis,
  conditions, confidence, and evidence. Approved prices can feed historical candidates.
- `write_project_cost(project_id, project_cost)` — adds a supported project-wide cost to
  an untouched draft. It is not a back door for direct installation scope.
- `list_project_documents(project_id)`, `get_document_outline(project_id,
  project_document_id)`, `search_project_documents(project_id, query, limit)`,
  `read_project_document_section(project_id, section_pointer)`, `read_project_document_chunk(
  project_id, chunk_id)` — read-only project document context. Uploaded specs/addenda/drawings
  keep their source row in `project_documents`; extracted text is split into page/section-aware
  `project_document_chunks`, each classified with a `heading_kind` (`csi_section` |
  `csi_division` | `outline` | `None`) and, when CSI-coded, a `csi_division` — see
  `app/core/ingestion/chunking.py`. `get_document_outline` returns a document's sections as a
  navigable table of contents; each entry is a contiguous run of chunks sharing one section
  identity (a section number recurring non-contiguously — e.g. a table-of-contents mention vs.
  the real body section — is two separate entries, not merged) and carries a `section_pointer`
  (the id of that run's first chunk). `search_project_documents` is a hybrid lexical search
  (full-text + `ILIKE` + `pg_trgm` fuzzy matching for near-miss spellings, plus LLM query
  expansion grounded in the project's own section titles and takeoff label/assembly/location
  vocabulary — see `app/core/project/query_expansion.py`) rolled up to one ranked result per
  matching *section*, not per chunk; each result also carries a `section_pointer`.
  Drawing results may include separately citeable `cached_observations`, searched from
  `drawing_sheet_observations` alongside the immutable ingestion summary. Observations
  are scoped to the exact uploaded `project_document_id` and page, remain additive, and
  are omitted from search after `invalidated_at` is set.
  `read_project_document_section` reads a section's full text from that pointer, for
  understanding a requirement in context rather than as an isolated excerpt.
  `read_project_document_chunk` is the narrower fallback for a match with no `section_pointer`
  (content outside any detected section). Deliberately lexical + fuzzy, not semantic/embedding
  search — specs lean on exact codes/standards where embeddings often do worse than lexical
  matching; revisit only if a concrete retrieval failure is found, not speculatively.
- `inspect_drawing_sheet(project_id, section_pointer, question)` — performs a fresh vision
  read of one actual drawing page. The standalone tool remains read-only. The reusable
  `research_project_documents` workflow can synthesize and append a durable observation
  afterward, but only when one final atomic fact is supported by exactly one fresh drawing
  inspection; mixed spec/drawing facts, multi-sheet facts, and reused cached facts are not
  written. Cached observations can satisfy a future question without vision; if they are
  insufficient, the researcher expands and reinspects the sheet normally.
- `research_project_documents(project_id, questions)` — bounded, reusable multi-question
  document research; see "Document research workflow" above for the full plan → retrieve
  → synthesize → follow-up pipeline behind it. Takes 1-12 `DocumentResearchQuestion`
  objects in one call (each one atomic factual gap, never a combined decision) and
  returns one `resolved`/`partial`/`unresolved` finding per question with cited evidence,
  cited `conflicts`, a `stop_reason` when research stopped short of resolving the
  question (the discriminator between "the documents don't say" and "retrieval didn't
  find it" — see "Document research pipeline hardening" below), and a source inventory.
  It never makes the caller's Estimate grouping, CSI, allocation, quantity, or approval
  decision — only establishes facts for the caller to reason from.
  Internally restricted to exactly four read-only retrieval tools
  (`search_project_documents`/`read_project_document_chunk`/`read_project_document_section`/
  `inspect_drawing_sheet`); not a substitute for calling those directly outside a
  bounded research workflow, and not the same as `inspect_drawing_sheet` (which performs
  one fresh page read, no planning/synthesis, no batching).
- `get_project_document_summaries(project_id, project_document_id)`,
  `list_document_understanding_cards(project_id, project_document_id, card_kind, limit)`,
  `search_document_understanding(project_id, query, limit)` — read-only access to the
  `document.understand` job's output (per-chunk orientation cards plus a per-document
  summary, `app/core/project/understanding.py`): the cheapest first read of an ingested
  document, cheaper than paging through raw chunks via `search_project_documents`. A
  document with no summary yet (job still pending, or the role isn't in
  `_UNDERSTANDING_ROLES`) returns an empty result, not an error.
- `get_project_setup(project_id)` — reads the current versioned setup scope ledger:
  setup status/snapshot, building-system dispositions and evidence, resolved/pending
  setup questions, and explicitly defaulted assumptions. It never returns a project-fact
  or specified-material cache; retrieve those facts from source documents.
- `get_project_scope_brief(project_id)` (`app/mcp/tools/project_context.py`) — read-only
  access to the optional `ProjectScopeBrief` produced by Identify Scope. It contains the
  CSI divisions and narrative trade scope for orientation, never creates Estimate
  content, and is not itself citable Estimate evidence. Categorization proceeds directly
  from project evidence and the QTO when no current brief exists.
- `search_historical_material_price(project_id, label, unit)`,
  `search_historical_labor_rate(project_id, label, unit)`
  — historical lookups, kept as **separate tools** (not one combined tool) because material
  and labor follow genuinely different fallback paths per the product flow. Each merges
  candidates from up to three tiers (see "Historical data source seam" below) and returns
  a `HistoricalPriceSearchResult`: `candidates` (top-10 ranked, with a compatibility score
  and rejection reasons — `unit_mismatch`, `weak_label_match`, `missing_price`) plus
  `stats` (`count`/`mean`/`median`/`mode`/`min`/`max`/`quartiles`) computed over the
  **full accepted pool**, which may be larger than the 10 shown — use `stats` to judge
  the typical price range even without inspecting every candidate. An empty/all-rejected
  result means "fall back to RFQ," not "price is zero." Every candidate carries a
  `source_table` field saying which tier it came from (`estimate_cost_components` |
  `historical_prices` | `synthetic_estimate_lines`) — cite that alongside the candidate's
  `id` as evidence when calling `write_estimate_component_price`. Scoring lives in
  `app/core/pricing/scoring.py` (token overlap + unit-alias compatibility), written
  fresh — see "Reference repo" below for why; scoring itself doesn't weight by tier,
  that's left to the agent's judgment.
- `search_historical_estimate_items(csi_code, label)` — company-wide (no `project_id`,
  same posture as `historical_prices`), finds previously-priced Estimate Items from
  **other** projects under the same CSI code, optionally narrowed by wording overlap.
  Returns full item context (evidence, components with latest prices), not a flat price
  number, so the agent can judge applicability the way it judges `historical_match` vs
  `agent_estimate` elsewhere. Deliberately a **separate, heavier, on-demand** tool
  rather than a fourth tier merged into the two search tools above — same precedent as
  `inspect_drawing_sheet` being separate from the cheap always-on
  `search_project_documents`.
- `get_estimate_pricing_status(project_id)` — read-only: which of the active Estimate's
  components already have a price and which don't. A component absent from the result
  hasn't been priced yet — same "absence is the signal, no stored flag" philosophy the
  now-retired `get_pricing_status` used for takeoff lines. Cross-reference against
  `get_estimate_structure`/`get_estimate_items` for full scope vs. what's missing.

**Retired as agent tools**: `write_priced_line` and `get_pricing_status` (the
per-takeoff-line, pre-Estimate `priced_line_items` tools) are no longer registered —
every project now builds an Estimate, and pricing happens through
`write_estimate_component_price`/`get_estimate_pricing_status` instead. This is an MCP
boundary change only: the underlying `app.core.pricing.service.write_priced_line`
function and the `priced_line_items` table are untouched, since
`app/core/spreadsheets/service.py`'s legacy Apply-Pricing branch and the
`priced_line_proposal` verification resolver both still call it directly (never through
MCP). A pre-Estimate project's chat pricing is now **queue-only**: the system prompt's
fallback flow always routes through `request_user_verification(kind=
priced_line_proposal)` rather than writing directly — see "Product chat agent" below.
- `list_vendor_contacts(trade, material)`, `create_rfq(project_id, line_item_ids,
  vendor_ids)`, `send_rfq(rfq_id)`, `get_rfq_status(rfq_id)` — RFQ tools. `create_rfq`
  writes an editable `draft` RFQ and selected line/vendor rows; `send_rfq` transitions that
  draft to `pending` and writes the `rfq.send` `Job` row. Neither sends email inline. The
  **automatic** inbound quote handler — the one that would mark an RFQ `fulfilled` and
  write a `source=rfq_quote` price the moment a vendor's reply lands — is
  **not yet implemented** — model exists (`quote_submissions`), handler doesn't. A
  **manual** path to the same `source=rfq_quote` write exists today via the quote
  comparison spreadsheet's apply-pricing action (see "Quote comparison spreadsheet"
  below); that's an estimator picking among already-submitted quotes, not the inbound
  webhook itself.
- `get_quote_comparison(project_id)` — read-only: compares vendor `quote_submissions`
  from RFQ responses and direct document imports. Takeoff projects render matched quote
  lines; no-takeoff projects render persisted quote-material groups. Does not create an
  RFQ, send email, or ingest a vendor reply. Delegates to the same spreadsheet builder the
  `/api/projects/{id}/spreadsheets/quote-comparison/state` REST endpoint uses (see "Quote
  comparison spreadsheet" below), so its return shape is the full `SpreadsheetWorkbook`
  (per-cell formatting/overlay/protection metadata) built for the estimator grid UI, not a
  lean agent-specific projection — heavier than the other read tools here, worth trimming
  if it turns out to add noticeable noise to the agent's context.
- `list_vendor_quote_line_items(project_id)` — reads every
  `VendorQuoteLineItem` extracted from uploaded quote documents, matched and unmatched.
- `create_takeoff_line_item_from_vendor_quote(vendor_quote_line_item_id)` — the only tool
  that writes `takeoff_line_items` outside of CSV/XLSX ingestion. Its legacy MCP name
  promotes one unmatched quote line into a new takeoff line item.
  Raises if the line is already matched, or if it's missing quantity/unit (never
  fabricates placeholder values).

- `request_user_verification(project_id, kind, reason, payload, confidence)` — queues a
  low-confidence proposal for the user's explicit review instead of writing it. Only two
  `agent_raisable` kinds are allowed: `priced_line_proposal` (legacy, pre-Estimate
  projects — resolver routes through `write_priced_line` with `source=user_input`) and
  `estimate_component_price_proposal` (Estimate-domain — resolver routes through
  `write_estimate_component_price`). Use whichever matches the project's state; raising
  `priced_line_proposal` for an Estimate component targets the wrong table. Raising
  again for the same subject supersedes the earlier pending request. Not a direct write
  tool — the docstrings state this both ways.
- `list_pending_verifications(project_id)` — read-only view of the project's pending
  verification queue (all kinds, not just agent-raised ones), so the agent can report
  what's awaiting the user's review. Resolution itself is user-only, via REST.
- `price_estimate_items(project_id, estimate_item_ids)` — queues a background pass
  (`estimate.price_batch` job) that prices the given Estimate Items: one independent
  subagent per item, run concurrently across items (bounded by
  `settings.estimate_pricing_concurrency`), each pricing its own components one at a
  time. Validates the ids up front and returns a job id immediately — it does not price
  inline and does not return prices; check `get_estimate_pricing_status`/
  `get_estimate_items` afterward. The one entry point for both a hand-picked subset
  (infer ids from what the user asked for) and, later, a whole-project trigger — see
  "Parallel pricing execution" below.

There's also a `ping` tool (proves the `/mcp` mount works, nothing to do with pricing).
`app/core/agent/tools.py` excludes it when building the tool list for the chat agent so
it doesn't add noise to what the LLM has to reason about — external MCP clients (Claude
Code, Claude Desktop) still see it.

## Estimate artifact absorbs the legacy pricing artifact

The `"pricing"` `ArtifactKind` (backed by `PricedLineItem`, the pre-Estimate
per-takeoff-line world) is fully retired, not just deprioritized — `app/core/
artifacts/types.py`'s `ArtifactKind` no longer includes it, and `PricingArtifact`/
`PricingArtifactRow` are deleted. This follows the `write_priced_line`/
`get_pricing_status` MCP retirement (see "MCP tool catalog" above): since every project
is Estimate-based now, the Estimate artifact (`kind="estimate"`) already covers both
halves of what the pricing artifact used to show on its own — the unpriced structure
right after `propose_estimate_structure`/`categorize_estimate`, and the priced one once
`write_estimate_component_price`/`update_estimate_item`/the pricing batch fill it in —
so keeping a second artifact for the "priced" half was redundant, not complementary.

- **Removed**: `get_pricing_artifact` (`app/core/artifacts/service.py`), `pricing_ref`
  (its discovery/display-existence helper), the `GET /api/projects/{id}/artifacts/
  pricing` REST route, the `kind == "pricing"` branch in `display_artifact_ref`, and the
  `_refs_for_tool_call` auto-attach branch keyed on `{"get_pricing_status",
  "write_priced_line"}` — that branch was already dead code (those tool names haven't
  been in the MCP registry since the prior pass) before this cleanup removed it outright.
  `PricedLineItem`/`write_priced_line` themselves are untouched — they still back the
  legacy spreadsheet Apply-Pricing branch and the `priced_line_proposal` verification
  resolver for a pre-Estimate project (see "MCP tool catalog"'s retirement note); only
  the *artifact* (display/discovery layer) is gone, not the underlying write path.
- **`GET /overview`'s `pricing` section is now Estimate-based, not `PricedLineItem`-based.**
  `ProjectOverviewArtifacts.pricing` field is removed (there's no artifact ref for it
  anymore); `ProjectOverviewPricing` (the `pricing` field on the overview payload) keeps
  its existing shape (`priced_count`/`unpriced_count`/`extended_total`/`source_counts`/
  `last_priced_at`) but is now built by `estimates/service.py::get_estimate_pricing_summary`
  over `EstimateCostComponent`/`EstimateComponentPrice` (all components of the active
  Estimate, direct and project-wide, outer-joined to each one's latest price) instead of
  `PricedLineItem`. Unlike `get_estimate_pricing_status` (the MCP tool, which lists only
  priced components and raises if there's no active Estimate — correct for an agent that
  already confirmed one exists), `get_estimate_pricing_summary` counts unpriced
  components too and returns an all-zero summary rather than raising when a project
  hasn't been categorized into an Estimate yet, since a dashboard must render that state,
  not error on it.
- **`ProjectOverviewScope.assemblies[].priced_count` is also Estimate-based now**, via
  `app/core/project/overview.py::_priced_takeoff_line_item_ids` — a takeoff row counts as
  priced if it has a component-level `EstimateSourceLink` (a `row_type=material` mapping,
  see "Estimating domain model") into a component that currently has a price.
  Deliberately does **not** resolve item-level links (`installed_work`/`allowance`
  mappings) transitively through an item's components: an item-level link traces "this
  row seeded this item existing," not "this row's own material is priced," and the two
  are kept semantically distinct everywhere else in the domain model (see "Estimating
  domain model"'s provenance section) — conflating them here would be a new, undocumented
  exception to that rule, not a faithful port of the old per-line-item "is this priced"
  signal. This logic lives directly in `overview.py` rather than `estimates/service.py`,
  matching this file's existing pattern of querying `TakeoffLineItem`/`Rfq`/
  `QuoteSubmission` directly for dashboard-only aggregations — assembly grouping is a
  takeoff/overview concept, not an Estimate one (Estimate Items are never grouped by
  assembly; see "Estimating domain model").
- **Not verified against a live Postgres this pass** — same limitation as the rest of
  this session's work (no local Postgres credentials available); the two new queries
  (`get_estimate_pricing_summary`'s outer-join-to-latest-price subquery and
  `_priced_takeoff_line_item_ids`'s source-link join) were confirmed to compile to valid
  SQL against the real model definitions (`Compile()`), and `ruff`/`pytest`/a full
  `app.main` import all pass, but the actual aggregate numbers have not been checked
  against seeded rows in a real database. Run that pass, per the "How this was verified"
  convention, before trusting `GET /overview`'s `pricing`/`scope.assemblies[].priced_count`
  fields in production.

## Product chat agent (manual bridging loop)

`POST /api/projects/{project_id}/agent/messages` is a real product-chat surface — the
backend itself acts as the MCP *host*, not just the tool server. There's no external app
(Claude Code, Claude Desktop) doing the host job here. This section describes the main
project chat surface specifically — see "Agent run streaming and the three chat
surfaces" below for how it relates to `side_chat` and `user_wide_agent`, and for the
`start_message_run`/`complete_message_run` split that now sits in front of the
`handle_message` entrypoint described below (kept as a thin synchronous wrapper over
both, for callers that don't need to stream).

- **One tool registry, two consumption paths.** `app/core/agent/tools.py` calls
  `mcp.list_tools()` / `mcp.call_tool()` **in-process** on the same `FastMCP` instance
  Claude Code talks to over HTTP at `/mcp`. Tool schemas and behavior can never drift
  between "external MCP client" and "product chat" because there's only one definition.
- **Provider seam, not a single hardcoded call.** `app/core/agent/provider.py` defines
  `LLMProvider.run_agentic_turn(system, conversation) -> AgentTurnResult`. A provider owns the
  *entire* multi-turn tool-calling loop for one message, not just one HTTP request —
  Claude's content-block format and OpenAI's tool-calls format differ enough that
  leaking either into the orchestrator would defeat the seam. Two equivalent
  implementations exist: `ClaudeProvider` (`app/core/agent/claude_provider.py`, uses
  Claude's Messages API) and `OpenAIProvider` (`app/core/agent/openai_provider.py`, uses
  OpenAI's Responses API — the only OpenAI API surface that supports both custom tools
  and a hosted web search tool in one call). A third value selects
  `CodexSubscriptionProvider` — see the subscription-transport bullet below.
  `AGENT_LLM_PROVIDER=claude|openai|openai-codex` selects between them (default
  `claude`); the selection lives in `app/core/agent/factory.py`'s `get_llm_provider`,
  not in `provider.py` itself, since that module is deliberately implementation-free and
  every provider already imports from it — a factory there importing them back would be
  circular. (`orchestrator.py` still has a `_get_llm_provider`, but it is now a two-line
  delegate to `factory.get_llm_provider`, not the place the choice is actually made.)
  Both API-key providers bridge the same MCP tool
  registry, run a bounded multi-turn loop (`MAX_TOOL_TURNS = 12`,
  `MAX_OUTPUT_TOKENS = 8192` in each), declare a server-side web search tool
  (`web_search_20260209` for Claude, `web_search` for OpenAI) that's resolved entirely
  server-side within one API call, and treat a token-limit truncation the same way:
  run whatever tool calls did come through complete, then nudge the model to continue
  rather than discarding the turn's progress (Claude: `stop_reason == "max_tokens"`;
  OpenAI: `response.status == "incomplete"`). `run_agentic_turn` also takes an
  `allowed_tool_names: frozenset[str] | None` (a strict allow-list, applied before
  `excluded_tool_names`) and `include_server_tools: bool` (set `False` to hide the
  hosted web search tool from a bounded internal workflow) — see "Estimate
  categorization skill" below for where both are used.
- **`AGENT_LLM_PROVIDER=openai-codex` is a third provider: the same OpenAI agent loop
  run over a ChatGPT subscription instead of an API key.**
  `CodexSubscriptionProvider` (`app/core/agent/codex_provider.py`) **subclasses**
  `OpenAIProvider` rather than reimplementing it — it inherits the whole multi-turn
  tool-calling loop and overrides only what the subscription transport needs:
  `__init__` (holds a placeholder `AsyncOpenAI` the parent uses for capability
  detection only; every real request gets a freshly refreshed subscription-authenticated
  client), `_create_response`, `_stream_agentic_response`, and `run_structured_turn`.
  The recurring reason for the last two is the same: **the ChatGPT subscription
  Responses transport rejects `max_output_tokens` outright**, so
  `_stream_agentic_response` pops it out of the kwargs the parent loop supplied and
  `run_structured_turn` deletes its own copy, keeping the parameter in the signature
  for provider parity while letting the subscription endpoint apply its own output
  bound. Both request paths also force `store=False` and
  `include=["reasoning.encrypted_content"]`. Auth comes from
  `codex_auth.get_access_credentials` (account id + access token, sent as
  `ChatGPT-Account-ID` plus originator/version/session headers against
  `openai_codex_base_url`, default `https://chatgpt.com/backend-api/codex`), and a
  rate/usage cap raises `CodexQuotaError` — there is deliberately **no API-billing
  fallback**. `app/config.py` gates it: `agent_llm_provider` is a
  `Literal["claude", "openai", "openai-codex"]`, `resolved_openai_codex_model` falls
  back to `openai_model` when `openai_codex_model` is blank, and a non-development
  `openai-codex` run without `openai_codex_token_encryption_secret` fails startup.
  The same transport also serves ingestion-side vision (`run_codex_pdf_prompt`/
  `run_codex_image_prompt`).
- **A second, schema-constrained call shape exists alongside the tool-calling loop.**
  `LLMProvider.run_structured_turn[T: BaseModel](system, conversation, output_type,
  max_output_tokens, reasoning_effort) -> T` returns one tool-free, schema-validated
  response — for internal reducers that need reliable structured output without the
  overhead/nondeterminism of a full tool-calling turn, not for anything the chat host
  itself calls directly. Both `ClaudeProvider`/`OpenAIProvider` implement it by mapping
  `output_type` to their native structured-output API (Claude:
  `messages.parse(output_format=...)`). Its first (and so far only) caller is the
  "Document research workflow" above's planning/synthesis steps.
- **The orchestrator sees a provider-neutral final answer shape.** `app/core/agent/orchestrator.py`
  (`handle_message`) loads a project's `AgentMessage` history as plain
  `SimpleMessage(role, content)` pairs, hands them to the provider, and persists the
  final prose plus ordered `content_blocks` for UI placement. The tool_use/tool_result
  exchange within a turn is never persisted to chat history, matching the "chat history
  is continuity, not audit trail" rule.
- **`current_run_id` ContextVar threads the audit trail through, without touching the
  public tool schema.** Each call to `handle_message` creates one `AgentRun` row (one
  per chat message, covering however many tool calls that message took), sets
  `current_run_id` for the duration of the provider's loop, and every `run_logged_tool`
  call picks it up automatically as its `tool_calls.run_id` if the caller didn't pass one
  explicitly. External MCP clients never set this ContextVar, so their tool calls still
  get `run_id = NULL` exactly as before — this is additive, not a behavior change for
  Claude Code.
- **Separation between projects is structural, not special-cased.** Every query is
  scoped by `project_id`; two projects' conversations, runs, and tool calls never share
  any in-memory state, only the underlying `historical_prices`/`vendor_contacts` tables
  (which are intentionally company-wide, not project-scoped — see the cross-project
  historical-match test in "How this was verified").
- **Guardrails and the pricing-flow policy live in the system prompt**
  (`SYSTEM_PROMPT_TEMPLATE` in `orchestrator.py`), not in the tools — refusal of
  off-domain questions, the historical→estimate/RFQ/user-input fallback order, and
  "always write or queue a price, never just say a number in chat" are all prompt-level
  rules the orchestrator doesn't enforce in code. If those need to be hard guarantees
  rather than LLM-followed conventions, that's future work. **One exception**: a skill
  turn's tool exclusions (`app.core.agent.skills`, see "Estimate categorization skill" below)
  are a real code-level guarantee, not a prompt convention — `run_agentic_turn`'s
  `excluded_tool_names` param removes the excluded tools from what the provider even
  sends the model, so `categorize_estimate` is structurally incapable of calling a
  pricing-write tool regardless of what the model decides to do.

## Identifiers in chat prose, and clickable Estimate row links

Two halves of one problem on the "Ask about this estimate" surface: raw UUIDs were
reaching the user's screen, and there was no way for the agent to point at an Estimate
row except by printing its id.

**The leak, and why it happened.** A real reply (project "Test Project",
`agent_messages.id=1b7135c3-…`) opened "I chose the selected components because they are
the QTO rows…" and then listed six components by raw UUID. Confirmed against the DB, not
inferred: those six are exactly the `logical_key`s of the six `workbook_annotation`
blocks on the preceding user message (`0fd26ec1-…`), in order — the user had selected an
item plus six component cells. They are not takeoff rows at all (none of the ids exist in
`takeoff_line_items`); the model's own "QTO rows" wording was wrong too.
`_with_annotation_context` (`orchestrator.py`) rendered each selection as
`component "<name>" (logical_key: <uuid>)` and then instructed the model to "address each
selection directly, using its logical_key with get_estimate_draft/patch_estimate_draft."
Both named tools are dead ends here — `patch_estimate_draft` is excluded from plain chat,
and `get_estimate_draft`'s `ItemOut`/`ComponentOut` expose no logical key at all — so
there was no way to *use* the id except to repeat it, and no rule anywhere forbidding
that.

- **The key still gets passed** — `set_component_price` consumes exactly this value, and
  the links below need it. What changed is that it is labelled machine-only, the
  instruction now names `set_component_price` (the one tool that actually takes it), and
  the model is told to refer to each selection by its quoted name.
- **`csi_node_id` is now in `_CONTEXT_EXCLUDED_FIELDS`** (`chat_annotations.py`). It is a
  top-level scalar on `InstalledWorkItemDraft`, so it landed in `snapshot_values`
  automatically and was a second raw UUID in the same prompt text, with no agent-facing
  use — the CSI code/title it stands for already arrive via `get_estimate_draft`.
- **An "Identifier policy" section in `CONTENT_BLOCKS_FORMAT_INSTRUCTIONS`**
  (`app/core/agent/content.py`) generalizes the rule, because the annotation suffix was
  not the only leak: `get_takeoff_line_items.id`, `QtoRowCoverageOut.takeoff_line_item_id`,
  and `ComponentPricingOut.component_id` are all reachable in freeform and all previously
  undocumented for prose. That section is appended to *every* turn, so the rule covers
  skills too. It also promotes the `R<n>`/`C<n>` display aliases to a prompt-level
  convention — until now `ComponentPricingOut.component_ref`'s field docstring ("for
  prose/questions") was the only place that convention existed anywhere.

**`workbook_ref`, the inverse of a workbook annotation.** An annotation is the user
pointing at a cell; a `workbook_ref` is the assistant pointing back. It is a fourth
`AgentResponseBlock` (alongside text/artifact/citation) and a new `ChatContentBlock`,
carrying `anchor_text` + `target_kind` + `logical_key`, enriched server-side with `label`
and `artifact_id`. `anchor_text` is a verbatim substring of the message's own prose (the
material name as written) that the frontend wraps in a link; there is deliberately **no
visible marker** like a citation's `[N]`, since the entire point is that the reader sees
a name and never an id. `logical_key` equals `EstimateItemOut.id`/`EstimateComponentOut.id`
in the Estimate artifact payload verbatim (both are `str(logical_*_key)`), so a click
resolves a row by direct id equality with no extra request — which is why the click
target is the existing `kind="estimate"` artifact rather than the workbook grid (the
workbook is REST-only and not a registered `ArtifactKind`).

- **Validation is post-turn, modeled on `_enrich_citations`.**
  `orchestrator._resolve_workbook_refs` drops any reference whose `logical_key` isn't a
  row of the project's current draft, whose `anchor_text` doesn't occur in the message's
  own prose, or that duplicates a row already linked. **Dropping, not keeping, is the
  deliberate difference from citations**: an unresolvable citation is kept because the
  frontend can retry it through the evidence resolve endpoint, whereas a stale row id has
  no retry path — and dropping is lossless here, since the anchor text is already
  ordinary prose and simply renders unlinked.
- **`lookup_workbook_row_labels`** (`chat_annotations.py`) is the read-only, non-raising
  counterpart to `resolve_workbook_annotations`, sharing one `_draft_row_lookups` helper
  rather than duplicating graph loading in the orchestrator. The two differ on purpose:
  an unknown key from a *user* click is a bad request, while an unknown key from the
  *model* just means the reference gets dropped.
- **Scope limit**: the agent can only link a row it was handed the key for — a selected
  cell, or a component from `get_estimate_pricing_state`. Since `get_estimate_draft`
  returns no logical keys, a reply naming a component nobody selected generally cannot
  link it and writes the name unlinked. Adding keys to that read shape is the follow-up
  if it matters.
- **Additive and frontend-gated.** No migration (`content_blocks` is JSON), no changed
  field, no changed endpoint; an unrendering frontend just shows plain text. The
  rendering contract is `docs/chat-workbook-refs-frontend-handoff.md`, following the
  `docs/price-book-frontend-handoff.md` precedent.
- **Not verified**: no live LLM run has emitted a `workbook_ref` yet — the block,
  validation, and prompt layers are unit-tested (`tests/test_agent_runs.py`,
  `tests/test_chat_annotations.py`), but whether the model reliably emits one with a
  correctly copied `anchor_text`, and whether the prompt changes actually stop the UUID
  echo under a real model, both need a live pass. The exact reproducing input is still in
  the DB (user message `0fd26ec1-…` and its seven annotation blocks) — replay that turn
  and assert no UUID appears in the persisted `body`/`content_blocks`. This environment
  is `AGENT_LLM_PROVIDER=openai`; the Claude path is unverified, as everywhere else here.

## Estimate categorization skill

`categorize_estimate` is the first (and so far only) entry in `app.core.agent.skills`'s
`SKILL_DIRECTIVES`/`SKILL_ALLOWED_TOOLS` — a fixed, mandatory procedure the estimator
triggers explicitly, distinct from the freeform "Estimate workflow" prose in
`SYSTEM_PROMPT_TEMPLATE` that only runs when the model itself judges a message calls for
it. Freeform chat's own "Estimate workflow" prose still reasons tool-by-tool over
`inspect_drawing_sheet`/`search_project_documents` directly and is never routed through
`research_project_documents`; only this skill's dedicated procedure is.

- **Trigger is a structured field, not text parsing.** `POST .../agent/messages`
  (`SendMessageRequest.skill: Literal["categorize_estimate"] | None`) — the frontend
  renders the "/" affordance and sets this explicitly, the same way `mentioned_document_ids`
  is already a structured field rather than something parsed out of the raw message body.
  `handle_message(..., skill=...)` threads it through.
- **This skill gets an entirely separate system prompt, not just an appended directive.**
  `orchestrator.py::_build_system_prompt` swaps in `CATEGORIZATION_SYSTEM_PROMPT_TEMPLATE`
  (its own restatement of the Estimate invariants, tuned for this skill) instead of the
  ordinary `SYSTEM_PROMPT_TEMPLATE` when `skill == "categorize_estimate"`, then still
  appends `SKILL_DIRECTIVES["categorize_estimate"]` and
  `ESTIMATE_RESEARCH_ADMISSION_RULES` (`app.core.agent.skills`) on top. The directive
  itself no longer reads as a literal three-question walk — it's a multi-step audit: form
  a provisional structure from the QTO, run an explicit multi-system material-allocation
  sweep and a labor-coverage sweep across every provisional item, convert only genuinely
  consequential gaps into atomic questions for `research_project_documents` (submitted as
  one batched call — see "Document research workflow" above), then map rows and propose.
  The admission rules exist specifically to stop the skill from either over-researching
  (asking about everything) or under-researching (guessing past a real gap) — research
  only a decision with a credible competing interpretation, never for reassurance.
  `include_server_tools=False` for this skill turn — web search is unavailable, keeping
  categorization grounded only in this project's own documents and QTO.
- **Tool restriction is now a hard allow-list, not an exclude-list.** The
  `LLMProvider.run_agentic_turn` ABC (`app/core/agent/provider.py`) takes both an
  `excluded_tool_names: frozenset[str]` and a newer `allowed_tool_names: frozenset[str] |
  None` param, both threaded into `list_tool_definitions()`/`execute_tool()`
  (`app/core/agent/tools.py`) — a call naming a disallowed tool is hard-rejected before it
  reaches `app.mcp.server.mcp`, not merely discouraged. **Stale below, corrected in
  "Stage 2-4"**: `SKILL_ALLOWED_TOOLS["categorize_estimate"]` as described in this
  paragraph (`{get_takeoff_line_items, research_project_documents,
  get_estimate_proposal, patch_estimate_proposal, finalize_estimate_proposal}`) was the
  v1 optimized-mode set; the Stage 2-4 rewrite replaced it with a 12-tool set that
  includes `get_project_scope_brief`/`get_project_document_summaries`/
  `search_document_understanding`/`list_document_understanding_cards`/
  `get_document_outline`/`search_project_documents`/`read_project_document_section`/
  `inspect_drawing_sheet` alongside `research_project_documents` and the three
  `get`/`patch`/`finalize_estimate_draft` v2 tools — see "Stage 2-4"'s own "New MCP
  tools" subsection for the current, accurate list. The direct document-read tools were
  never removed for this skill; what changed in the research-pipeline pass below is only
  that they're the escalation path (a `stop_reason` retrieval gap, a returned conflict,
  a verbatim schedule cell a synthesized claim flattened, a gap a rejected patch
  exposed), not the default one — a single batched `research_project_documents` call is.
  The real, still-accurate guarantee this hard allow-list gives: no pricing-write tool
  is reachable from this skill regardless of which document tools are.
- **Managed categorization is resumable and terminal only on finalization.** The
  orchestrator opens or resumes an `EstimateProposal`, preloads its QTO aliases and
  persisted research facts, and requires explicit allocation/exclusion/unresolved
  coverage for every row. Patches replace only named item keys. A successful
  `finalize_estimate_proposal` is terminal and deterministically displays the committed
  artifact; provider failure leaves the draft and active Estimate intact.
- **No more fixed 40-tool-turn override.** `SKILL_MAX_TOOL_TURNS["categorize_estimate"]`
  was removed (the dict is empty), reverting this skill to each provider's default budget
  of 12 — the old override existed because the now-deleted `categorize_batch.py`
  pre-investigation pass made every categorization turn long-running by construction; the
  batched research tool is expected to need far fewer round-trips, though this hasn't been
  load-tested against a large, ambiguity-heavy real project (see "Document research
  workflow"'s unhandled >12-question overflow case above).
- **`propose_estimate_structure` requires a `research_dispositions` argument on every
  call, not only from this skill.** One `EstimateResearchDispositionIn` (`question_id`,
  `action` — `applied_facts`/`decision_left_unresolved`/`no_decision_change` —,
  `rationale`) per question the proposal's reasoning actually drew on, or `[]` when no
  research was needed; it's a tool-call audit trail only, never persisted as Estimate
  content, and is only logged into `tool_calls.arguments`, not stored on the Estimate
  itself.
- **No new `AgentRun` field for skill attribution** — deliberately, to avoid a second
  migration alongside `EstimateItem.evidence` (see "Database state" below). If skill
  attribution is needed later, prefix `AgentRun.goal` instead of adding a column.

## Skill auto-detection

A skill's stricter procedure previously only ran when the frontend's "/" picker set
`SendMessageRequest.skill` explicitly — a user who typed the same intent in ordinary
chat ("build out the estimate structure," "go ahead and price the rest of the
estimate") got freeform chat instead, missing the research-driven categorization rigor
or the whole-Estimate pricing queue entirely. `app/core/agent/skill_router.py` closes
this gap with a small, bounded classifier, not text/keyword parsing — it reuses the
same `LLMProvider.run_structured_turn` seam "Document research workflow" built for
schema-constrained, tool-free reasoning.

- **Whitelisted and conservative.** `SKILL_AUTO_INVOCABLE = {"categorize_estimate",
  "price_estimate"}` gates which skills this mechanism may ever select — extensible
  for future skills, not automatic for every entry in `SkillName`. `detect_skill`
  (called from `orchestrator.handle_message`, only when the caller left `skill=None`
  and `settings.agent_skill_auto_detection_enabled` is true) sends the last
  `SKILL_DETECTION_HISTORY_LIMIT` (6) conversation messages to one `run_structured_turn`
  call against `SkillDetection` (`skill`, `confidence: low|medium|high`, `rationale`).
  Only a `"high"`-confidence, non-null result (checked by
  `orchestrator._resolve_effective_skill`, not inside `detect_skill` itself) ever
  overrides freeform chat — `"low"`/`"medium"`/`skill=None` all mean "proceed as
  ordinary chat," matching the deliberately cautious posture of not guessing past real
  ambiguity that already governs `categorize_estimate`'s own research admission rules.
- **`price_estimate` has a narrower trigger than `categorize_estimate`.**
  `SKILL_TRIGGER_GUIDANCE["price_estimate"]` explicitly instructs the classifier to
  match only a request to price the *entire* remaining Estimate ("price everything,"
  "price out what's left") — never a request naming one material, labor activity, CSI
  code, or a named subset ("price the drywall labor," "price these 3 items"). That
  narrower case must fall through to ordinary freeform chat, which already calls
  `price_estimate_items` directly with just the relevant ids — auto-invoking the
  `price_estimate` *skill* for a subset request would be wrong, since that skill's
  directive always queues every unpriced item in the whole active Estimate, regardless
  of what the user actually asked for.
- **Fails closed, same convention as the takeoff-column-mapping LLM fallback.** Any
  exception in `detect_skill` (timeout, malformed structured output, provider error) is
  caught and treated as "no match," mirroring
  `app.core.ingestion.column_mapping.maybe_map_columns_with_llm`'s existing
  LLM-failure-returns-`None` pattern — a classifier hiccup must never break or block an
  ordinary chat turn. `agent_skill_auto_detection_enabled` (`app/config.py`, default
  `True`) is a plain kill switch if it needs disabling without a code change.
- **Never overrides an explicit choice, and is visible when it fires.**
  `_resolve_effective_skill` only ever fills in a skill the caller left unset; it can
  never override `SendMessageRequest.skill`. When it does apply, `_build_system_prompt`
  appends one extra instruction telling the model to open its reply with a short, plain
  sentence stating it's treating the message as a `categorize_estimate`/`price_estimate`
  request — so a user isn't confused by a turn that behaves differently (no web search,
  a restricted tool set) than an ordinary chat reply would. The turn is also tagged for
  audit purposes: `AgentRun.goal` is prefixed with `f"auto_skill:{skill}:"`, the same
  no-migration-needed convention already used for
  `"estimate.price_batch:{job_id}:item:{item_id}"`.
- **Not verified against a live LLM this pass** — same limitation as the rest of this
  repo's LLM-tool-choice-dependent work (see "How this was verified"): fake-provider
  unit tests (`tests/test_skill_router.py`, `tests/test_orchestrator_skill_routing.py`)
  confirm the confidence-threshold/fail-closed/never-override-explicit-choice logic and
  the transparency-instruction wiring, but whether the classifier prompt actually
  distinguishes "price everything" from "price this one item" reliably in practice
  needs a manual pass against a real project before trusting it in production.

## Parallel pricing execution (retired — moved to docs/)

Retired: `price_estimate_items`/`estimate.price_batch`/`app/core/agent/pricing_batch.py`
are all gone. Pricing now happens through the interactive, reference-only tools in
"Stage 5: interactive pricing" below instead of a background batch fan-out; there is
currently no batch/parallel pricing mechanism at all. Full design writeup (bounded
concurrent fan-out, per-item independent LLM turns, why `max_attempts=1`) moved to
`docs/history/v1-tool-surface-and-retired-designs.md`.

## Live tool-call visibility and queued-job tracking

**This polling design is now the fallback/discovery mechanism, not the primary one —
see "Agent run streaming and the three chat surfaces" below, which describes the SSE
event stream that superseded it as the main live-progress channel.** Everything below
is still live and still correct (`GET .../agent/runs/current` still exists, still reads
`current_tool_name`/`current_tool_started_at`), it just isn't the whole story anymore:
`AgentRunCurrentOut` now also returns a `stream_url`, so the intended flow is poll
`runs/current` once to *discover* an in-progress run, then switch to its SSE stream for
live updates, rather than keep polling. `estimate.price_batch` (referenced below) no
longer exists — see "Parallel pricing execution" above — but `AgentRun.kind`/
`current_tool_name` and `Job.run_id`/`queued_jobs` remain accurate as a general
mechanism for whatever else queues a job from inside a tool-calling loop today.

The frontend previously had no way to show anything more specific than "thinking"
while a chat turn ran, and no visibility into `estimate.price_batch` work still
running after the main chat answer already returned. Three additive pieces close
that gap, all keyed off the existing `AgentRun`/`ToolCall`/`Job` audit trail rather
than a new tracking system:

- **`AgentRun.kind`** (`"chat"` default, `"price_item"` for a
  `price_one_estimate_item` pricing subagent — see "Parallel pricing execution")
  distinguishes a main chat turn from a pricing subagent's own run. Needed because
  both share `project_id`, and without this a concurrently-running pricing subagent
  could be picked up instead of the actual chat turn a poll is waiting on. This is a
  first-class column alongside, not a replacement for, the pre-existing convention of
  encoding a subagent's batch/item identity as text in `AgentRun.goal`
  (`"estimate.price_batch:{job_id}:item:{item_id}"`) — that stays exactly as it was.
- **Live "current tool" tracking** — `AgentRun.current_tool_name`/
  `current_tool_started_at` are set by `app/core/audit.py::set_current_tool` from
  inside `app/core/agent/tools.py::execute_tool`, the one function both
  `ClaudeProvider` and `OpenAIProvider` already call through for every
  client-dispatched MCP tool call. Set right before `mcp.call_tool`, cleared again
  (both fields back to `NULL`) right after, success or failure — a `finally` block, so
  a finished tool never shows as still in flight. Two small point-`UPDATE`s per tool
  call, chosen deliberately over a cheaper "set-only, clear when the whole turn ends"
  design: the latter would leave a stale tool name visible while the LLM is just
  generating text between calls, undermining the point of a live-progress signal.
  `GET /api/projects/{id}/agent/runs/current` (`app/api/agent.py`) reads this —
  returns the most recently started `kind="chat"` run still `in_progress` for a
  project, or `null` if none — for the frontend to poll (its own cadence, no
  backend-side polling interval imposed) while waiting on `POST .../messages`.
- **`AgentMessageOut.tool_calls`** (`app/api/agent.py::_agent_message_out`, shared by
  `GET`/`POST .../messages` so both get it identically) — every `ToolCall` row for
  that message's `run_id`, in order, as `{tool_name, display_name, status,
  latency_ms}`. Populated only for an assistant message with a `run_id` (mirrors the
  pre-existing legacy-artifact-inference conditional already in that function).
  **Web search is deliberately absent** — Anthropic/OpenAI execute it server-side and
  it never passes through `execute_tool`, so it's never logged as a `ToolCall` at all;
  confirmed as a decision, not an oversight (see Open follow-ups).
- **`display_name_for_tool`** (`app/core/agent/tools.py`) — a small pure function,
  `search_historical_material_price` → `"Search Historical Material Price"`,
  `create_rfq` → `"Create RFQ"` (a tiny acronym table, `{"rfq": "RFQ"}` today, extend
  as needed). Sent as an *additional* field alongside the raw `tool_name`, in both
  `tool_calls` entries and `runs/current`'s `current_tool_display_name` — the
  frontend keeps the stable machine-readable identifier and doesn't have to
  reimplement the formatting itself.
- **`Job.run_id`** (nullable FK to `agent_runs.id`) links a queued job back to the
  chat `AgentRun` that queued it, if any. Only `price_estimate_items`
  (`app/mcp/tools/estimates.py`) sets it today, via `current_run_id.get()` — the only
  job-creation call site that runs from inside an agent tool-calling loop; the other
  three (ingestion, `rfq.send`, quote regroup) are queued from REST routes directly,
  where `current_run_id` is already unset, so `run_id` stays correctly `NULL` there
  without touching those call sites.
- **`AgentMessageOut.queued_jobs`** — `{job_id, job_type}` for every `Job` with
  `run_id == message.run_id`. Paired with new **`GET /api/projects/{id}/jobs/{job_id}`**
  (`app/api/jobs.py`, generic — not `estimate.price_batch`-specific) returning
  `{status, attempts, max_attempts, result, error, ...}`. This is what lets the
  frontend show "still pricing in the background" after the main chat answer already
  came back: read `queued_jobs` off the message, poll the job's status while
  `pending`/`running`. `Job` has no `project_id` column, so the endpoint scopes via
  `job.payload["project_id"]`, matching every job handler's existing convention.
  **Deliberately not built here**: a reverse FK from `Job` to the `AgentRun`s *it
  spawns* (i.e. `estimate.price_batch`'s N `price_one_estimate_item` subagent runs)
  — without it, `GET /jobs/{id}` gives "batch running/done" but not "3 of 5 items
  priced" granularity. Noted as a follow-up, not a gap in this pass's own scope.

## Agent run streaming and the three chat surfaces

A real Server-Sent-Events architecture now exists on top of the polling design above,
and there are three distinct agent "surfaces" in this codebase, not one — none of this
is documented anywhere else in this file.

### The SSE run-event stream

`AgentRunEvent` (`app/models/audit.py`) is a new table: `run_id` (FK → `agent_runs.id`,
cascade-deleted), `user_id`, `sequence` (a DB `Identity()` `BigInteger`, globally
monotonic, unique, indexed together with `run_id`), `event_type`, a JSON `payload`, plus
the usual `created_at`. `sequence` is what resumption keys off. `TERMINAL_EVENT_TYPES =
{"final_message", "run_failed", "run_stopped"}` (`app/core/agent/events.py`). Other
observed event types: `run_started`, `tool_call_preparing`, `tool_call_started`,
`tool_call_finished`, `skill_selected`, `assistant_message_delta`,
`assistant_reasoning_summary_delta` (OpenAI-only), `provider_response_retry`, and
`project_handoff_suggested` (user-wide agent only).

**The start/complete split.** Every surface now separates fast DB prep from the actual
LLM turn: `start_*_run` persists the user message, creates an `in_progress` `AgentRun`
row, emits `run_started`, and returns immediately with `{run_id, stream_url}` — before
the LLM has produced anything. The caller backgrounds the real work
(`asyncio.create_task(complete_*_run(...))`) so the frontend can open the SSE stream
against `run_id` right away instead of blocking on the whole turn. All three surfaces
use this pattern: main chat (`orchestrator.start_message_run`/`complete_message_run`
— `handle_message` is now a thin synchronous wrapper over both, kept for non-streaming
callers), `user_wide_agent` (`app/core/user_wide_agent/orchestrator.py`, same two
function names), and `side_chat` (`start_side_chat_message`/`complete_side_chat_run`).

**The stream endpoints**, one per surface, near-identical bodies: `GET /api/projects/
{id}/agent/runs/{run_id}/events/stream`, `GET /api/user-wide-agent/conversations/{id}/
runs/{run_id}/events/stream`, `GET /api/projects/{id}/side-chat/runs/{run_id}/events/
stream`. Each resumes from a `Last-Event-ID` header or `after_sequence` query param
(header wins if further along), polls the DB every 0.5s, sends an `: heartbeat\n\n`
SSE comment every 15s of no new rows, and breaks the loop (emitting one final synthetic
`done: {run_id, status}` event) either on a `TERMINAL_EVENT_TYPES` row or when no new
rows arrive and `run.status != "in_progress"`. Real events are emitted as `id:
<sequence>` / `event: <event_type>` / `data: <json>` via `app/api/agent.py`'s
`_sse_event`/`_agent_run_event_out`, shared (not reimplemented) by the other two
routers.

**The old polling design coexists, as a fallback/discovery mechanism** — see "Live
tool-call visibility and queued-job tracking" above for what it still does and why it
hasn't been removed.

### `AgentRun.kind` values

`"chat"` (default — main project chat), `"project_setup"` (the `identify_scope` skill's
automatic orient/resolve turns, see "Thin mandatory project setup" below), `"side_chat"`,
`"user_wide_chat"`. `"price_item"` is a retired value that may still appear on old rows
from the deleted per-item pricing subagent (see "Parallel pricing execution" above) —
don't build new logic that expects to see it created going forward.

**The isolating rule is the same on every surface, and is the thing that keeps one
surface's run from being adoptable through another's route**: every stream/events/stop
endpoint re-derives the run by id and checks both project/conversation ownership *and*
`AgentRun.kind` before accepting a `run_id` — main chat's `_ensure_chat_run` accepts
`kind in ("chat", "project_setup")`, `side_chat`'s `_ensure_side_chat_run` requires
exactly `kind == "side_chat"`, `user_wide_agent`'s `_ensure_run` requires exactly
`kind == "user_wide_chat"`. A `run_id` from one surface 404s against another surface's
stream route by construction, not by convention.

### The three surfaces

- **Main project chat** (`app/core/agent/orchestrator.py`) — project-scoped, the full
  read/write tool catalog gated per-skill (see "Estimate categorization skill" and
  "Skill auto-detection" above); this is everything the rest of this file's "Product
  chat agent" section describes.
- **`side_chat`** (`app/core/agent/side_chat.py`, `app/api/side_chat.py`) — also
  project-scoped, but structurally read-only via a hard `SIDE_CHAT_ALLOWED_TOOLS`
  allow-list that excludes every write tool. Seeded with the main chat's recent history
  as read-only context (`_render_main_chat_context`, capped to the most recent 50
  messages) but never writes back into it — its own messages live in a separate
  `SideChatMessage`/`side_chat_sessions` table. Has no `GET` history-replay endpoint at
  all, which is why the terminal SSE event (`final_message`/`run_failed`) has to carry
  the complete finished-turn payload inline (session_id/body/tool_calls) — it is the
  only place a finished side-chat turn's content is ever recoverable from, not just a
  progress signal. **This entire start/stream/complete implementation is committed and
  live** (confirmed 2026-08-25 against `HEAD`: `app/core/agent/side_chat.py` defines
  `start_side_chat_message`/`complete_side_chat_run`, `app/api/side_chat.py` serves
  `POST .../messages/start` and `GET .../runs/{run_id}/events/stream`, and all three
  files including `tests/test_side_chat.py` are clean in the working tree) — the older
  synchronous, non-streaming `send_side_chat_message` it replaced is gone. Still not
  verified against a live LLM turn, per this file's own "How this was verified"
  convention.
- **`user_wide_agent`** (`app/core/user_wide_agent/`, `app/api/user_wide_agent.py`) —
  the one surface that is *not* project-scoped: cross-project, user-scoped, under `/api/
  user-wide-agent/conversations/{id}/...` rather than `/api/projects/{id}/...`. Its own
  tool set (`user_wide.py` in the MCP registry — `list_user_projects`,
  `get_project_overview`, `list_global_memories`/`write_global_memory`/
  `forget_global_memory`) is read-only over projects plus global-memory writes and web
  search; it cannot act inside a specific project. Instead it can emit a
  `project_handoff_suggested` event/block directing the user into a specific project's
  main chat to actually do the work — a routing surface, not a second place estimating
  logic lives. `list_memories`/`write_memory`/`forget_memory` (project-scoped, § MCP
  tool catalog / "Agent memory" below) and `list_global_memories`/`write_global_memory`/
  `forget_global_memory` (user-scoped, this surface) are two genuinely separate stores,
  not the same tool under two names.

## Agent memory

Freeform chat (never `categorize_estimate`/`determine_scope`/`price_estimate`) can save
a durable, repeating user/workspace preference or workflow pattern -- not a one-off
project fact -- so it's available in every future chat turn, on any project. Modeled
after ChatGPT/Claude's own memory feature, deliberately much smaller than an earlier,
abandoned `agent/memory-architecture` branch (6 typed memory kinds, versioned
revisions, hybrid pg_trgm+pgvector retrieval, a bundled Clerk multi-tenant auth system
this codebase doesn't have): **one plain table, three MCP tools, no embeddings.**

- **`AgentMemory`** (`app/models/memory.py`, `agent_memories` table): `scope`
  (`"global"` | `"project"`), `project_id` (nullable, null iff `scope="global"` --
  enforced by a DB `CHECK` constraint, same paired-condition pattern as
  `estimate_item_revisions`' unit-resolution constraint, backstopping
  `app/core/memory/service.py`'s own `_validate_scope_project`), `text` (capped 300
  chars, one plain sentence), `category` (free label, not enforced), `source_run_id`
  (nullable FK to `agent_runs.id`). No revisioning and no soft-delete status column --
  `forget_memory` hard-deletes; the `tool_calls` audit row is this feature's
  provenance record, not a stored status.
- **No retrieval machinery.** `active_memory_facts_for_prompt` fetches every
  `scope="global"` fact plus the current project's `scope="project"` facts, most-
  recently-updated first, capped at `settings.agent_memory_max_injected_facts`
  (default 40) -- fetch-all and inject, the same mechanical shape ChatGPT/Claude use at
  this scale (dozens of facts, not thousands). A separate
  `agent_memory_max_active_per_scope` cap (default 200, per scope bucket) rejects a new
  (non-update) write past it with a message telling the agent to `forget_memory`
  something first -- a runaway-write backstop, not a working limit any real workspace
  should approach. `agent_memory_enabled` is a plain kill switch (same posture as
  `agent_skill_auto_detection_enabled`) that disables only the per-turn injection
  fetch, not the tools themselves.
- **Three MCP tools** (`app/mcp/tools/memory.py`): `write_memory` (create, or update in
  place when `memory_id` is given -- the model's correct-instead-of-duplicate path),
  `list_memories` (read-only, for a dedupe check before writing), `forget_memory` (hard
  delete). **Freeform-chat-only by construction**: none of the three are in any
  skill's `SKILL_ALLOWED_TOOLS` (`app/core/agent/skills.py`), so they are structurally
  unreachable during a skill turn -- no exclude-list or runtime check needed, the same
  guarantee `categorize_estimate`'s hard allow-list already gives pricing-write tools
  (see "Estimate categorization skill" above). Regression-guarded by
  `tests/test_agent_tools.py::test_memory_tools_unreachable_during_skill_turns`
  (asserts disjointness against all three skills' allow-lists).
- **Prompt injection is two-tiered, deliberately different by skill.**
  `_build_system_prompt` (`app/core/agent/orchestrator.py`) appends the *facts
  themselves* (a plain bullet block) whenever `memory_facts` is non-empty, regardless
  of skill -- harmless read-only context, useful even mid-`categorize_estimate`. It
  only appends `MEMORY_TOOL_INSTRUCTIONS` (how/when to use the three tools) when
  `skill is None`, since that's the only turn kind where they're actually callable --
  telling the model about tools it can't reach during a skill turn would just be noise.
  `run_agent_turn` takes an optional `memory_facts: Sequence[str] = ()` param threaded
  straight into `_build_system_prompt`; `complete_message_run` (the chat-turn wrapper)
  fetches it via `active_memory_facts_for_prompt` and passes it down. The
  `scope_batch.get_or_create_scope_brief` nested `determine_scope` call (the only other
  `run_agent_turn` caller) doesn't pass it and simply runs memory-free -- accepted scope
  for this pass, not a gap requiring a fix.
- **Brace-safety is load-bearing, not incidental.** A saved fact is model-authored free
  text and could itself contain a literal `{...}` sequence. `_memory_facts_section`
  builds its block via plain string joining and is appended to `sections` *after*
  `SYSTEM_PROMPT_TEMPLATE`/`CATEGORIZATION_SYSTEM_PROMPT_TEMPLATE`'s own `.format()`
  call already ran -- never merged into that `.format()` call itself, which would crash
  every subsequent turn's prompt assembly on any fact containing `{`. Regression-tested
  (`tests/test_agent_memory_prompt.py::test_brace_containing_fact_does_not_crash_prompt_assembly`).
- **REST surface** (`app/api/memory.py`, `/api/memories`) for a future "manage
  memories" settings page: `GET` (optional `scope`/`project_id` filters), `PATCH`,
  `DELETE`. Top-level, not nested under a project, since global memories have no
  project to nest under. The chat agent itself only ever uses the MCP tools.
- **A real async-SQLAlchemy bug was found and fixed via live verification, not caught
  by any fake-session unit test.** `AgentMemory.updated_at` is a server-side
  `onupdate=func.now()` column (`TimestampMixin`); SQLAlchemy's default
  `eager_defaults=False` means an UPDATE (not INSERT) leaves that column *expired*
  rather than eagerly `RETURNING`-refreshed, and reading it afterward triggers a lazy
  reload that raises `MissingGreenlet` under the async engine -- reproduced against the
  real shared DB's schema on a throwaway Neon branch, both through `write_memory`'s
  `memory_id` update path and through the REST `PATCH`/`DELETE` routes (which
  originally serialized to `MemoryOut` *after* `session.commit()`, which
  `expire_on_commit=True` -- the default -- would have expired identically for every
  field, not just `updated_at`). Fixed two ways, matching the two failure sites:
  `write_memory`'s update branch and `patch_memory` (`app/core/memory/service.py`) now
  call `await session.refresh(row)` after `flush()`; both REST routes
  (`app/api/memory.py`) now build the `MemoryOut` result *before* `session.commit()`,
  matching `app/api/verifications.py::resolve_verification`'s existing (already-correct)
  ordering. No fake-session test can catch this class of bug -- a mock's `flush()`/
  `commit()`/`refresh()` are no-ops that never actually expire an attribute the way
  real SQLAlchemy does; only a live-Postgres pass exercises it. Worth remembering for
  any *other* in-place update elsewhere in this codebase that reads `updated_at`
  immediately afterward within the same session -- most existing code avoids this
  simply because its output types don't include `updated_at` at all (e.g.
  `VerificationRequestOut` only has `created_at`), not because the underlying pattern
  is actually safe.
- **Migration**: `alembic/versions/b1c2d3e4f5a6_add_agent_memories.py` (additive-only,
  one table). **Applied to a throwaway Neon branch of the shared DB this pass**
  (forked from and deleted after) -- confirmed the live schema (columns, both FKs, the
  composite index, the `CHECK` constraint) matches the model exactly, confirmed
  `write_memory`/`list_memories`/`forget_memory`/`patch_memory` against real rows
  (including the cap rejecting a write past `agent_memory_max_active_per_scope` and the
  `CHECK` constraint rejecting a validation-bypassed bad row), and confirmed all three
  MCP tools through the real `execute_tool`/`mcp.call_tool` dispatch path (not just
  direct Python calls to the service functions) -- **not yet applied to the actual
  shared/production DB**. Per this repo's live-DB verification discipline: apply
  `alembic upgrade head` to the shared DB as an explicit, separate step **before**
  deploying this branch's application code -- `complete_message_run`'s
  `active_memory_facts_for_prompt` call would otherwise `SELECT` against a nonexistent
  table on every single chat turn, degrading to the same generic
  `"Sorry, I hit an error..."` failure this function already produces for any other
  exception -- a full chat outage, not a memory-only degradation.
- **Not verified this pass**: a live LLM turn actually calling `write_memory`/
  `list_memories`/`forget_memory` off its own judgment through a real chat conversation
  (the prompt-assembly logic and the tool-dispatch/DB layers were each verified
  directly and separately, but not end-to-end through a real model deciding when to
  save something); the default `AGENT_LLM_PROVIDER=claude` path (this environment
  overrides to `openai`, same limitation nearly every other stage's live pass in this
  file has already noted). No consolidation/summarization job for when the active count
  approaches its cap -- a disclosed follow-up, not implemented this pass.

## Project ingestion readiness

Projects are created as lightweight JSON shells. Documents upload independently through
the direct multipart API, and chat is available immediately; there is no fixed
takeoff/spec/drawing bundle and no chat-wide readiness gate.

- **Readiness is derived, never stored.** `app/core/project/readiness.py` projects the
  current active document set into `missing | processing | ready | failed`. It reports
  counts for active, searchable, processing, and failed revisions. A failed optional
  source does not invalidate already published searchable evidence.
- **Setup waits for the active upload set.** Project setup orientation snapshots the
  project only after current active uploads finish processing. It defers while any are
  uploading/processing and reports a failure only when active sources produce no
  searchable evidence. Copy and status are about active uploads, not required roles.
- **Capabilities remain granular.** `/api/projects/{id}/ingestion` reports chat,
  document-search, visual-search, takeoff, and estimating capability separately, so
  clients do not infer one monolithic project-ready flag.

### Queue control-plane isolation and cancellation

Queue claims, 20-second lease heartbeats, and graceful lease releases use
`control_async_session`, a six-connection/zero-overflow pool separate from both normal
API/job-handler traffic and agent tool calls. Do not move those operations back to
`async_session`: the reserved pool is what keeps the 90-second leases renewable when
business traffic saturates its own pools. `/ready` validates both the main and control
pools plus every enabled coordinator's recent poll.

Heartbeat transactions are bounded to five seconds and track the lease deadline using
monotonic process time. Explicit renewal rejection or local expiry fences and cancels the
attempt without releasing/decrementing it; the coordinator waits at most two seconds for
cleanup before freeing its slot. A cancellation-resistant task is supervised outside the
slot and terminates the service after ten more seconds so Render replaces the process.
Deploy shutdown is intentionally different: it drains, then releases still-owned leases
without consuming an attempt. Preserve that lease-loss-versus-shutdown distinction.

## Thin mandatory project setup

Project setup starts only when the user explicitly chooses the `identify_scope` chat
skill after the current source snapshot is fully enriched. Document ingestion never
queues orientation on its own. Setup is a scope-decision ledger, not a project-fact
cache: specifications, products, conditions, and material requirements remain in
document retrieval.

- **Four MCP tools, all in `app/mcp/tools/project_setup.py`** (previously described here
  only as the abstract `project_setup.orient`/`project_setup.resolve` job/turn names —
  the tools themselves are named and allow-listed per skill):
  - `get_project_setup(project_id)` — read-only ledger dump (systems, status, questions).
  - `get_project_setup_input(project_id, setup_id)` — the orientation turn's input:
    source snapshot, deterministic type/CSI candidates, takeoff scope, prior decisions.
  - `save_project_setup_orientation(project_id, setup_id, scope_brief, systems,
    questions=None)` — the orient turn's terminal write. Caps at 12 questions, rejects
    vague defaults (`"unknown"`/`"unresolved"`), requires `scope_brief.evidence`. If no
    questions are pending, calls through to mark the setup `ready` directly; if
    questions exist, status becomes `awaiting_answers` and `project_setup.resolve` gets
    queued for later. This is the `project_setup_orient` skill's one terminal tool.
  - `finalize_project_setup(project_id, setup_id, systems)` — the resolve turn's
    terminal write. Requires status `awaiting_answers`, zero pending questions, and an
    identical system-tag roster to the provisional one; sets status `ready`. This is the
    `project_setup_resolve` skill's one terminal tool.
  Also relevant: `ask_user` (§ "Live MCP tool registry" above) is what the orient/resolve
  turns actually use to raise and durably wait on setup questions.
- **One normal orientation turn.** `project_setup_orient` reads `get_project_setup_input`'s
  compact bundle of deterministic type/CSI entities, grouped takeoff scope, the source
  snapshot, and prior decisions. It makes exactly one batched `retrieve_project_evidence`
  call, may expand a selected node, and may inspect at most one genuinely scope-changing
  visual page. Its eight-tool-turn limit (`SKILL_MAX_TOOL_TURNS["project_setup_orient"]`)
  prevents the old per-system deep-extraction behavior; its allow-list is
  `{get_project_setup_input, retrieve_project_evidence, get_document_context,
  inspect_document_page, save_project_setup_orientation}`.
- **Persisted output.** `ProjectBuildingSystem` rows are versioned by `setup_id` and store
  only the system identity, scope disposition/source, confidence, rationale, and current
  document-node evidence. `ProjectScopeBrief` is written by the same terminal orientation
  save and remains derived navigation context rather than citable Estimate evidence.
  There are no setup fact, specified-material, or extraction-unit tables.
- **Questions are part of setup.** Consequential contract/scope judgments use the existing
  `project_setup_question` verification kind, raised via `ask_user`. Every question gates
  readiness indefinitely until the user answers or explicitly chooses its resolving
  default; setup never applies a default because time elapsed. The final answer queues
  one `project_setup_resolve` turn, allow-listed to just `{get_project_setup,
  finalize_project_setup}` (three-turn budget) — it only reads the provisional ledger
  and finalizes resolved dispositions, nothing more.
- **Lifecycle.** The normal states are `pending -> orienting -> ready`, or
  `pending -> orienting -> awaiting_answers -> ready`; `stale` is terminal for that
  snapshot (only ever produced by a genuinely new snapshot hash, which always creates a
  fresh row instead of reviving the old one). **Correction — `failed` is not always
  terminal**: `get_or_create_setup` (`app/core/project_setup/service.py`) revives a
  `failed` (or `stale`) row back to `pending` when the *incoming* `source_snapshot_hash`
  matches the existing row's exactly — i.e. a same-snapshot retry after a failure reuses
  the row rather than treating `failed` as a dead end, which the older wording here
  didn't capture. Publishing a new source revision stales prior setup and supersedes its
  pending questions, but does not schedule the new snapshot; the user must explicitly run
  Identify Scope again. Old resolved answers remain non-authoritative orientation context.
- **Downstream boundary.** `get_project_setup` supplies scope decisions and user answers.
  When a current ready setup exists, categorization treats those decisions as
  authoritative. Missing, stale, incomplete, or failed setup never blocks
  `categorize_estimate`; it derives the system roster from searchable project evidence
  and the QTO instead. Categorization must still call `retrieve_project_evidence` for
  document facts. The same setup states never block `price_estimate` either; pricing is
  gated on deterministic completion of the current Estimate categorization, not on
  whether Identify Scope ran. `get_project_scope_brief` is read-only; there is no lazy
  `determine_scope` sub-turn.

## Stage 2-4: Estimate Item identification, structure, and labor basis

The next three stages of the 7-stage rehaul (identify the sellable Estimate Items a
sub delivers → structure their materials/labor/equipment/subcontract scope → determine
each labor activity's structural basis), all run inside one `categorize_estimate` skill
turn. This is the first stage that actually writes `estimates_v2`
content — and the first real v1→v2 tool replacement in the strangler-fig migration
agreed for this rehaul (see the earlier discussion in this file's history): `categorize_
estimate` now targets `estimates_v2` exclusively, not `app/core/estimates` (v1). The
deliverable is a fully organized, unpriced Estimate: real items, real components, real
labor bases, real CSI codes, real (optional) locations — with **no price, unit rate, or
productivity rate anywhere**, since no agent-facing type in this pass has a field for
one. Pricing (Stage 6) is a deliberately accepted gap until it lands on v2.

- **Identification is document-led, takeoff-validated, not takeoff-first.** An Estimate
  Item's identity comes from the design documents (a wall type, a door schedule entry, a
  ceiling system) — a takeoff row only confirms a system is present and how much of it
  there is; a bare takeoff row is never turned into an item on its own. The directive's
  order: read the ready setup/scope brief when available, otherwise derive the trade and
  system roster directly from searchable project evidence → *locate* (not read) the
  sections/sheets naming the building-system types this trade's documents actually cover → confirm
  against the takeoff → **research now, as the normal path**: one batched
  `research_project_documents` call reads the schedules/legends located in the step
  before and returns cited facts, skippable only by stating why in the narration before
  the first patch → adopt a location grain once → cut items by a fixed granularity test
  (different system type always separate; same type + different spec always separate;
  same type + spec separate only at the adopted location grain) → build components/labor
  basis → save incrementally → finalize once. A direct `read_project_document_section`/
  `inspect_drawing_sheet` call is an escalation from here on — a `stop_reason` showing a
  retrieval gap, a returned conflict, a flattened verbatim cell, or a gap a rejected
  patch exposes — not the way schedules get read by default. This was a live, fixed
  bug: an earlier ordering (locate-and-read together, before deciding whether research
  was warranted) let the agent read every schedule directly and then use the research
  step's own stated-skip permission to justify never calling it — confirmed on a real
  "categorize_estimate" run against project "Jack test 2" (~162s across 12 serial
  document tool calls, zero `drawing_sheet_observations` cached, since only the research
  pipeline writes that cache). See "Document research pipeline hardening" below.
- **Two coverage ledgers, not one.** Takeoff→items (every row allocated, excluded, or
  unresolved — this is the one `finalize_estimate_draft` hard-blocks on) and docs→items
  (every in-scope system type has an item, is out of scope, is by another trade, or is a
  real gap — this is the agent's own running account, submitted as `system_type_
  dispositions` at finalize, logged for audit only exactly like v1's `research_
  dispositions` always were — never stored as Estimate content, and not itself validated
  against anything, since there is no canonical system-type list to check it against).

### New v2 authoring/reading layer (`app/core/estimates/`)

`estimates/service.py` speaks only in raw UUIDs and a strict copy-on-write changeset
contract (`ReplaceSourceLinksChange`/`ReplaceSourceExclusionsChange` each replace the
**whole** version's list, never one entry) — nothing about that shape is safe for an LLM
to author directly, and until now nothing outside its own tests called into it at all
(confirmed via `grep` before this pass: zero external callers). Three new modules bridge
that gap, none of which existed before this pass:

- **`authoring.py`** — the only place agent-facing input (`ItemIn`/`ComponentIn`/
  `ComponentBasisIn`/`AllocationIn`/`ExclusionIn`/`RemoveItemIn`, all plain strings/
  numbers, no raw UUIDs) gets resolved into `VersionGraphDraft`'s UUID-keyed types.
  `build_changeset` is the main entry point: resolves a CSI code into its full
  MasterFormat ancestor chain (`09` → `09 22` → `09 22 16`, via `encounter_csi_node`, so
  sections roll up correctly — only the leaf gets the title, ancestors backfill on later
  reuse), resolves a unit string via `app.core.price_book.units.resolve_unit` (falling
  back to `raw_unit_text` when unrecognized, never rejecting), get-or-creates
  `ProjectLocationLevel`/`ProjectLocation` rows from an ordered location path (v2's own
  `create_location_level`/`create_location` are plain creates, not get-or-create —
  `authoring.py` adds the lookup-by-natural-key layer they lack), resolves an `R<n>` QTO
  alias against the project's takeoff rows in the same canonical `(created_at, id)`
  order used everywhere else in this codebase, and builds/merges the version's full
  `source_links`/`source_exclusions` lists (since those are version-wide, replace-only
  changes — a patch that touches one item's allocations must recompute the *entire*
  links list: keep every link untouched by this patch, drop the touched item/
  components' old links, append their new ones).
  - **There is no agent-chosen item/component key.** An item's identity is `uuid5` of
    `(project_id, slugify(name + location path))`; a component's is `uuid5` of
    `(item_uuid, slugify(name))`. The same name (at the same location) always resolves
    to the same identity on a later patch, including across a resumed conversation with
    no memory of prior tool calls — no separate key needs to be persisted or remembered
    anywhere. The deliberate tradeoff: renaming an item or component changes its
    identity too. This is not benign — confirmed live, a bare rename-via-upsert leaves
    the old identity's stale source links in place *and* double-claims its takeoff rows
    against the new one, since the old links never get cleaned up. The fix:
    `patch_estimate_draft`'s `remove_items` param (→ `RemoveItemChange`, which v2
    already cascades source-link removal for) — a rename is `remove_items=[old]` +
    `items=[corrected]` in the *same* call. The directive tells the agent this
    explicitly; there is no automatic detection of "this looks like a rename."
  - **Allocation conservation is hand-built here — v2 has none.** (v1 only has this
    check on its material-mapping path; v2's `SourceLinkDraft.attributed_quantity` is
    written verbatim, never summed or compared against `TakeoffLineItem.quantity`
    anywhere in `estimates/`.) `_check_allocation_conservation` sums every link's
    `attributed_quantity` per takeoff row across the **whole** draft (not just the
    current patch) and rejects a total exceeding the row's own quantity; a row claimed
    both by an unquantified ("full row") link and a quantified (partial) link in the
    same patch is rejected outright as ambiguous, before any DB query.
  - **Evidence validation** reimplements v1's exact whitelist (`{project_documents,
    project_document_chunks, drawing_sheet_observations}` + project-scoped existence
    check) natively, since v1's `_validate_evidence_refs` is private and raises v1's own
    exception type.
  - **Non-material waste normalization must be replicated here, not just left to v2's
    persist layer.** `service.py`'s `_apply_project_waste` forces every non-material
    component's `waste_application` to `"not_applied"` at persist time regardless of
    what was submitted (waste is material-only). Copy-on-write revision reuse
    (`_item_payload_equal`/`_component_payload_equal`) compares the **freshly submitted**
    draft against the **persisted, already-normalized** one — so without mirroring that
    exact normalization in `authoring.py`'s own `ComponentDraft` construction, re-saving
    a semantically-unchanged labor/equipment/subcontract/other component always forced a
    new revision. Confirmed live before the fix (every re-patch created a new item
    revision) and after (identical re-patch correctly reuses the existing revision) —
    this is the same caveat flagged during design review that "a caller that hand-builds
    an item draft ... will miss the item-level fast path even when semantically
    unchanged," now closed for the one caller that exists.
- **`readers.py`** — v2's only native read is `get_version_graph`; there is no
  `get_estimate`/`list_estimates`/`get_estimate_by_project` at all. `get_or_create_draft_
  estimate` is the lazy turn-start bootstrap (`get_or_create_draft_estimate` → `configure_
  project_for_v2` if needed → `create_estimate` with an empty graph on first use) —
  unlike v1's proposal workflow, nothing needs to run *before* the categorize_estimate
  turn starts; the tools bootstrap themselves on first write. `configure_project_for_v2`
  raises `ConflictError` for a project with any existing v1 `ProjectEstimate` row
  (**even archived** — its own docstring: "without migrating V1 estimate data"), which
  `get_or_create_draft_estimate` re-raises with a clearer message — a project categorized
  once under the old v1-writing skill cannot use the new one; there is no migration
  path, matching the agreed hard-cutover decision. `get_estimate_draft_state` resolves
  the graph's UUIDs back to human-readable form (CSI code/title, unit text, a
  reconstructed location path by walking `parent_id` pointers) and builds the
  takeoff-side coverage ledger by walking `source_links`/`source_exclusions` against
  each row's own quantity — **works even before any draft exists** (returns an empty
  state with the QTO catalog/`R<n>` aliases already populated, since that only needs the
  project's takeoff, not an Estimate) so the agent's very first read already shows it
  what `R1`, `R2`, ... refer to.
- The Estimate chat artifact is the canonical V2 workbook returned by
  `app.core.estimates.workbook.get_workbook`. `finalize_estimate_draft` returns an
  `AgentArtifactRef` to that workbook rather than constructing a second, legacy-shaped
  Estimate payload.

### Neon read-latency projection

Estimate versions are immutable, but the normalized V2 graph spans many revision and
manifest tables. Reconstructing that graph for every overview/artifact request used to
cost roughly 18 sequential SQL statements; the workbook then reconstructed it again
during approval validation. `estimate_version_read_models` is an additive, deterministic
JSONB projection keyed one-to-one by `estimate_versions.id`. The normalized tables remain
authoritative. Every version-creation path writes the projected normalized graph and its
approval validation in the same transaction; clone/restore copy the source projection.
`get_version_graph` reads the projection in one statement and only reconstructs from the
normalized tables for a pre-migration cache miss. That miss is persisted through a short
independent transaction because ordinary FastAPI read sessions do not commit.

After deploying migration `4d8f2a7c9e11`, run
`uv run python -m scripts.backfill_estimate_version_read_models` before routing user
traffic so pre-existing versions also receive the one-query path. The script is
idempotent and never changes authoritative estimate rows. A projection schema version
guards future shape changes.

The FastAPI lifespan also warms both SQLAlchemy pools before startup completes.
`pool_pre_ping` is intentionally disabled: against Neon it added one full round trip to
every checkout. `pool_recycle` replaces old sockets before checkout, and the frontend's
normal idempotent-query retry handles the rare optimistic-disconnect request. The `/`
skill catalog remains at its project-scoped URL for compatibility but is global metadata
and deliberately executes no SQL.

### New MCP tools (`app/mcp/tools/estimates.py`)

Three tools, deliberately different names from v1's so both stay registered without
collision (v1's tools remain available to external MCP clients and as a safety net —
only `categorize_estimate`'s allow-list stops pointing at them):

- `get_estimate_draft(project_id)` — the resume/orientation read: items, components,
  the takeoff-side coverage ledger (see above), validation issues.
- `patch_estimate_draft(project_id, items, exclusions, remove_items)` — the one write
  tool. Upserts named items (full component list each, matching v2's own aggregate
  semantics — `UpsertItemChange` carries an item's whole `components` list, there is no
  edit-one-component primitive), sets takeoff-row dispositions, and removes items by
  their old identity (the rename fix above). Surfaces v2's `ConflictError` (a stale
  `base_version_id` — read fresh, someone else patched concurrently) as a clear
  retry-with-refresh message rather than a raw exception.
- `finalize_estimate_draft(project_id, system_type_dispositions)` — terminal call.
  Blocks only on a takeoff row with **no** disposition at all (an `unresolved` row is
  allowed through and remains explicit in the draft's QTO coverage ledger). Returns an
  `AgentArtifactRef` to the canonical V2 workbook.

**Deliberately not built this pass**: a v2-native verification-queue kind for unresolved
rows. v1's `estimate_structure_issue` resolver is hard-coupled to v1's tables
(`app/core/verification/kinds.py`'s `_resolve_estimate_structure_issue` imports `app.
core.estimates.service` and writes `EstimateItem.user_confirmed`/`EstimateSourceExclusion`
directly) and cannot be reused for v2 subjects without a real rewrite. Unresolved rows
remain explicit in the draft's QTO coverage ledger, but a real
`estimate_v2_structure_issue` kind with its own resolver is still a disclosed follow-up,
not silently dropped.

### The retired "optimized" execution mode

`categorize_estimate` no longer runs the old v1-specific QTO-alias-substitution/preload
path (`app.core.agent.estimate_references` and friends) — the v2 tools take plain
`R<n>` strings directly and self-bootstrap the estimate lazily, so there's nothing to
preload. That machinery is left physically in place but unreachable, and ripping it out
is a disclosed, separate follow-up (see Open Follow-ups). Two of its user-facing
behaviors had to be restored directly in `complete_message_run`, just not through the
retired machinery: closing prose after `finalize_estimate_draft` succeeds, and
attaching the Estimate artifact (since `display_artifact` isn't in this skill's
allow-list). Full design writeup:
`docs/history/v1-tool-surface-and-retired-designs.md`.

### Verified

23 unit tests (`tests/test_estimates_v2_authoring.py`); a live throwaway-Neon-DB pass of
`get_estimate_draft`/`patch_estimate_draft`/`finalize_estimate_draft` end to end
(CSI parent chains, copy-on-write revision reuse, the `remove_items` rename fix); and a
live LLM run of the rewritten skill confirming the actual design goal — two takeoff rows
correctly merged into one document-derived item with 3 different labor bases, zero
pricing anywhere. One real, would-have-shipped bug found and fixed live: pydantic's
`Decimal` JSON schema was rejected outright by OpenAI's structured-output validator.
Full pass-by-pass log, including what's still unverified:
`docs/history/verification-log.md`.

### `categorize_estimate` latency pass — partial success, resolution caching, unit fixes

A real `categorize_estimate` run (project "Jack Test 3") took **17.9 minutes**
(`agent_runs.id=b3426729-...`). Output quality was excellent (13 document-derived
items, 39/39 takeoff rows allocated, 0 validation issues) — this was purely a
latency/cost problem, diagnosed from `agent_runs`/`tool_calls`/`llm_calls` directly:
**29% of the run (509s) was `patch_estimate_draft` retrying after rejection** — three
times, all traceable to `unrecognized result unit 'pieces'`/`'piece'`, then a
follow-on pydantic basis error once the model dropped `result_unit` entirely after two
failures. 50% of the run's output tokens went into payloads that were entirely thrown
away, since the tool was all-or-nothing. Separately, 16% (175s) was a nested
`determine_scope` turn computed synchronously on the critical path, and a further 7%
(80s) was `categorize_estimate` re-running the *same* document-orientation reads the
nested turn had just done. Fixed on four fronts, all in `app/core/estimates/`
(**not** `app/core/estimates_v2/` — see the note on that stale path below):

- **Unit resolution.** `resolve_unit` (`app/core/price_book/units.py`) tried only an
  exact code/alias match; no plural of any unit resolved (`sheet` worked, `sheets`
  didn't). Added a trailing-plural-strip fallback (`_singular_candidates`, tried only
  on a miss) plus a migration (`alembic/versions/965a28c7bc68_add_piece_unit_
  aliases.py`) seeding `PIECE`/`PIECES`/`PC`/`PCS` → `EA` — this project's takeoff
  used `pieces` as its unit on 20 of 39 rows, which had no mapping at all despite `EA`
  already existing. Applied to the shared DB (purely additive).
- **Validation errors now enumerate the valid set.** The model sees the full,
  untruncated `str()` of a raised `ValidationError` (confirmed by tracing
  `run_logged_tool`'s error path → FastMCP's `ToolError` → the provider — no
  truncation anywhere in that chain), so a message that only names the *bad* value
  and never the *valid* ones wastes a full round trip on a guess.
  `_resolve_controlled_unit_id`, `_resolve_qto_ref`, and the `remove_items` miss in
  `authoring.py` all now name what would have worked.
- **`patch_estimate_draft` supports per-item partial success.** A bad unit/basis/
  evidence citation on one item no longer discards the whole batch. `build_changeset`
  (`app/core/estimates/authoring.py`) resolves each item independently via
  `_resolve_item`/`_resolve_component` into a self-contained `_ItemBundle` — nothing
  touches the changeset's shared accumulators until a bundle is known to have
  succeeded, so a caught failure can never leave an item half-applied. Evidence
  existence-checking (`_evidence_existence_errors`) and allocation conservation
  (`check_allocation_conservation`, now a pure function taking a pre-fetched
  `rows_by_id`) both return per-id/per-row failures instead of raising, so
  `build_changeset` can attribute a failure back to the specific contributing item(s)
  — including an attribution *loop* for allocation conservation, since dropping one
  item restores links that can push a different row over. Returns
  `ChangesetBuild(changeset, base_graph, rejections: list[PatchRejection])`; raises
  only when *nothing* survived, with every rejection reason inlined in one message.
  Two no-op guards matter here and were both live-verified: if every item is rejected
  and nothing is removed, `full_links` would equal the unchanged base graph, and
  emitting a no-op `ReplaceSourceLinksChange` anyway would make `commit_changeset`
  raise `"changeset does not change the estimate"` — losing the whole rejection list
  in the resulting rollback; same for exclusions re-stating an identical existing
  disposition. `patch_estimate_draft` (`app/mcp/tools/estimates.py`) now returns
  `PatchEstimateDraftOut` (`EstimateDraftStateOut` plus `rejected_items`) instead of
  the bare read type, and logs rejections via `run_logged_tool`'s `result_payload`
  hook for audit. `EstimateDraftStateOut` itself is unchanged — a permanently-empty
  `rejected_items` field there would be schema noise on `get_estimate_draft`'s hot
  read path.
- **`ResolutionCache`** (`app/core/estimates/authoring.py`) memoizes CSI-node, unit,
  and location resolution for the lifetime of one `build_changeset` call —
  request-scoped by construction (never module-level/`ContextVar`/`lru_cache`, since
  these are per-project rows). Unit misses are cached too, not just hits, since a miss
  costs two queries and misses are exactly the strings an agent repeats across many
  components. `encounter_csi_node` (`app/core/estimates/service.py`) gained an
  optional `system_id` param (default `None` — every pre-existing call site
  unaffected) so the `ClassificationSystem` row, previously re-looked-up on *every*
  call, is fetched once per patch via the new `get_csi_system_id`.
  `commit_changeset` gained `base_graph: VersionGraphDraft | None = None`, skipping
  its own otherwise-duplicate `get_version_graph` fetch of the exact graph
  `build_changeset` already has; `apply_pricing_batch` (the other `commit_changeset`
  caller) got the same fix. Measured live: a 6-item patch with items sharing CSI
  code/unit/location dropped from 78 to 33 queries; a full patch→commit→read cycle
  confirmed correct end to end. The deeper win (`_persist_graph`'s own copy-on-write
  revision-creation loop, roughly another 25-30s/patch) was deliberately left
  untouched this pass — it has already broken copy-on-write reuse twice before (the
  Stage 2-4 waste-normalization bug and the Stage 5 pricing round-trip bug), so it's
  flagged for a separate, more careful pass rather than bundled in here.
- **`categorize_estimate`'s directive no longer re-orients.** Step 2 previously
  re-ran `get_project_document_summaries`/`search_document_understanding`/
  `list_document_understanding_cards`/`search_project_documents` from scratch to
  build its system-type roster, even though the nested `determine_scope` turn (step 1)
  had just read the same documents to write the scope brief. Verified live that the
  brief's own `evidence` already names the exact spec sections *and* schedule/legend
  sheets (a real brief's evidence cited `A7.10`/`A1.20`/`A8.12` sheets alongside CSI
  section numbers) with real `project_document_chunks` ids attached — directly usable
  without re-locating anything. Step 2 (`app/core/agent/skills.py`) now sources the
  roster from the brief first, and only issues a fresh locate call for a system type
  the brief's evidence doesn't already cover. `determine_scope`'s own step 5 was
  strengthened to require citing a schedule/legend's own section/sheet specifically
  (not just the governing division-level spec section) precisely so this handoff
  stays reliable. Confirmed against `tests/test_categorize_prompt.py`'s literal-phrase
  and step-ordering assertions and the 20,000-char prompt budget (assembled prompt:
  17,947 chars).
- **The scope brief is lazy.** The first active agent turn that needs one computes and
  caches it through `get_project_scope_brief`; there is no background warming job.
- **A pre-existing, unrelated staleness surfaced while writing this note**: this
  section (and "Stage 5" below) still reference `app/core/estimates_v2/` throughout —
  that package was renamed to `app/core/estimates/` in a prior commit
  (`ba25702`, "Delete the legacy v1 Estimate domain, collapse estimates_v2 into
  estimates"; internal `_v2`-suffixed identifiers and the `/estimate-v2` URL prefix
  were deliberately kept). Every path this pass touched or added uses the current,
  correct `app/core/estimates/` — the surrounding older prose was left as-is, since
  a full path-reference sweep across these two sections is a separate, larger cleanup
  from this pass's own scope.
- **New unit test coverage** (`tests/test_estimates_v2_authoring.py`,
  `tests/test_units.py`): `check_allocation_conservation`'s full branch set
  (over-allocation, the wholly/partially-claimed ambiguity, the tolerance boundary,
  a wrong-project row, multiple independently-violated rows) now runs with no DB
  session at all, previously only reachable via a live pass; `_evidence_shape_errors`,
  `_error_text` (both our `ValidationError` and pydantic's), `_format_location`,
  `_aggregate_rejection_message`; and `_resolve_item` against a fully pre-warmed
  `ResolutionCache` and a session that raises if touched at all, reproducing the exact
  production failure (an `installed_item` basis with a conversion factor but no
  result unit) attributed to the right component, with zero DB calls.
- **Verified live, this pass, against the shared DB** (all rolled back or read-only —
  see the migration note above for the one real write, the alias seed): 3 good items
  + 1 bad → 3 saved, 1 rejected naming the failing component; all items bad → single
  raise with every reason inlined, nothing written; two items whose allocations
  jointly over-claim one takeoff row → both rejected, an innocent third item survives,
  the resulting changeset still commits; a `remove_items` entry for a nonexistent item
  plus an `exclusions` entry with an unknown `R<n>` alias, alongside one good item →
  both rejected independently, the good item survives; an exclusions-only patch
  re-stating an identical existing disposition → the new, correctly-distinct "made no
  changes" message (not the misleading "everything was rejected" one — this exact
  case was caught and fixed live during this verification pass); the `ResolutionCache`
  query-count reduction (78 → 33) and a full patch→commit→read round trip.
- **Not verified this pass**: `_persist_graph`'s own N+1 (deliberately deferred, see
  above) or the categorize directive rewrite's effect on a live LLM turn's actual
  behavior (structural/prompt-budget constraints were confirmed, not a live run
  choosing what to locate on its own).

## Stage 5: interactive pricing

The fifth stage of the 7-stage rehaul: filling in `selected_pricing` on the unpriced
`estimates_v2` structure Stage 2-4 produces. The product rule, from the user: **the LLM
must never invent a price, wage, or productivity number.** It looks them up in a real
price book by reference; when the price book has no answer it asks the user in chat,
the user answers in a later message, and pricing resumes automatically — no background
batch, no separate "resume" affordance, just the next chat turn.

- **Enforcement is structural, not prompted.** `set_component_price`
  (`app/mcp/tools/pricing.py`) has **no parameter for a price, wage, hours-per-unit,
  or flat amount anywhere in its signature** — only reference ids
  (`material_price_id`, or `labor_productivity_id` + `labor_rate_id`), read by the
  backend off the referenced price-book row itself. `PricingSource="agent_estimate"` is
  structurally unreachable from this skill, the same guarantee `categorize_estimate`'s
  tool allow-list already established for structure (see "Estimate categorization
  skill"). The one number the agent may reason itself is a condition **multiplier**
  (`rate_multiplier`, `gt=0, le=3`) — because the price book cannot know site
  conditions — and even that is stored **separately**, applied on top of the referenced
  rate, never folded into it, and requires a basis + at least one document evidence
  citation whenever it isn't 1.0.
- **The multiplier declares which side of the labor calculation it hits.**
  `multiplier_applies_to: "hours" | "rate"` — `"hours"` for a productivity hit
  (restricted access, occupied-space phasing: the crew is slower) scales
  `calculated_labor_hours`, wage stays at book value; `"rate"` for a genuine wage
  premium (a night-shift differential) scales the extended cost, hours stay at book
  value. Neither `labor_hours_per_basis_unit` nor `labor_rate_per_hour` stored on the
  component is ever itself multiplied — both always reflect exactly what the price book
  said (`app/core/estimates/calculations.py::calculate_selected_pricing`). For
  `unit_rate` (material) pricing, a multiplier only ever scales `extended_cost`;
  `direct_unit_rate` stays exactly what was quoted.
- **The 4-branch material unit derivation** described in `MaterialPrice`'s own
  docstring (`app/models/price_book.py`) had never been implemented anywhere before
  this pass — `app/core/price_book/pricing_math.py::derive_material_price` is it:
  quoted directly in the estimating unit (unchanged) → quoted in the purchase unit
  (divide by `purchase_to_estimating_factor`) → quoted in a unit universally convertible
  to the estimating unit (divide by the conversion factor — price converts *inversely*
  to quantity, e.g. `$18/SY` with a SY→SF factor of 9 derives to `$2/SF`, not `$18 × 9`)
  → `unresolvable` (price `None`, surfaced for the agent to skip or ask about, never
  guessed past). A second hop repeats the same rule from the material's estimating unit
  into the pricing component's own unit, so callers never need to special-case "the
  component's unit already matches." `universal_factor` is a **non-raising** probe
  (mirrors `calculations.py::convert_quantity`'s compatibility checks, deliberately
  duplicated rather than shared — see the module's own docstring for why reusing
  `convert_quantity` directly would be wrong even setting aside the layering-cycle
  concern: "unpriceable" is a normal *result* here, not an exception).
- **Price-book search returns references, never numbers to retype.**
  `app/core/price_book/search.py`'s `search_materials`/`search_labor_activities`/
  `search_labor_classifications` (new this pass; the REST routes in
  `app/api/price_book.py` were refactored to share the same `material_match_clause`/
  `labor_match_clause` hybrid ILIKE+`pg_trgm` matching, per this repo's no-duplication
  rule) rank `is_preferred DESC, priced_at DESC` and **never** filter to preferred-only
  or sort by price — `MaterialPrice.is_preferred`'s own docstring is quoted verbatim in
  the module. Labor is two independent result sets (`activities` for productivity,
  `classifications` for wage) on purpose: labor pricing always needs two independently
  looked-up numbers, and an activity's `default_classification` is a suggestion only,
  never a binding.
  - **`csi_code`, on both material and labor activity search, is a ranking boost, not a
    hard filter** — fixed during this pass's own live verification (see "Verified live"
    below) after the very first live agent run passed a plausible-but-not-exact CSI
    code and got zero results for a material that had, in fact, just been priced
    correctly moments earlier by direct search. A material's own book classification
    routinely sits at a different CSI section than the Estimate component citing it
    (gypsum board priced under `09 29 00` while the owning item is coded `09 22 16`
    framing), and hard-filtering on the caller's CSI guess silently hid a real match.
    Both functions now order CSI-matching rows first via a `CASE` expression instead of
    excluding non-matching rows.
- **Direct price-book curation writes share one audited door.**
  `app/core/price_book/curation.py::record_user_answer` — the only function
  `record_user_supplied_price` (MCP tool) calls — resolves which Material/LaborActivity/
  LaborClassification the figure attaches to (get-or-create by name, same identity
  resolution the bulk-import path uses) then delegates to the existing
  `add_material_price`/`add_rate`/`add_productivity`, which already hardcode
  `source="manual"`. The user's own words are stored as the price/rate row's
  `notes`/`conditions` for audit. **Convergent resume, no correlation table**: since the
  explicitly curated answer becomes a real, `source="manual"` price-book row, the very next search for the
  same material/activity/classification naturally succeeds — no question↔component
  binding is needed for correctness, only for optional UX visibility (deliberately not
  built, see below). This path is only for a standalone request to curate the company
  price book. Estimate answers instead use immutable project-local inputs and require a
  separate explicit promotion before they become available to future projects.
- **`get_estimate_pricing_state` is the orientation-and-resume read**, always called
  first, every turn, including a resumed one — the model must never rely on what it
  priced or asked about from memory. Status is three-way: `priced` / `needs_price` /
  `blocked`, where `blocked` covers a component whose basis can never resolve into a
  quantity (an unresolved unit, an unknown quantity) — a categorization problem, not a
  pricing one, and without this bucket the agent would loop forever retrying a
  component no price-book lookup could ever fix. **Two resolution paths, deliberately
  not unified**: a labor component's driving quantity comes from the pre-existing
  private `service._basis_quantity` (read-only here); a non-labor component resolves
  its own `quantity`/`unit` directly (waste-adjusted for material) rather than reusing
  `_basis_quantity`, because that function only resolves a component's own quantity/
  unit when `selected_pricing.pricing_method == "unit_rate"` is *already set* — it was
  written for persist-time recomputation, not a pre-price probe, and calling it
  uniformly on an unpriced material wrongly reported every one of them as `blocked` (a
  real bug caught live before this split was added — see "Verified live" below).
- **`set_component_price` writes through the pre-existing, previously-uncalled
  `apply_pricing_batch`** (`app/core/estimates/service.py`, zero production callers
  before this pass) rather than adding a new `EstimateChange` type — it deep-copies the
  *persisted* graph and mutates only `selected_pricing` on named components, matching
  the copy-on-write discipline the rest of `estimates_v2` already follows. A
  `ConflictError` (stale `base_version_id`) is re-raised with a clear "read fresh state
  and retry" message; a `ValidationError` whose message says "does not change the
  estimate" (an identical re-price) is caught and reported as a benign no-op rather than
  propagated as an error.
- **The critical round-trip fix**: every new pricing field
  (`rate_multiplier`/`multiplier_applies_to`/`multiplier_basis`/`material_price_id`/
  `labor_rate_id`/`labor_productivity_id`) had to be added to *both*
  `SelectedPricingDraft`/`CalculatedPricing` (`types.py`) *and* `get_version_graph`'s
  reconstruction of a `SelectedPricingDraft` from a persisted
  `EstimateComponentRevisionPricing` row (`service.py`) — omitting it from the second
  place means a freshly-read draft never matches what's actually persisted, so
  copy-on-write's payload-equality check (`_component_payload_equal`) always sees a
  "change" and forces a new revision on every identical re-price. This is the exact
  same failure mode the Stage 2-4 waste-normalization bug was; caught the same way here
  (confirmed live, both the pre-fix failure and the post-fix correct no-op reuse).
- **Migration adds columns only** (`alembic/versions/b5c6d7e8f9a0_add_pricing_provenance_and_multiplier.py`):
  `rate_multiplier` (NOT NULL DEFAULT 1), `multiplier_applies_to`, `multiplier_basis`,
  and three nullable `ON DELETE RESTRICT` FKs into `material_prices`/`labor_rates`/
  `labor_productivity`, plus two new CHECK constraints on
  `estimate_component_revision_pricing`. Deliberately does **not** touch the existing
  `..._pricing_inputs` CHECK constraint — tightening it to require a reference id would
  wrongly reject legitimate reference-less `user_input`/`rfq_quote` rows written by
  other paths. Confirmed via `pg_constraint` before writing the migration that this
  table's *actual* live constraint names disagree with what `app/models/estimate.py`
  declares (a pre-existing, systemic mismatch across the v2 table set, documented in
  that model's own docstring) — never `op.drop_constraint` using the model's own
  constraint-name strings without checking `pg_constraint` first.
- **`validation.py` gained two advisory (non-blocking) checks**:
  `pricing_multiplier_undocumented` (multiplier ≠ 1 with no evidence) and
  `pricing_reference_missing` (`source="historical_match"` pricing with no price-book
  reference id at all). Both are advisory rather than blocking on purpose:
  `validate_graph` runs on every commit over the **whole** folded graph, not just the
  components a changeset touched, so a blocking check here would trip on any
  pre-existing historical_match row without a reference (v1-era or RFQ-sourced pricing
  has none) the next time *anything* on the estimate is committed. The real, structural
  guarantee is `set_component_price` having no numeric parameter — these are a hygiene
  signal layered on top, not a second enforcement mechanism.
- **`convert_quantity` gained a same-unit short-circuit**
  (`calculations.py`, before the `is_convertible` guard, after `is_active`) — without
  it, a component measured in a non-convertible unit (SHEET/ROLL/BAG/LS) could never be
  `unit_rate` priced at all, even with the rate already expressed in that exact unit.

### The `price_estimate` skill (v1 background batch → v2 interactive)

Rewritten in place, same precedent as `categorize_estimate`'s v1→v2 cutover — v1's
`price_estimate_items`/`estimate.price_batch` background-batch tool stays registered
for external MCP clients and v1 projects, untouched.

- **Hard allow-list**, not an exclude-list layered on the full catalog (same posture as
  `categorize_estimate`): the five `pricing_v2` tools
  (`PRICE_ESTIMATE_ALLOWED_TOOLS`, `app/core/agent/skills.py`) plus five read-only
  document tools needed only to ground a multiplier's required evidence citation
  (`get_project_scope_brief`/`search_project_documents`/`read_project_document_section`/
  `get_document_outline`/`inspect_drawing_sheet`). `SKILL_EXCLUDED_TOOLS` no longer has
  a `price_estimate` entry — the dict is empty now that neither `categorize_estimate`
  nor `determine_scope` used it either. Deliberately excluded from the allow-list, each
  for a stated reason in the code comment: every v1/v2 estimate-structure tool (this
  skill prices, it never restructures); `create_rfq`/`send_rfq` (RFQ-as-material-
  fallback is a disclosed later-stage follow-up); and — the one that actually narrows
  CLAUDE.md's general tier-1-historical-first flow —
  `search_historical_material_price`/`search_historical_labor_rate`, which return bare
  numbers with no stable reference id and would let the model retype one, defeating the
  whole reference-only mechanism.
- **No `SKILL_TERMINAL_TOOLS` entry.** An interactive turn that ends by *asking* the
  user a question has no terminal call to force an early stop on, unlike
  `categorize_estimate`'s `finalize_estimate_draft`.
  `SKILL_MAX_TOOL_TURNS["price_estimate"] = 30`, and `"price_estimate"` was added to
  `orchestrator.py::_run_agent_turn`'s `include_server_tools` exclusion tuple — a price
  comes from the price book or the user, never the web.
- **The directive's governing rule is stated first and hardest**: never invent, recall,
  or "reasonably assume" a number; every material price and labor wage/productivity
  must come from a price-book reference or an immutable project-local input. Nine
  numbered steps: read pricing state first every turn including resumed ones; on a
  resumed turn, recognize a bare reply to the assistant's own prior pricing question
  and call `apply_user_supplied_estimate_price`, then separately ask whether the user
  wants to promote it; skip
  `blocked` components and list them; material search (one lookup, choose by identity
  match then `is_preferred` then recency, never cheapest-because-cheapest, skip any
  `unresolvable` derivation); labor search (two independent lookups,
  `default_classification` is a suggestion only, `flat_hours` basis needs only a wage);
  the multiplier (basis + evidence required, roughly 0.85–1.35 absent an extraordinary
  cited condition, never a fudge factor); when the book has no answer, ask one clear
  question naming exactly what's needed and move on to the next component rather than
  stalling the turn, batching every open question into the closing reply; batch
  `set_component_price` calls; never claim a component priced unless the write actually
  succeeded.
- **Pricing readiness is deterministic and independent of Identify Scope.**
  `get_estimate_pricing_state` returns `categorization_readiness` from the current graph:
  an active draft must exist, it must contain at least one Estimate Item, every current
  QTO row must have an allocation/exclusion/unresolved disposition, and
  `validate_categorization_graph` must report no blocking issue. These are the same
  structural conditions enforced by `finalize_estimate_draft`; there is deliberately no
  mutable "categorized" flag that could remain true after a later breaking edit.
  `set_component_price` rechecks this result and rejects the write when it is not ready.
  The price skill still turns the returned deterministic blocker codes/messages into a
  natural-language explanation for the user before stopping.
- **`skill_router.py`'s `SKILL_TRIGGER_GUIDANCE["price_estimate"]` rewrite is the single
  highest-risk item in this pass, and was not deferred.** The old guidance told the
  classifier to leave any message naming a specific material/labor activity/trade
  unmatched (`skill=null`) — written for v1's all-or-nothing batch, where a subset
  request should bypass the skill and call the batch tool directly for just that
  subset. Left as-is, this old rule would have silently swallowed a **resume** reply
  too, since a real answer to "what's your rate for a drywall installer?" naturally
  repeats "drywall installer" — exactly the pattern the old rule told the classifier to
  leave unmatched. The rewrite splits into two explicit, independently-checked cases: a
  NEW request naming one specific material/activity/subset still stays unmatched (so
  ordinary chat can price just that subset directly with the same tools — an
  **unverified** assumption, see "Not verified this pass" below); a RESUME — a reply
  answering or declining a figure the assistant's own immediately preceding message
  asked for — must match regardless of whether it names the same specific thing the
  question did, and the guidance text says so explicitly with a worked example. Both
  `complete_message_run`'s `conversation` (fed to the turn itself) and `detect_skill`'s
  history slice include **both** user and assistant messages, with the assistant side
  being the real persisted final prose (not a placeholder) — confirmed by reading
  `_conversation_from_history_rows`/`_llm_content_for_history_row` before writing the
  guidance, not assumed; this is what makes the resume design possible at all, since the
  classifier and the turn both need to actually see the prior pricing question's text.

### Deliberately not built

- **A `price_book_gap` verification kind.** The convergent-resume design makes a
  correlation table visibility-only, not correctness-critical;
  `tests/test_verification_registry.py`'s set-equality assertion on
  `agent_raisable_kinds()` keeps future additions conscious. If built later: payload
  `{gap_kind, label, unit_code, component_id, question}`, decisions
  `supply_value`/`dismiss`, resolver reading the number from `resolution_data` and
  calling the same `record_user_answer` core function this pass already built — which
  is what would make the REST verification-queue path converge with chat by
  construction.
- RFQ as the material fallback when the price book has no answer (the user can still
  answer directly in chat; RFQ is a distinct, unimplemented escalation).
- Unifying `app/core/price_book/pricing_math.py`'s `universal_factor` with
  `app/core/estimates/calculations.py`'s `convert_quantity` into one shared module —
  deliberately duplicated this pass (see `pricing_math.py`'s own docstring for why the
  raising/non-raising distinction isn't just a style choice).
- Reconciling the v2 model-vs-migration constraint-name mismatch repo-wide (only
  confirmed and avoided in this pass's own migration, not fixed at the source).
- `flat_amount` pricing has no reference form (no price-book row backs a flat dollar
  amount) — it remains usable via direct `apply_pricing_batch` calls (e.g. tests), just
  not reachable through `set_component_price`, which only ever writes `unit_rate`/
  `labor_productivity`.

### Verified live

Confirmed against a throwaway Neon DB and three real, sequential LLM turns (`gpt-5.5`/
openai) against a real 12-component seeded project, including a "resume" flow via a
bare chat reply that `detect_skill` correctly auto-detected with no explicit skill
call. All four unit-derivation branches confirmed correct, a deliberately `blocked`
component correctly left alone and structurally rejected on a direct write attempt, and
across ~40 tool calls zero prices/wages/productivity figures were ever anything other
than a price-book reference or a user-supplied value. One real bug found and fixed
mid-pass: `search_materials`/`search_labor_activities` hard-filtered on `csi_code`
instead of using it as a ranking boost. Full pass-by-pass log and what's still
unverified (the default Claude provider path, a live multiplier decision, freeform-chat
discovery of the v2 pricing tools): `docs/history/verification-log.md`.

## Price-book search: word-level matching, match tiers, and a synonym dictionary

`app/core/price_book/search.py`'s `material_match_clause`/`labor_match_clause` used to
compare a whole query phrase against a whole material name -- an ILIKE substring hit OR
`similarity(name, query) > 0.2`. Both are whole-string comparisons, which fails in this
domain because price-book names are terse (`12-2 ROMEX`) while the pricing agent queries
with long descriptive phrases. Confirmed from the real "Azul Plan 1" session
(landon@hylang.com, project `31da0d5a-...`, read out of `tool_calls`):

| query | old result |
|---|---|
| `Revent RVS70 house trim 0.7 sone exhaust fan` | **0** (but `Revent RVS70 exhaust fan` -> 1, same material) |
| `12/2 NM-B cable` | wrong rows (never `12-2 ROMEX` — Romex *is* NM-B) [1] |
| `panel surge protective device` | **0** (book holds `Panel surge protection device`) |
| the 5-breaker `ETN 2P30A ... 2P50A ...` string | **0** |

[1] Correction, from a later head-to-head against the real book: `12/2 NM-B cable` is a
synonym PROBE this work added, not a query the session actually issued, and the old
clause returns ~10 rows for it rather than zero -- just never the ROMEX row, because
the two names share no letters. The failure is "cannot find the right entry", not
"returns nothing". The other three rows above are verbatim session queries with
verbatim zero results, and two of them are self-controlled: the same book answered
`Panel surge protection device` with 2 rows at 21:16:37 and `panel surge protective
device` with 0 at 21:16:58, and answered `Revent RVS70 exhaust fan` with 1 row ten
seconds after the longer phrasing returned 0.

The zero-result threshold made present materials look absent, so the user hand-typed
prices the book already had. **34 of 122 components were priced after two hours.**

### The four pieces

- **`search_terms.py` (new, pure logic, zero I/O).** Tokenizer, spec-token grammar,
  match ladder, scorer, tier assignment. The whole ranking algorithm is provable offline
  -- `tests/test_price_book_search_terms.py` replays every production failure above as a
  fixture. The metric that actually fixes the problem is **name coverage**: of the
  CANDIDATE's own tokens, what fraction did the (synonym-expanded) query hit. `12-2
  ROMEX` has two tokens; a ten-word query hitting both is a perfect match for that row
  regardless of how many query words went unused. This is deliberately NOT what
  `ts_rank_cd` computes (no corpus-IDF, query-side weighted), which is why ranking lives
  in Python over a small recall set rather than in the SQL ORDER BY.
- **Generated `search_tsv` columns** on `materials`/`labor_activities`
  (`Computed(..., persisted=True)`, mirroring `DocumentNode.search_tsv`). Postgres
  maintains them, so none of the seven Material write paths (including the sync,
  no-flush bulk-import cache) needs a sync point, and `ADD COLUMN` populated every
  existing row -- **no backfill script exists or is needed**. `english` for name/prose
  (bridges `receptacles`/`receptacle`, `protective`/`protection` for free), `simple` for
  identifier fields. `notes` excluded as noise.
- **`price_book_synonyms` + `synonyms.py` (new).** Flat rows sharing a `group_key`, the
  canonical term stored as its own row, so an N-way group is ONE indexed self-join.
  Expansion is **query-time only** -- a dictionary edit takes effect immediately for
  every existing row with no recomputation. Scope follows `unit_aliases` exactly (system
  rows `user_id IS NULL` + per-tenant rows, two partial unique indexes, deliberately not
  under RLS as controlled vocabulary); the reader filters BOTH sides of the self-join to
  `user_id IS NULL OR user_id = :uid`. Seeded with 53 groups / 182 terms.
- **The spec-token guard.** Tokens containing digits match EXACT-ONLY -- never fuzzily,
  never by plural strip -- and a same-shape/different-digits pair (`12-2`/`12-3`,
  `RVS70`/`RVS110`, `30A`/`50A`) forces tier `partial` no matter how well the rest
  scored. Grouped per skeleton, not per token: a five-breaker query that matches one
  real row is agreement, not four conflicts (that per-token version was a real bug,
  caught by test before it shipped -- it scored the correct candidate to zero).

### Tiers, and what the agent does with them

`exact` (full name coverage, no fuzzy match, no spec conflict) -> use directly.
`strong` (>= 0.6 coverage, no conflict) -> use, but disclose the unmatched query terms
when reporting. `partial` -> never price silently; show candidates and ask.

**The never-zero fallback is the AGENT surface only, deliberately.**
`search_materials`/`search_labor_activities` (what the MCP tools call) fall back to
un-thresholded similarity ordering when recall comes back empty, so an all-`partial`,
low-score set becomes the "the book probably doesn't have this" signal instead of the
old silent zero that made the agent hand the question back to the user. The REST list
routes (`GET /api/price-book/materials|labor`, the human curator's search box) do NOT
do this -- a person typing a genuine non-match should see "no results", not five
unrelated rows. Confirmed by the HTTP edge-case pass: the same nonsense query returns
closest-matches through the MCP tool and `total=0` through REST. Both surfaces share
one match clause and one ranker; only this fallback differs, and it differs on purpose.
Every candidate carries `match` (score, tier, matched/unmatched terms, `spec_conflicts`,
`synonyms_applied`); the rules live in the search tools' docstrings.

### `record_price_book_synonym` (new MCP tool)

Closes the learn-from-confirmation loop: when the user confirms a `partial` candidate is
their item under another name, the agent records it and every future search finds it
directly. Idempotent; refuses to re-point a term at a different group; writes a
user-scoped row only (a tenant's correction never changes shipped vocabulary); records
vocabulary, never a number, so Stage 5's reference-only pricing guarantee is untouched.
In `PRICE_ESTIMATE_ALLOWED_TOOLS` and step 2 of the price directive. Its docstring
forbids calling it on a `spec_conflicts` near-miss -- `12-3` and `12-2` are different
cables, not two names for one thing, and recording that would corrupt both.

### Latency: the fuzzy lane is escalated-to, not always-on

Measured on the real 2,637-material book (server-side `EXPLAIN ANALYZE`, so network is
excluded): the full-text lane alone is **1.4ms**; adding one `<%` word-similarity probe
per recall term takes the same query to **29ms** -- the probes are ~95% of the cost,
computed per row. The old whole-phrase clause was **7.6ms**. So the trigram lane runs
only when the fast lane returned fewer than `_FUZZY_ESCALATION_THRESHOLD` (3) rows, and
is capped at the 8 longest terms. The common path is therefore ~5x FASTER than the
search it replaced, and typo tolerance is preserved for the queries that need it.
Note the planner scans by tenant and filters rather than using the GIN indexes at this
table size; the indexes are there for when a book grows large enough to change that.

### Verified live

Confirmed on a throwaway Neon branch forked from the shared DB (2,637 real materials):
every production failure this feature was built to fix now returns the right entry
ranked #1, and the synonym-learning loop was driven through real MCP dispatch end to
end. Full log: `docs/history/verification-log.md`. **Deploy ordering is load-bearing**
— `alembic upgrade head` must run against the shared DB before this code deploys, or
every price-book search raises `relation "price_book_synonyms" does not exist`.

## Pricing-request artifact (structured "these lines need prices" form)

The `price_estimate` skill used to end a pass by batching its open questions into
closing chat prose; the user typed answers back into chat and nothing tracked which
lines were still open. The `pricing_request` chat artifact replaces that: a durable,
structured list of unpriced components the frontend renders as an interactive form.
Per line the user can type a price (saved as an immutable project-local input and
applied only to this Estimate by default — no agent turn involved), explicitly check
"Add to price book" and supply durable-record identity/provenance fields, mark
material lines for a draft vendor RFQ, or deliberately leave the line open. There is
NO agent-suggestion/estimate anywhere in the schema — the product owner scrapped
suggestions outright, and leaving a line unpriced is a first-class outcome the agent
must never backfill. Spec origin:
`rialto-frontend/docs/pricing-request-artifact-backend-spec.md`, adapted to backend
reality (reference-only Stage 5 pricing, v2 estimates, takeoff-only RFQs — the spec's
`write_priced_line`/single-unit-price/dual-target-RFQ assumptions were all stale).

- **Own tables, deliberately not verification-queue kinds** (`pricing_requests` +
  `pricing_request_items`, `app/models/pricing_request.py` — the model docstring is
  the design document). The queue's one-row-one-decision vocabulary fits a grouped,
  agent-ordered, multi-item form as poorly as it fit project_review findings; the
  product owner explicitly approved the deviation, demanding strong FKs in exchange.
  The FK answer: `component_id` targets `estimate_v2_components.id`
  (`EstimateComponentIdentity`) — the durable logical identity `set_estimate_prices`'s
  `component_id` already is, never deleted by copy-on-write edits, RESTRICT so rows
  can't dangle. A rename re-keys identity without breaking the FK; readers detect that
  as `stale`, never as a referential error. `(observed_component_revision_id,
  component_id)` is a composite provenance FK onto `estimate_component_revisions (id,
  component_id)`; answer columns reference both project-local inputs and any explicit
  material_prices/labor_rates/labor_productivity promotions with RESTRICT provenance.
- **Statuses derived, never stored.** The request stores only `open`/`superseded`
  (a new pricing pass supersedes prior open requests wholesale); "resolved" and every
  per-item `open`/`priced`/`rfq_drafted` status are computed on each read from the
  live pricing state and RFQ line rows — a line priced through any other path shows
  resolved with zero bookkeeping. Item display values (label/qty/unit/cost_type) are
  snapshotted SERVER-side at creation from `get_estimate_pricing_state`; the agent
  only ever names component_ids + group_label/order (+ optional note and, for labor,
  a `trade_context` hint that prefills the wage form's trade).
- **`create_pricing_request` MCP tool** (`app/mcp/tools/pricing.py`, in
  `PRICE_ESTIMATE_ALLOWED_TOOLS`, deliberately NOT in `SKILL_TERMINAL_TOOLS`) — the
  skill directive's steps 2/7/9 were rewritten around it: one call at the end of a
  pass with every gap, no prose restatement. **Chat attach is automatic**:
  `displayed_artifact_refs_for_run` (`app/core/artifacts/service.py`) also picks up
  open `PricingRequest` rows by `run_id`, because the pricing skill has no
  `display_artifact` in its allow-list and `_refs_for_tool_call` is legacy-only —
  deterministic, no model cooperation needed.
- **REST**: `GET /api/projects/{id}/artifacts/pricing-requests/{request_id}`
  (`app/api/artifacts.py`; a superseded request still serves, its status says so) and
  `POST /api/projects/{id}/pricing-requests/{request_id}/respond`
  (`app/api/pricing_requests.py`). Respond semantics
  (`app/core/pricing_requests/service.py`): typed figures create append-only
  `ProjectPricingInput` rows with synthesized form provenance, then ONE
  `apply_pricing_batch` (`actor_type="user"`) prices every answered component using
  those local-input references. Optional nested promotion metadata creates reusable
  rows and an idempotent promotion ledger from the original input ID. Labor answers
  are two numbers (wage + hours-per-unit,
  wage-only for a `flat_hours` basis, hours-on-flat_hours rejected) or a
  `flat_amount`; a `blocked` component accepts ONLY `flat_amount` (mirrors
  `set_estimate_prices`' blocked rule). Error map: 404 not-found, 409 superseded and
  409 estimate-changed (CAS), 400 everything caller-fixable (stale items carry
  `stale_item_ids` in the detail). Unlisted items get no writes at all.
- **Idempotent double-submit, found live**: the respond `operation_id` is
  `uuid5(request_id + canonical sorted payload)`, and the service checks for an
  existing `EstimateVersion` with that operation id BEFORE recording anything —
  because a naive replay first mints fresh append-only price-book rows, which changes
  the changeset fingerprint and turns the same operation id into a hard
  `ConflictError` ("operation id was already used with different changes") instead of
  a benign replay. Unit tests could not catch this; the live pass did.
- **RFQ lines are dual-target again** (restoring, adapted, what `ba25702` deleted
  with v1): `RfqLineItem` is takeoff-row XOR estimate-component
  (`ck_rfq_line_items_target`), and a component-target line carries a display
  SNAPSHOT (label/description/quantity/unit, `ck_rfq_line_items_component_snapshot`)
  frozen at draft time — required, not stylistic: v2 component display fields live on
  copy-on-write revisions and are partial (CHECKs force NULL quantity/unit in
  unknown/unresolved states) while the vendor-facing contract is total, and a later
  estimate edit must not change what a vendor was already sent. `create_rfq` (core +
  MCP) takes `estimate_component_ids` alongside `takeoff_line_item_ids` (snapshots
  resolved from live pricing state; labor/unpriceable/removed components rejected);
  `update_rfq_draft`/`PATCH .../draft` replace per-kind (sending only one list leaves
  the other kind's lines untouched, so the existing takeoff-only frontend can't wipe
  component lines); the vendor magic-link form and the RFQ chat card both OUTER-join
  takeoff rows (the old inner joins silently dropped component lines) and branch to
  snapshots; `submit_rfq_response` writes real `target_kind`/`estimate_component_id`
  into `quoted_lines` JSON; `available_line_items` on the RFQ card now also offers
  quotable estimate material components. The email-send path needed zero changes (it
  never enumerates lines). The quote-comparison spreadsheet still has no component
  mode — its skip of component-target quoted lines is now logged, not silent, and the
  vendor's quoted price is instead surfaced per-item on the pricing-request artifact
  read (`vendor_quotes`, matched by `rfq_line_item_id`).
- **Migration `c4e9f1a2b3d5`** (hand-written): both tables, the `rfq_line_items`
  alters + CHECKs, `sync_user_id_from_parent` triggers, and RLS enable + tenant
  policies for the new tables (matching `a40b0a7ff6f0`'s fleet). Applied and
  downgrade/upgrade round-tripped on a throwaway Neon branch; **not yet applied to
  the shared/production DB** — run `alembic upgrade head` there as the usual explicit
  step before deploying this code.
- **`delete_project` now deletes pricing-request rows explicitly before the estimate
  identity rows** (sibling-cascade ordering vs the RESTRICT FKs is not guaranteed by
  Postgres), and — a pre-existing, unrelated bug found by this pass's live
  verification — also deletes `project_review_findings`/`project_reviews` before
  `agent_runs` (their `run_id` FK made deleting ANY reviewed project fail).

### Verified live

Full pass green on a throwaway Neon branch against a real seeded project — creation,
respond (material/labor/flat-amount answers), idempotent double-submit, RFQ marking
producing a snapshot-carrying draft, the vendor magic-link flow, and `delete_project`
all confirmed with no FK errors. Full log, including what's not yet verified (a live
LLM turn actually choosing to call `create_pricing_request`, a mixed takeoff+component
RFQ draft, the REST routes over real HTTP): `docs/history/verification-log.md`.

### Disclosed follow-ups

- Quote-comparison spreadsheet component mode (the logged skip above).
- Document-import quote matching to estimate components
  (`matched_estimate_component_id` restoration).
- Frontend contract mirroring (frontend repo's `CONTRACT.md` +
  `docs/pricing-request-frontend-handoff.md` in this repo is the handoff): new
  `pricing_request` ArtifactKind, widened RFQ line shape
  (`target_kind: "takeoff" | "estimate_component"`), labor form = two numbers or flat
  amount (never a single unit price), no suggestions.

## Takeoff-file prices as a price source

Real takeoff exports often carry their own pricing columns; ingestion has always
stored them verbatim on `TakeoffLineItem.imported_material_unit_price` /
`imported_labor_hours_per_unit` (+ the two `*_factor` columns), but nothing
downstream ever read them — Stage 5 pricing is reference-only, so a number that never
becomes a price-book row is structurally invisible to it. Confirmed live on the
"Azul Plan 1" session (project `31da0d5a-...`): all three takeoff uploads carried
labor hours on every row and two carried material prices on every row, yet the agent
ran 120 price-book searches, asked the user 16 times, and 10 of the 14 material
prices the user hand-typed back exactly matched a price already sitting in their own
uploaded file. Closed by three additive pieces, none of which weaken the "the model
never retypes a number" guarantee:

- **`app/core/price_book/takeoff_promotion.py`** — the one door file numbers pass
  through into the price book. `promote_takeoff_prices` re-reads each current takeoff
  row's stored number server-side (the caller passes no figures, same posture as
  `set_estimate_prices`) and writes ordinary `source="imported"` `MaterialPrice` /
  `LaborProductivity` rows with `source_project_id` (+ `source_document_id` for
  materials) provenance — all pre-existing legal values/columns, **no migration**.
  Material identity resolves via the same `_find_or_create_material` path curation
  uses (label + resolved unit); labor hours become a `LaborActivity` named by the row
  label plus a productivity row. Rows with an unresolvable unit or zero/absent figure
  are skipped and reported, never guessed; the `*_factor` columns are recorded in the
  price row's notes but never applied (their semantics aren't knowable from the file
  — see `TakeoffLineItem`'s own docstring); re-promotion is idempotent (duplicate
  current price/productivity on the same identity → skipped). `add_material_price` /
  `add_productivity` gained optional `source`/provenance params (default `"manual"`,
  every pre-existing caller unchanged) rather than a second write path.
- **`get_estimate_pricing_state.takeoff_file_prices`** (`TakeoffFilePricesOut`,
  `app/core/estimates/pricing_state.py`) — present only when a current takeoff row
  carries a usable embedded figure: per-kind row counts, the carrying filenames, and
  per-kind `*_promoted` flags (existence of `source="imported"` rows with this
  project's `source_project_id`). Counting reuses the takeoff rows the state read
  already loads; only the two promoted-flag EXISTS queries are extra, and only when
  a file price exists at all. The MCP tool's summary message also nudges ("embedded
  prices not yet in the price book") so the model can't miss it.
- **The confirmation flow** — `promote_takeoff_prices` and `ask_user` were added to
  `PRICE_ESTIMATE_ALLOWED_TOOLS`, and the price directive gained step 2 (later steps
  renumbered 3-10, cross-references updated): when state shows unpromoted file
  prices, ask the user ONCE via `ask_user` (stable question key
  `use_takeoff_file_prices`, so `raise_setup_question`'s dedupe returns a prior
  answer instantly instead of re-asking — the "don't ask again after a decline"
  behavior is structural, not remembered), promote on a yes, proceed with the book
  alone on a no. The gate itself is prompt-level (like `record_user_supplied_price`'s
  only-what-the-user-typed rule); what's structural is that the tool takes no
  numbers. `ask_user` is scoped in the directive to this one question — ordinary
  missing-price questions still go through `create_pricing_request`.

**Verified**: full pytest suite green (1256 passed); `ruff` clean; live pass against a
throwaway Neon branch of the shared DB using the real Azul Plan 1 rows — see "How this
was verified" conventions (summary read → promotion → counts → idempotent rerun →
search finding the promoted prices). **Not verified**: a live LLM turn choosing to ask
and promote on its own judgment (same limitation as every other pass here), and the
`ask_user` question's frontend rendering in the pricing context.

**Deliberately not built**: automatic promotion at ingest (the user must confirm —
file prices can be stale bid-day numbers); a per-component "this row's file price"
hint on `ComponentPricingOut` (promotion makes ordinary search find them, which
covers the need without a second lookup path); applying the `*_factor` multipliers.

## Price book PDF ingestion

`docs/price-book.md` (§4) specified a CSV/XLSX-only, mapping-confirmed import flow for
materials/labor; PDF was out of scope there. This pass added PDF support for the
**materials** import (`POST /api/price-book/materials/preview` /
`.../materials/import` — no new routes) without touching a single line of the existing
CSV/XLSX code (`app/core/price_book/parsing.py::parse_table`). The only branch point is
`materials.py`'s `_load_table`: a `.pdf` filename routes to
`app/core/price_book/pdf_extraction.py::extract_pdf_price_table` instead of
`parse_table`; every downstream step (mapping, validation, `_import_one_row`, identity
resolution, unit resolution, duplicate detection) only ever sees the same `ParsedTable`/
`SourceRow` shape either path produces, and stays completely format-blind.

- **Two tiers, deterministic-first**, same precedent as takeoff's
  `column_mapping.py`/`table_layout.py`: `_extract_grid_table` (`pdfplumber`, a new
  dependency) reconstructs a real bordered table from actual text tokens and cell
  boundaries — same trust level as CSV/XLSX, no model involved. It abstains (`None`)
  on anything it isn't confident about (fewer than 2 named header columns, a duplicate
  normalized header, no rows at all) rather than guessing, since tier 2 is the safety
  net. `_extract_structured_llm` is that safety net: a Claude `messages.parse` call
  (`output_format=ExtractedPriceBookMaterials`) reads either the extracted text layer
  or, for a scanned PDF with none, native PDF vision (mirrors
  `app/core/vendor_bids/extraction.py`'s exact text-then-vision pattern), for a
  document with no grid to reconstruct at all (a repeating record/card layout, no
  ruling lines). Runs inline in the preview/import request cycle (not a background
  job, unlike vendor quote extraction), bounded at 90s.
- **The model both locates and transcribes in tier 2, unlike everywhere else in this
  pipeline** — a real, disclosed departure from "never let the model retype a
  number." Every row produced this way is still written immediately (so it's usable
  right away, same posture as vendor_bids' quote extraction), but its
  `price_book_import_rows` staging row is marked `resolution_status="ambiguous"`
  (already defined in the schema, previously unused by anything) with an explanatory
  note, rather than `"resolved"` like a trusted CSV/XLSX/pdf_grid row — both on the
  create path and on the duplicate-match path (a "this matches an existing price"
  determination rests on the same transcribed values a fresh row would; see "Verified
  live" below for the bug this caught). `preview`/`import` responses both carry a new
  `extraction_method` field (`null` | `"pdf_grid"` | `"pdf_llm"`) so a caller always
  knows which trust tier produced a given result without inspecting the file itself.
- **Closed real mapping-vocabulary gaps the sample files exposed, independent of
  PDF-ness** — a rich CSV/XLSX with the same columns would have hit them too.
  `TARGET_FIELDS`/`_HEADER_ALIASES` (`materials.py`) grew five fields: `csi_code`/
  `category` write onto `Material`'s existing (previously unmapped) columns;
  `priced_on` is now a per-row mappable date column, not just a whole-file override;
  `price_source`/`region`/`source_material_id` have no dedicated column and fold into
  `notes` instead of being silently dropped (`_compose_import_notes`).
  `price_source`'s `"vendor_quote"` value (case/spacing-insensitive) maps onto
  `MaterialPrice.source`'s real CHECK vocabulary; anything else (including a real but
  unlisted label like `"historical_avg"`) stays `"imported"`, never invented outside
  the CHECK — the raw label is preserved in `notes` either way
  (`_resolve_price_source`). `_find_or_create_material`'s bulk-import path gained the
  same fill-in-if-blank posture `create_material`'s manual-entry path already had for
  `csi_code`/`category`.
- **Not a new Anthropic-vs-OpenAI seam** — `price_book_pdf_extraction_model`
  defaults to `anthropic_model` and this is Claude-only, like takeoff column mapping
  and vendor quote extraction, not routed through `app/core/agent/provider.py`'s
  dual-provider abstraction (this is a pure ingestion-time concern, not agent-facing).
- **Identity/duplicate matching hardened against spacing and capitalization,
  format-agnostic (not a PDF-specific fix)** — every identity/duplicate lookup in
  `materials.py`/`labor.py`/`curation.py` compared a Python-side `key_text()`-normalized
  value (casefolded, internal whitespace collapsed) against a SQL-side
  `func.lower(func.trim(column))` — which only handles case and leading/trailing
  whitespace, not an internal run like `"Type  X  GWB"` (double space). New
  `app.core.price_book.parsing.normalized_text_column()` closes that gap
  (`func.regexp_replace(func.lower(func.trim(column)), r"\s+", " ", "g")`), applied
  everywhere: `Material.name`/`size_spec`/`manufacturer_part_number`/`manufacturer`,
  `VendorContact.company_name`, `MaterialPrice.vendor_name`, `LaborActivity.name`,
  `LaborClassification.trade`/`level`. Found one real, live bug in the process: labor
  import's `trade` parameter (a whole-file value, not a per-row column) was never run
  through any whitespace cleanup at all before comparison or storage — a stray leading/
  trailing space (an easy copy-paste accident) silently created a second, redundant
  `LaborClassification` instead of reusing the existing one. Fixed by normalizing
  `trade` once at `import_labor_price_file`'s entry (`clean_text(trade) or "General"`),
  the same "normalize once at the boundary" pattern used elsewhere in this codebase.
  Stored values always keep their original casing/punctuation (only the *comparison*
  is casefolded) — `key_text()`/`normalized_text_column()` are never used to decide
  what gets written, only what counts as "the same thing already exists."

### Verified live

Both extraction tiers (grid-based `pdfplumber` and LLM-based vision) confirmed against
real sample PDFs — 33/33 rows correct on both a clean-table and a gridless sample. A
throwaway Neon branch confirmed the database write path produces identical results
(33 materials, not 99) regardless of which extraction tier ran, meaning tier 2's
transcription matched the deterministic paths exactly. One real bug found and fixed
live: a tier-2 row matching an existing price was wrongly marked as fully trusted
(`resolution_status="resolved"`) instead of `ambiguous`. A preview/import consistency
gap (two separate HTTP requests re-calling the LLM on the same bytes) was found and
closed with a content-hash cache. Full log, and what's not yet verified (labor import
has no PDF support; no page-batching for large catalogs; runs inline in the request
cycle rather than as a background job): `docs/history/verification-log.md`.

## Audit/evidence design

- `agent_runs` / `tool_calls` / `evidence_refs` exist, but `agent_runs` is **not** created
  per MCP tool call — there's no protocol-level "conversation turn" boundary to hang it on.
  `tool_calls.run_id` is nullable and stays null for a bare tool call from a chat host; only
  a future whole-workflow REST endpoint (e.g. "price this project") would create one run and
  thread its id through.
- `run_logged_tool` (`app/core/audit.py`) is the only thing that writes `tool_calls`, and the
  only thing that commits *business-logic writes*. On success it commits the tool's writes +
  the success log together; on failure it rolls back the tool's writes and logs a separate
  failed row. Core service functions (`app/core/*/service.py`) must never call
  `session.commit()`. **One narrow, deliberate exception**: `set_current_tool` (also in
  `app/core/audit.py`, see "Live tool-call visibility" below) commits too, but only ever
  writes `agent_runs.current_tool_name`/`current_tool_started_at` — a live-progress marker,
  never `tool_calls` itself and never anything business-logic-bearing. `tool_calls` write
  exclusivity through `run_logged_tool` is unchanged.
- `evidence_refs` is populated **only** via the `evidence` argument callers pass to
  `write_priced_line` — a generic wrapper cannot know which historical row justified a price,
  only the caller that picked it knows that.

## Historical data source seam (retired — moved to docs/)

This described deleted v1 code (`EstimateHistoricalProvider`/`SyntheticHistoricalProvider`/
the tiered `HistoricalDataProvider` merge in `app/core/pricing/provider.py`).
`RialtoHistoricalProvider` is the one survivor — kept only for `write_priced_line`'s
promotion into `historical_prices`. Stage 5's `search_price_book_materials`/
`search_price_book_labor` are the current price-lookup tools and search the price book
directly, not this tiered seam. Full tier-design writeup:
`docs/history/v1-tool-surface-and-retired-designs.md`.

## Quote comparison spreadsheet

`app/core/spreadsheets/service.py` builds one of two DB-backed workbook modes, chosen
by one check: whether the project has any `takeoff_line_items` at all — nothing else
(quote count, import source) affects which mode is picked, and there is no mixed mode.
Projects with takeoff rows use `mode="takeoff"`: one row per takeoff line, vendor quote
columns, and estimator selection/adjustment columns that can be applied through
`POST .../apply-pricing`; only vendor quote lines matched to a takeoff line item appear
here; unmatched lines (including from a directly-uploaded quote document, even when the
project does have takeoff scope) are excluded from the grid but remain visible via
`list_vendor_quote_line_items`. Projects without takeoff rows use `mode="quote_material"`:
one stable row per persisted `QuoteMaterialGroup`, with vendor source lines assigned to
it regardless of takeoff match (there is none to match against). Quote-material mode is
comparison-only and rejects Apply Pricing because there is no takeoff scope. A project
with neither takeoff nor any uploaded quotes still resolves to `mode="quote_material"`,
just empty (`quote_count`/`row_count`/`vendor_count` all 0) rather than an error. Both
modes overlay estimator-owned JSON state from `project_spreadsheets`; canonical takeoff,
quote, and material-group rows remain protected source data.

Direct imports and RFQ responses share `QuoteSubmission` as the quote header. RFQ replies
use `source_kind="rfq_response"` and keep `rfq_id`; uploaded quote documents use
`source_kind="document_import"`, `rfq_id=NULL`, and `project_document_id`. Extracted rows
are stored in `vendor_quote_line_items`. The old `vendor_bid` document role, job name, MCP
names, and synthetic-RFQ data remain readable only as migration compatibility; new paths
normalize to `vendor_quote` and do not create synthetic RFQs.

## Vendor quote ingestion and grouping

`document_role=vendor_quote` uploads store the file and enqueue `vendor_quote.extract`.
The worker downloads the original, uses extracted text when available, and sends scanned
PDF page batches to Claude vision when text extraction is empty. Structured extraction
creates the `QuoteSubmission` and all `VendorQuoteLineItem` rows before grouping. Exact
duplicate rows from a single model response are removed; takeoff matching remains a
separate deterministic step used only when takeoff scope exists.

Each quote upload ensures one durable `quote_material.regroup` coordinator exists for the
project. The project-row lock serializes coordinator creation across simultaneous uploads.
The coordinator defers while any extraction is pending/running; deferral schedules the job
again without consuming an attempt or recording an error, then grouping runs as soon as
all extraction jobs are terminal. Failed documents do not block successfully extracted
quotes. Retry-import requeues failed extraction jobs and restores the coordinator.

Grouping loads all unprotected quote lines for the project, batches them through the
configured Claude grouping model, globally reconciles provisional groups, and persists
stable `QuoteMaterialGroup` identities where source-line overlap permits. One normalization
boundary repairs metadata-level model problems before strict validation: omitted known
lines become reviewable singletons, unapproved multi-line groups split into review
candidates, and malformed optional suggestions are dropped. Unknown source ids and
duplicate assignments remain hard failures. There is no fuzzy grouping fallback.

The material-group REST endpoints expose source lines and allow canonical edits, merges,
and moving/splitting lines. User corrections are authoritative: edited groups and groups
containing manually moved/merged lines are excluded from future automatic regrouping.
Import progress and failures are projected through both project overview and the quote
comparison artifact.

Below-threshold "these may be the same material" merge proposals are persisted as
`verification_requests` rows (kind `quote_group_merge`) rather than a bespoke suggestion
table — see "User verification queue" below. Each regroup supersedes the previous pending
proposals wholesale and raises fresh ones; user edits/merges/moves on a group supersede
pending proposals referencing it. The old `.../grouping-review-suggestions` endpoints
remain as thin compatibility wrappers over the same rows (same id namespace), so either
surface resolves the same request.

## User verification queue

One generic mechanism for "this output is too uncertain to stand without the user's
sign-off," shared by every pipeline stage and the chat agent — do not add another
bespoke review table/endpoint pair for a new stage; register a kind instead.

- **Envelope vs. meaning.** `verification_requests` (app/models/verification.py) holds
  only queue semantics: `kind`, `status` (`pending | resolved | dismissed | superseded`),
  `confidence`, `reason`, a polymorphic `subject_table`/`subject_id` pointer, a JSON
  `payload`, the user's `decision`, `raised_by` (`ingestion | grouping | agent`), a
  nullable `run_id` (threaded from `current_run_id` for agent-raised rows), and a
  `dedupe_key`. What a kind *means* lives in the registry
  (`app/core/verification/registry.py`), with one `VerificationKindSpec` per kind in
  `kinds.py`: payload schema (pydantic), allowed decisions, and a resolver that
  dispatches into the owning domain package. `kind`/`status` are plain strings validated
  by the registry, deliberately not Postgres enums.
- **Current kinds** (`app/core/verification/kinds.py`) — six registered: `quote_group_merge`
  (raised by grouping; resolver calls `material_groups.apply_group_merge_decision` —
  decisions `merge`/`keep_separate`); `takeoff_column_mapping` (raised by ingestion when
  the LLM header-mapping fallback ran; `accept` is a no-op confirm — a `remap` decision
  that re-parses is a deliberate follow-up, not implemented); `estimate_structure_issue`
  (raised by the estimates package for a low-confidence structural item/component;
  `confirm` sets `user_confirmed=True`, `exclude` writes an `EstimateSourceExclusion`);
  `quote_estimate_match` (raised when a quote line ambiguously matches more than one
  Estimate material component; `assign` sets
  `VendorQuoteLineItem.matched_estimate_component_id`, `leave_unmatched` clears it);
  `priced_line_proposal` (agent-raised via MCP, legacy pre-Estimate projects only;
  `accept` writes through `write_priced_line` with `source=user_input`); and
  `estimate_component_price_proposal` (agent-raised via MCP, the Estimate-domain analog
  — `accept` writes through `write_estimate_component_price` instead, since
  `priced_line_proposal` targets the wrong table once an Estimate exists). Only the last
  two are `agent_raisable`; the other four are pipeline-raised only. `dismiss` is
  universal on every kind and never runs a resolver.
- **One REST surface** (`app/api/verifications.py`): list + resolve. Decisions are
  enumerated in the response so a frontend can render unknown kinds generically.
  Pending counts per kind are projected into `GET /overview`.
- **Convergence rule — supersede on authoritative write.** Any authoritative write to a
  subject supersedes its pending requests: `write_priced_line`/
  `write_estimate_component_price` supersede pending proposals for that subject (so chat
  re-prices, spreadsheet apply-pricing, and the accept-resolver itself all clear stale
  proposals by construction); group edits/merges/moves supersede pending
  `quote_group_merge`s that reference the touched groups; producers re-raising the same
  finding supersede via `dedupe_key`. Nothing pends forever against reality, and nothing
  blocks on a pending request — they are advisory, not locks.
- **Import-order note:** domain modules import `verification.service` at module level,
  while `kinds.py` is imported lazily by the service on first use
  (`ensure_kinds_registered`) precisely so its resolvers can reference those same domain
  modules. Anything touching the registry *before* a service call (e.g. the MCP tool)
  must call `ensure_kinds_registered()` first.

## Takeoff column mapping (LLM fallback)

Real takeoff files arrive with arbitrary column headers (`"What estimator wrote"`,
`"Counted amount"`, `"Measure-ish"`, etc.) that a fixed alias list will never fully
cover, sometimes with no single clean header row at all (a logo/title block, a header
split across two physical rows, several distinct tables in one sheet), and sometimes
with a column-name choice that's structurally *wrong* rather than the data itself
being bad. Despite the section title, this now covers all of takeoff-file parsing, not
just column-name mapping — four layered mechanisms, each opt-in and best-effort (any
LLM failure degrades to whatever the prior layer already produced, never raises):

1. **Format support.** `app/core/ingestion/parsers.py`'s `parse_takeoff_file` (async)
   handles CSV, XLSX (`openpyxl`), and legacy binary XLS (`xlrd` — `openpyxl` only
   reads `.xlsx`/`.xlsm`; `.xls` is still common from older estimating-tool exports).
   `_xls_rows` and `_xlsx_rows` both funnel into the same `_rows_from_worksheet_values`,
   so every mechanism below applies identically regardless of which container format
   the file arrived in — only the raw-grid extraction differs.
   `app/core/ingestion/service.py`'s `_is_supported_takeoff_file`/
   `normalize_document_role` accept `.xls` the same way as `.xlsx`. PDF takeoffs remain
   unsupported — a deliberately deferred scope decision, not an oversight.
2. **Deterministic header-row detection, multi-block-aware.** `_find_header_rows`
   (plural) scans the *whole* sheet, not just an initial window, for every row
   matching a real takeoff header signature (a quantity header alongside a
   label/description header), and `_rows_from_worksheet_values` gives each occurrence
   its own independent header→column mapping and data span. This exists because real
   exports sometimes repeat the exact same title+header block multiple times down one
   sheet (one printed "page" per phase) — treating only the first occurrence as
   authoritative silently dropped every later block. Falls back to a loose "≥2 filled
   cells in the first 10 rows" heuristic, once, only when no strict signature is found
   anywhere in the sheet.
3. **Column-mapping LLM fallback (tier 1).**
   `app/core/ingestion/column_mapping.py`'s `maybe_map_columns_with_llm`, gated by
   `TAKEOFF_COLUMN_MAPPING_MODE` (currently `llm_fallback` in `.env`; code default is
   `deterministic` | `llm_fallback` | `llm_always`, same shape as
   `document_query_expansion_mode`). In `llm_fallback` mode it's only invoked when the
   header row itself can't resolve label/quantity/unit deterministically (checked once
   at the header level, not per row). Uses `takeoff_column_mapping_model`
   (`claude-haiku-4-5-20251001`), a separate setting from `anthropic_model` — a small
   classification-style call (headers + a few sample rows in, a header-to-schema-field
   mapping out). It never transcribes a cell value itself — row values are still parsed
   by the existing deterministic code (`_parse_quantity`, subtotal rejection, etc.)
   against whichever header the mapping points at, so a confidently-wrong mapping
   produces skipped rows, never silently-wrong data, by construction.
   - **Two deterministic guards run on whatever mapping the LLM returns, before it's
     ever accepted** — both added after live (non-mocked) testing against two real
     files (`temp-test-files/Oro Takeoff.xlsx`, `Azul Takeoff.xls`) surfaced the model
     making a plausible-but-wrong column choice, even when the *correct* column was
     identified elsewhere in the very same response:
     - `_guard_against_fragment_label` — swaps `label`↔`description` when `label`'s
       chosen column's sample values are, on average, at least 2x shorter *and* at
       least 6 characters shorter than `description`'s. Confirmed live case: the model
       mapped `label` to a "Size" column (`'3"'`, `'7504W'`) while correctly
       identifying a separate "Description" column holding the real item names in the
       same response, producing labels like `'3"'` instead of `'3" HOCKEY STICK'`.
     - `_guard_against_extended_total_rate` — rejects a `material_unit_price`/
       `labor_hours_per_unit` mapping whose *own header text* contains a rollup token
       (`result`/`total`/`extended`/`subtotal`/`amount`/`sum`), swapping to an unused
       header containing `"unit"` if one exists, else nulling the field outright.
       Confirmed live case: the model mapped `material_unit_price` to a "Material
       Result" column (= unit price × quantity) — invisible on quantity=1 rows, but
       silently 2x wrong on a quantity=2 row (`$16` unit price imported as `$32`).
       Nulling when no replacement candidate exists is deliberate: a missing price is
       far safer than a wrong one silently feeding pricing math later.
     Both guards are pure functions with dedicated tests (`tests/test_column_mapping.py`).
     Both prompt sections were also strengthened to explicitly warn against the exact
     failure pattern observed, as a cheap complement to the hard guard — not a
     replacement for it, since the guard is what still catches a future regression on
     a model call the prompt fix doesn't prevent.
4. **Table-layout LLM fallback (tier 2), a further fallback beyond tier 1.**
   `app/core/ingestion/table_layout.py`'s `maybe_locate_tables_with_llm`, gated by
   `takeoff_table_layout_mode` (`.env`/code default `deterministic`, i.e. off). Only
   consulted when tier 1 (deterministic scanning plus column mapping together) still
   produces zero usable rows for a worksheet, *or* a suspiciously high skip ratio
   (`_should_try_table_layout`, gated by `takeoff_table_layout_skip_ratio_threshold`,
   default `0.5` — deliberately conservative, well above a normal messy real file's
   ~25% skip rate, and only applied once a worksheet has enough nonblank rows for a
   ratio to mean anything). Unlike tier 1, this is handed the raw, un-sliced worksheet
   grid (logo/title block and all) and returns only structural coordinates — header row
   range(s), a data row range, and a column *index* (never a header string, since a
   multi-row or missing header may have no reliable text to key off at all) for each
   schema field — never a transcribed value; `parsers.py`'s existing value-parsing code
   still owns every number, mirroring tier 1's safety invariant one level up. Uses
   `takeoff_table_layout_model` (defaults to `anthropic_model`, not the cheap one —
   locating a table amid noise is a harder structural-reasoning task than tier 1's
   "map this one already-clean header row"). `_LLM_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=180`/
   `_MAX_GRID_ROWS=2000`/`_MAX_GRID_COLS=60`/`_MAX_OUTPUT_TOKENS=8000` were widened from
   initially conservative defaults after a real 425-row/33-column/6-table paginated
   export needed all of that headroom and over 60s of reasoning to locate every block —
   this fallback only ever runs inside an async ingestion job, never a live request, so
   the wider budget costs nothing user-facing. No `temperature` param is passed to the
   API call — newer Claude models (e.g. `claude-sonnet-5`, this setting's default)
   reject it outright with a 400 that the caller's best-effort exception handling was
   silently swallowing as "the LLM found nothing," which masked this fallback failing
   on every single call regardless of grid size until traced down directly.
5. **Assembly propagation from unitless header rows.** Real takeoff exports often
   interleave category/subgroup header rows directly in the row stream instead of
   using a dedicated assembly/category column — e.g. a zero-quantity divider row like
   `"UNDERGROUND"`, then a nested `"LENNAR 200A"` title row, then the real, unit-bearing
   line items belonging to it. These rows fail the ordinary quantity/unit checks and
   used to be silently dropped with no trace. `parse_takeoff_file`'s main loop now
   tracks a running "current group label" while walking rows in order: a skipped row
   updates it only when it has **no unit at all** but does carry real label/description
   text (`_group_header_text`) — the one reliable signal distinguishing an
   organizational divider from a real line item, since even a genuine zero-quantity
   catalog item still carries a real unit and correctly leaves the context untouched.
   Every real parsed row that doesn't already have its own `assembly` (from a dedicated
   column, when one exists) gets stamped with the most recently seen group label.
   Confirmed live against Azul Takeoff.xls: all 138 real rows now carry the correct
   nested group label matching the file's own structure, where previously `assembly`
   was null for all of them. **Only wired into the main deterministic/column-mapping
   path so far** — the table-layout fallback's separate row-extraction code
   (`_rows_from_table_region`) doesn't get this treatment yet, since neither real file
   tested this session ever needed tier 2 to engage.
- Any LLM failure at either tier (no API key, timeout, malformed JSON, rejected
  request) makes the corresponding `maybe_*` function return `None` rather than raise —
  the caller keeps whatever the prior layer already produced.
- When either LLM tier *was* used, ingestion raises a verification request so the user
  can confirm the parse before it's trusted (see "User verification queue" above) —
  `takeoff_column_mapping` (tier 1, carrying the chosen mapping/headers/parsed-skipped
  counts) or `takeoff_table_layout` (tier 2, carrying the located region(s) per sheet).
  Both are accept-only today (no `remap`/`reclassify` resolver yet, matching the
  existing deferred-follow-up pattern elsewhere in this queue) — the upload itself is
  never blocked on either.

**Not verified against a live Postgres this pass** — `.xls` support, both
column-mapping guards, and the assembly-propagation fix were all verified via
`ruff check .`, the full test suite, and live (non-mocked) Anthropic API calls against
two real files, but not re-run through the full ingest→job-handler→DB-write path this
session. That full path *was* exercised once, manually, against the live shared Neon
DB for the Oro file (see "How this was verified" below) — but before these fixes
existed, so the corrected label/price/assembly values haven't themselves been checked
against a live database row yet.

## File storage (Cloudflare R2)

R2 speaks the S3 API directly, so `app/core/storage/blob.py` talks to it with
`aioboto3` (`endpoint_url=R2_ENDPOINT_URL`, `region_name="auto"`,
`Config(signature_version="s3v4")`) — no subprocess sidecar needed, unlike the earlier
Vercel Blob integration this replaced. Credentials come from `R2_ACCOUNT_ID`/
`R2_ACCESS_KEY_ID`/`R2_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY`/`R2_BUCKET_NAME`/`R2_ENDPOINT_URL`. The bucket
has **no public base URL** configured (no custom domain, no r2.dev binding), so nothing
is ever stored as a permanently public link: `upload_document` returns the object's
storage key, and `ProjectDocument.blob_url` persists that key (the column name predates
the switch and now holds a key, not a URL). Reads never persist a presigned URL — every
caller that needs a fetchable link mints one fresh, on demand, via `get_presigned_url`
(default 1-hour expiry): `app/core/project/document_catalog.py`'s `list_project_documents` and
`app/api/documents.py`'s upload response both call it directly at the point they return
a link to the frontend, rather than presigning once and storing the result. Server-side
re-reads that never leave the backend (e.g. the `vendor_quote.extract` job) skip
presigning entirely and call `download_document`, which fetches bytes straight from R2
via an authenticated `get_object`.

## Hosting identity — resolve before every infrastructure operation

Do not infer live topology from this file or `render.yaml`. Railway provenance does
not prove a database identity or establish that legacy Render workers are retired.
Web, worker and control-plane bindings may differ. Inspect current provider service,
environment, source commit and resolved database endpoint read-only before testing.
Do not redeploy, repoint or delete a shared workload based on these historical notes.
Only verified isolated test resources have standing mutation authorization; retain
the exact test databases/projects/storage until Tomasz confirms deletion.

## Production deployment (Render — RETIRED, historical only)

One Render environment (`evm-d9a87apo3t8c738fb5j0`, workspace "Rialto"), both live
services deployed from `github.com/UseRialto/rialto-backend`, branch `main`, region
`oregon`, auto-deploying on every new commit to `main`. Confirmed directly against the
Render API (not from `render.yaml` — see the drift warning below), current as of
2026-08-20, both live services on commit `2b235a9`:

- **Web** — service name `rialto-backend-1` (`srv-da1ag449v7es73auv800`), Standard
  plan, 1 instance, `https://rialto-backend-1.onrender.com`. Build: `uv sync --frozen
  && uv cache prune --ci`. Start: `uv run uvicorn app.main:app --host 0.0.0.0 --port
  $PORT`.
- **Worker** — service name `rialto-backend` (`srv-da18bo0jo6nc7383670g`), Standard
  plan, 1 instance, no public URL (background worker). Build: `uv sync --frozen
  --no-dev`. Start: `uv run python -m app.workers.document_queues` — runs all three
  document queues (`document_structured`, `document_native`, `document_enrichment`) as
  concurrent asyncio tasks in one process, deliberately, per that module's own
  docstring: three separate `app.workers.jobs --queue ...` processes would each pay the
  full Docling/transformers/torch import cost independently, enough to OOM a small
  instance.
- **Suspended, dead — do not debug against this URL** — a third service, also named
  `rialto-backend` (`srv-d9a87aurnols739stec0`), Free plan, `suspended: true` (suspended
  by a user, not Render), `https://rialto-backend.onrender.com`. This is the original
  pre-worker-split web service, superseded by `rialto-backend-1` above. Same repo/branch,
  stale build config (no worker split). It receives no traffic; if it's ever noticed
  running again that's a configuration mistake, not a second production instance.
- **No Render Postgres.** The database is Neon (see "Database state" below); Render
  hosts only compute. File storage is Cloudflare R2 (see "File storage" above), also
  off-Render.

### `render.yaml` is aspirational, not authoritative — a real, confirmed drift

The blueprint in the repo root and the live dashboard-configured services disagree on
several points. Comparing `render.yaml`'s web-service block against `get_service` on
the actual live `srv-da1ag449v7es73auv800`:

| | `render.yaml` | live service |
|---|---|---|
| service name | `rialto-backend` | `rialto-backend-1` |
| `autoDeployTrigger` | `checksPass` | `commit` |
| `healthCheckPath` | `/ready` | *(empty — not wired)* |
| `preDeployCommand` | `uv run alembic upgrade head` | *(absent)* |
| build command | `uv sync --frozen --no-dev` | `uv sync --frozen && uv cache prune --ci` |
| start command | `... --workers 1` | *(no `--workers` flag)* |
| worker service name | `rialto-document-workers` | `rialto-backend` |

The live services were configured through the Render dashboard independently of the
blueprint file, and the two have drifted apart. **Two consequences, both real, not
hypothetical:**

1. **Migrations are not automatically applied on deploy.** `render.yaml` claims a
   `preDeployCommand: uv run alembic upgrade head`, but the live web service has no
   pre-deploy command configured at all. This actually *matches* — rather than
   contradicts — this file's existing "Database state" convention below of applying
   `alembic upgrade head` as a separate, explicit, manual step before/after deploying
   application code; just don't trust `render.yaml`'s text as evidence that step is
   automated. It isn't, on the service that's actually live.
2. **Render's own health-check probe is not wired to `/ready`** on the live web
   service (`healthCheckPath` is empty), even though `/ready` exists and does real
   work — see "Queue control-plane isolation and cancellation" below ("`/ready`
   validates both the main and control pools plus every enabled coordinator's recent
   poll"). A broken `/ready` would not currently block Render from routing traffic to
   or restarting this instance. If you rely on `render.yaml`'s `healthCheckPath: /ready`
   line as documentation of current behavior, you'll be wrong until this is either
   fixed in the dashboard or the dashboard config is exported back into the blueprint.

Until someone reconciles the two (either push the dashboard config into `render.yaml`
and adopt Blueprint-driven deploys for real, or delete/relabel `render.yaml` as a
reference-only starting point), **treat the live dashboard config above as the ground
truth for what's actually running, and `render.yaml` as a stale draft of it** — flagged
in "Open follow-ups" below.

### The web/worker document-queue split, and how it's enforced

`app/config.py`'s `_cross_field_runtime_bounds` validator hard-fails startup whenever
`RENDER=true` (which Render injects automatically) unless: `RUNTIME_ENVIRONMENT=
production`, `DOCUMENT_EXTRACTOR=native`, `WEB_CONCURRENCY=1`, every R2/DB/Voyage
secret is present and non-localhost, and **exactly one** of `DOCUMENT_WORKERS_ENABLED`
/ `DOCUMENT_WORKERS_DELEGATED` is `true` — the validator explicitly rejects both `true`
(ambiguous — in-process queues and a separate worker service both trying to own the
same queues) and both `false` (nobody would be running the document queues at all).
This is a real, code-level guarantee, not just a convention documented here — a
misconfigured Render service refuses to boot rather than silently double-processing or
dropping document jobs.

- **Web service env**: `DOCUMENT_WORKERS_ENABLED=false`, `DOCUMENT_WORKERS_DELEGATED=
  true`, `JOBS_WORKER_ENABLED=true`. The web process does *not* run the document
  queues in-process, but still runs every *other* job queue in-process (RFQ send, quote
  regroup, etc.) via its own `JOBS_WORKER_ENABLED` flag — the split is document-queue-
  specific, not "web never runs jobs."
- **Worker service env**: `DOCUMENT_WORKERS_ENABLED=true` (and no `JOBS_WORKER_ENABLED`
  — it only runs the three document queues via `app.workers.document_queues`, nothing
  else).
- Anyone changing one service's worker-split flags must know the other's — they're a
  matched pair, not independently tunable. Both services also set `DOCUMENT_PIPELINE_
  VERSION=document-foundation-v3` identically; letting these drift between the two
  processes would be a real, hard-to-diagnose ingestion bug.
- `maxShutdownDelaySeconds: 300` on both services is not an arbitrary number — it's
  what makes room for the already-documented "Queue control-plane isolation and
  cancellation" behavior below: deploy shutdown drains in-flight work, then releases
  still-owned leases without consuming a retry attempt, rather than hard-killing the
  process mid-lease.

### Environment variables (names and purpose only — never fetch or paste actual values)

Every secret below is `sync: false` in `render.yaml` (Render's marker for "set this in
the dashboard, don't template it") and must stay that way — do not add real values to
this file, a `.env.example` entry beyond a placeholder, or any tool call that would
echo them: `DATABASE_URL` (the table-owning Alembic role), `APP_DATABASE_URL` (the
low-privileged RLS role — see "Authentication and multi-tenancy" above; note this env
var's mere presence doesn't confirm RLS enforcement is actually cut over, only that a
value is configured for it), `R2_ACCOUNT_ID`/`R2_ACCESS_KEY_ID`/`R2_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY`/
`R2_BUCKET_NAME`/`R2_ENDPOINT_URL`, `VOYAGE_API_KEY`, `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY`,
`JWT_SECRET_KEY`, `RFQ_MAGIC_LINK_SECRET`, `GMAIL_CLIENT_ID`/`GMAIL_CLIENT_SECRET`/
`GMAIL_TOKEN_ENCRYPTION_SECRET` (web service only — the worker never sends email).
Non-secret config (concurrency/pool-sizing knobs like `DB_POOL_SIZE`,
`AGENT_DB_POOL_SIZE`, `CONTROL_DB_POOL_SIZE`, `DOCUMENT_*_CONCURRENCY`) is set as plain
values in `render.yaml` and mirrors the tuning already documented throughout this file
(e.g. "Queue control-plane isolation and cancellation" below for why `CONTROL_DB_*` is
sized the way it is).

## Database state — read before touching migrations

- `.env`'s `DATABASE_URL` now points at a **fresh, dedicated Neon database for this app**
  (confirmed empty before the first migration ran). It is no longer the old reference
  project's production DB — that risk is resolved. Migrations have been applied directly
  to it via `alembic upgrade head`; the chain has grown well past the original five (initial
  schema, `add_agent_messages`, `add_project_document_chunks`, `heading_kind`/`csi_division`,
  `pg_trgm` indexes) through RFQ drafts/vendor links, agent run-id threading, RFQ email
  body/magic links, Gmail delivery, the `priced_line_items.notes`→`estimate_basis` rename,
  and now `project_spreadsheets` + `quote_submissions.lead_time_days` — check
  `alembic/versions/` directly rather than trusting a specific count here, it will drift
  again. Two migrations (the `estimate_basis` rename and the Gmail-delivery one) briefly
  forked from the same parent revision; a later merge revision
  (`f7a8b9c0d1e2`, `down_revision` as a tuple) resolved it back to a single head — if
  `alembic upgrade head` ever reports multiple heads, that's the pattern to reach for. It's
  a real, live, seeded database (two test projects, see "How this was verified") — still
  treat schema changes with the same care as any live database: prefer additive incremental
  migrations (`alembic revision --autogenerate`) over regenerating history, and don't
  drop/recreate the schema on it the way earlier verification passes did against the
  throwaway container.
- **Resolved (2026-07-19):** the shared Neon DB (project `rialto-backend`,
  `proud-sun-97778593`) is now cleanly on this branch's chain — confirmed live via
  `SELECT version_num FROM alembic_version` and `alembic current`. The
  `feature/construction-computer-agent` divergence noted below as discovered
  2026-07-16 (DB stuck at `b6c7d8e9f0a1`, a foreign head this branch's alembic
  couldn't resolve) is no longer the DB's actual state — whatever caused it isn't
  present now. `a2b3c4d5e6f7` (verification queue) was already applied. `a3b4c5d6e7f8`
  (`estimate_items.evidence`) was the one migration still pending — its absence was
  the direct cause of `propose_estimate_structure`/`categorize_estimate` failing with
  "column `estimate_items.evidence` does not exist" in chat. Applied via a plain
  `alembic upgrade head` (no merge revision needed, since the DB's `f1b2c3d4e5f6` was
  a clean, unforked direct parent of the local head) and confirmed against the live
  table schema (`evidence json NOT NULL DEFAULT '[]'::json` now present on
  `estimate_items`). `bbeb2f37dbd6` (`agent_runs.current_tool_name`/
  `current_tool_started_at`/`kind` + `jobs.run_id`, see "Live tool-call visibility and
  queued-job tracking") was applied the same way immediately after — confirmed live via
  `describe_table_schema`, including the data backfill (`UPDATE agent_runs SET
  kind='price_item' WHERE goal LIKE 'estimate.price_batch:%'`) correctly relabeling 6 of
  210 existing rows. **`alembic heads` is a single head and the shared DB matches it —
  don't assume future migrations need reconciliation without checking live first**,
  the way this one turned out not to.
  convention below.
- `.env`'s `HISTORICAL_DATA_SOURCE=synthetic` is now the intended setting — see
  "Historical data source seam" above for what it actually does (additive, not an
  either/or switch; `rialto` would mean skipping the synthetic reference tier only).
- Neon connection strings (`postgresql://...?sslmode=require&channel_binding=require`) are
  not directly usable by asyncpg. `app/db.to_asyncpg_url` strips `sslmode`/`channel_binding`
  and converts them to an asyncpg `ssl=True` connect arg, and swaps the driver to
  `+asyncpg`. Both the app engine and `alembic/env.py` go through this function — don't
  construct an engine directly from `settings.database_url`.
- Postgres native enums (`pricing_source`, `rfq_status`, `job_status`) need an explicit
  `DROP TYPE` in the migration's `downgrade()` — Alembic's autogenerate does not emit this
  for `op.drop_table()`, so re-running `upgrade` after a `downgrade` fails with "type already
  exists" unless it's added by hand. The current initial migration already has this fix;
  keep it if you regenerate.
- All ad-hoc `datetime` columns (not from `TimestampMixin`) must use
  `mapped_column(DateTime(timezone=True))` explicitly — a bare `Mapped[datetime]` creates a
  naive-timestamp column, which breaks the moment app code passes a timezone-aware
  `datetime.now(UTC)` value (asyncpg raises `can't subtract offset-naive and offset-aware
  datetimes`). This bit `historical_prices.priced_at`, `agent_runs.started_at/completed_at`,
  `jobs.run_after/locked_at`, and `quote_submissions.received_at` during development —
  watch for the same mistake on any new datetime column.

## How this was verified

The repository has both pure/fake-session tests and real PostgreSQL integration
tests. CI provisions fresh pgvector PostgreSQL, creates `rialto_app`, applies Alembic
and runs pytest; see `.github/workflows/ci.yml` for the actual commands. Local
integration flags must point only to a verified disposable target. A skipped test is
not a pass, a fake-session test is not transaction proof, and fixture-backed browser
tests are not live provider/storage/worker evidence. Record the exact checkout,
commands, pass/skip/xfail counts and resource identities in the evidence ledger.
Run Gauntlet fast during work and survivor before acceptance. Never disable a gate
to obtain a green result. Provider-driven changes also need the relevant real
isolated flow; preserve the distinction between verified and expected behavior.

**If you touch** `app/core/pricing/service.py`, `app/core/pricing/provider.py`,
`app/core/rfq/service.py`, `app/core/audit.py`, `app/core/ingestion/`,
`app/core/project/document_catalog.py`, `app/core/project/readiness.py`,
`app/core/jobs/service.py`, `app/core/verification/kinds.py`, `app/api/projects.py`,
`app/core/price_book/`, or anything under `app/core/agent/` or `app/core/estimates/`,
re-verify the same way before trusting it — `pytest` alone won't catch DB-level,
parsing, or LLM-tool-choice regressions here.

## Reference repo (context, not a dependency)

Early tool/schema design was informed by skimming `UseRialto/rialto-ai-for-estimating`
(the `rr-main` branch) — a large, tangled TypeScript/Python monorepo for what looks like an
earlier iteration of this same product idea. Nothing was ported: no code, no exact schema,
no architecture. What carried over was *intent*: pricing material/labor as separate lookups,
historical matching that rejects on unit/label mismatch instead of forcing a price, and RFQ
send being explicitly approval-gated/async. The `DATABASE_URL` and `SYNTHETIC_DATABASE_URL`
credentials in `.env` are inherited from that project (see "Database state" above for why
that matters) — this backend is a fresh implementation, not a continuation of that repo.

## Open follow-ups

- **Production moved to Railway; nothing in the repo describes the live Railway
  environment yet** — see "Production deployment (Railway) — current" above. The old
  `render.yaml`-drift follow-up that used to sit here is obsolete (Render is retired;
  consider deleting `render.yaml` outright). What replaced it: document the actual
  Railway topology/start commands/env vars once the Railway CLI or MCP connector is
  available, and decide whether `app/config.py` should grow a Railway-triggered
  production-contract gate the way the now-inert `if self.render:` block was — until
  then, no env var (including `ADMIN_URL_SECRET`/`ADMIN_PASSWORD`) is enforced at boot
  in production.
- **The MCP tool catalog, "Historical data source seam", and "Parallel pricing
  execution" sections describe a substantially retired v1 tool surface** — see "Live
  MCP tool registry (authoritative)" near the top of this file, which lists the real
  current 41-tool registry and flags this explicitly rather than silently leaving it
  wrong. A full rewrite of those sections (in particular the ~280-line MCP tool catalog)
  was deliberately not attempted in the same pass that added the correction banner —
  the risk of destroying accurate design-history prose while chasing every stale tool
  name outweighed doing it under time pressure. Worth a dedicated pass.
- `get_document_outline` is confirmed dead with no replacement — whole-document
  table-of-contents browsing is a real capability gap today, not a renamed tool. See
  the correction in "Current document ingestion and retrieval foundation" at the top
  of this file.
- The Assembly recipe library (`app/core/assemblies/`, `app/api/assemblies.py`,
  `docs/assemblies.md`) has creation/publish/retire/search implemented and verified on
  a Neon dev branch, but is **not yet migrated to production, has no MCP tools (not
  agent-facing at all), and has no "recipe expansion" step** — nothing applies a
  published `AssemblyVersion` into actual `EstimateItem`/`EstimateCostComponent` rows
  yet, despite `estimate_version_assembly_instances` already reserving the lineage FK
  for it. Also unresolved: `Assembly`/`AssemblyVersion` have no `user_id` column at all
  (only an unused nullable `org_id`) — confirm whether this is intentional (a shared,
  company-wide library) or a gap that reintroduces the exact bug `3698854` fixed
  elsewhere, before this system reaches production traffic. See "Estimating domain
  model"'s correction above.
- Agent memory ("Agent memory" above) has not run `alembic upgrade head` against the
  shared/production DB yet — a required, separate step before this branch's
  application code deploys (see that section's own note on why). No consolidation/
  summarization job exists for when the active-memory cap is approached, and no live
  LLM turn has exercised a real model choosing to call write_memory/list_memories/
  forget_memory on its own judgment end-to-end.
- Price book PDF ingestion ("Price book PDF ingestion" above) covers materials only —
  labor import has no PDF path yet — and runs the tier-2 LLM extraction inline in the
  request/response cycle rather than as a background job. Extending to labor is a
  straightforward repeat of the same `_load_table`/two-tier pattern; moving tier 2 to
  a job (matching `vendor_quote.extract`) is worth it if real catalogs turn out larger
  or slower than the samples this pass tested against. **`POST /materials/import` is
  still inline for the same reason and remains the larger of the two gaps** — a
  3,000-row file's *write* alone measured ~28s before any extraction, and unlike the
  unit-gap resolve below its body is an uploaded file, which cannot go into a JSON job
  payload; backgrounding it needs an upload-staging step first.
- **Unit-gap resolve is now queueable** (`price_book.unit_gaps.resolve`, `POST
  .../unit-gaps/resolve/start` + `GET /api/jobs/{job_id}`; frontend contract in
  `docs/price-book-unit-gaps-frontend-handoff.md`). The synchronous route is unchanged
  and still capped, with one deliberate contract change: an over-cap answer is now that
  answer's own `status="invalid"` rather than a whole-request 422, so it can never raise
  out of the job handler and hand `record_failure` a bare string. Measured live at
  3,137 rows: 28s synchronous, 37s as a job, extrapolating to ~240s for a full
  20,000-row replay — comfortably inside the 1200s worker timeout, so no separate
  job-path cap was added. **Still open**: the job holds the single concurrency-1 default
  slot in the web process while it runs, delaying `rfq.send` and friends (a dedicated
  queue is the fix); there is no checkpointing, so a failed attempt replays from row
  zero (safe, since replay is idempotent, but wasteful); and two jobs answering
  different unit strings that resolve to the same batch unit could race on
  `ensure_unit_alias` and raise `IntegrityError` — defused today only by that same
  concurrency of 1, and it needs a per-answer savepoint to fix properly.
- The `categorize_estimate` directive's research-then-draft restructure ("Document
  research pipeline hardening" above) has not been run live end-to-end since the
  rewrite landed — the pre-commit probe that validated finding quality used
  hand-written questions, not a real agent turn's own judgment about what to ask
  before it has done any deep reading. Run a live `categorize_estimate` turn (ideally
  against "Jack test 2" again, the same project the original bypass was found on) and
  check: research is actually called before the first `patch_estimate_draft`; the
  question batch is well-formed; direct `read_project_document_section`/
  `inspect_drawing_sheet` calls, if any, are narrated escalations tied to a named
  finding, not the default path again.
- The document-research call budget (one batch + one validation follow-up, per
  `categorize_estimate` step 4) remains unenforced in code — a directive convention
  only, same as it was before this pass. If it needs to be a hard guarantee, that's
  future work, same note as the pre-existing "prompt convention, not enforced" caveat
  this pass otherwise cleaned up.
- PDF takeoff support is still deliberately out of scope (CSV/XLSX/XLS are supported;
  see "Takeoff column mapping (LLM fallback)") — a real product ask, not implemented
  yet. The table-layout fallback's architecture (structural coordinates only, never a
  transcribed value) would need a different retrieval step for a PDF (text/table
  extraction rather than a worksheet grid) but the same safety invariant should apply.
- Assembly propagation from unitless header rows (same section) only runs in the main
  deterministic/column-mapping row loop — the table-layout fallback's own row
  extraction (`_rows_from_table_region`, `app/core/ingestion/parsers.py`) doesn't track
  group context yet. Low priority unless a real file is found that both needs tier 2
  *and* has this same interleaved-header-row structure; neither real file tested so far
  needed tier 2 to engage at all.
- Automatic inbound RFQ-quote webhook/inbox handler (`quote_submissions` → a price the
  moment a vendor reply arrives, without an estimator manually applying it). Should
  target `write_estimate_component_price` when the project has an active Estimate
  (the normal case now), not the legacy `write_priced_line` path — whoever builds this
  should follow the same routing `app/core/spreadsheets/service.py`'s Apply-Pricing
  action already does (Estimate materials first, legacy takeoff fallback only when no
  Estimate exists). The manual equivalent — an estimator selecting a vendor in the
  quote comparison spreadsheet and applying it — already exists (see "Quote comparison
  spreadsheet").
- OCR/scanned-PDF/image handling for project specs — spec ingestion only extracts text
  from text-bearing files, stores full text in `project_documents.text_content`, and stores deterministic
  page/section chunks in `project_document_chunks` (each classified with `heading_kind`/
  `csi_division` — see MCP tool catalog). Vector/embedding retrieval is deliberately not
  planned unless a concrete lexical-search failure is found — see the `search_project_documents`
  entry in the MCP tool catalog for why.
- Company-wide intelligence / cross-project memory tools were explicitly deferred out of
  this pass's scope.
- Guardrails/pricing-flow policy for freeform chat still live only in the system prompt
  (`app/core/agent/orchestrator.py`) — no code-level enforcement that the agent actually
  refuses off-domain requests or always writes/queues a price rather than just stating a
  number. `categorize_estimate`'s and the pricing-batch subagent's tool exclusions (see
  "Estimate categorization skill" / "Parallel pricing execution") are the code-level
  exceptions so far, each scoped only to their own turn.
- Direct quote imports do not resolve `vendor_contact_id`; `VendorQuoteLineItem` preserves
  the extracted vendor name and vendor consolidation is an explicit estimator correction.
- `takeoff_column_mapping` verification requests only support `accept`/`dismiss`; a
  `remap` decision (user corrects the header mapping, rows re-parse) is the one genuinely
  new resolver behavior deferred out of the initial queue implementation.
- The old `.../grouping-review-suggestions` endpoints are compatibility wrappers now —
  delete them once the frontend reads `GET /api/projects/{id}/verifications` instead.
- The raw `source_takeoff_line_item_ids` list field (on `EstimateItemIn`/
  `EstimateComponentIn`, distinct from agent-only `source_row_mappings`) remains a
  quantity-blind loophole — multiple items/components can reference the same takeoff row
  through it with no allocation accounting, unlike the new `source_row_mappings` quantity
  conservation check (see "Estimating domain model"). Closing it for real (folding it into
  the same conservation check, or deprecating the raw field for agent proposals entirely)
  is a deliberate follow-up, not fixed by this pass.
- `write_priced_line` (`app/core/pricing/`) and `write_estimate_component_price`
  (`app/core/estimates/`) are two independent writers that both promote into
  `historical_prices` — `estimates/service.py`'s `_promote_price` builds the row directly
  instead of going through `RialtoHistoricalProvider.record_price`
  (`app/core/pricing/provider.py`). The two have already diverged in behavior (only
  `_promote_price` has an idempotency guard). Reconcile by having `estimates/` call the
  shared provider instead of reimplementing the insert. **Partially addressed**: the
  project-scoped historical-search tier no longer reads the wrong table for this (see
  `EstimateHistoricalProvider`, "Historical data source seam") — what's still open is
  purely the double-insert into the *company-wide* `historical_prices` tier itself.
- Pricing math within a component is still just "call `write_estimate_component_price`
  with your conclusion" — the actual labor-productivity mechanics and how a material's
  price gets selected *within* its Estimate Item's specific context (the "price after
  categorizing, not before" ordering) live entirely in the pricing subagent's prompt
  (`PRICE_ITEM_SYSTEM_PROMPT_TEMPLATE`), not in any code-level calculation helper beyond
  `calculate_price`'s two pricing-method cases. Deeper pricing-math tooling (if the prompt
  alone proves insufficient) is a deliberate future pass, not done here.
- The parallel pricing batch (`estimate.price_batch`, "Parallel pricing execution") has
  not been verified against a live Postgres + real worker + real LLM calls — only
  ruff/import/pytest (fake-session and pure-logic tests) this pass. Run the same manual
  throwaway-Postgres pass described in "How this was verified" before trusting it in
  production, especially the connection-pool-under-concurrency and per-item-failure-
  isolation behavior.
- Web search is not logged in `AgentMessageOut.tool_calls` — a deliberate decision
  (confirmed with the user), not an oversight: Anthropic/OpenAI execute it server-side
  and it never passes through `execute_tool`, so making it appear would mean a second,
  after-the-fact write path into `tool_calls` with no real latency number and no live
  current-tool visibility (the search already happened by the time we'd see it in the
  response). If this gap becomes a real problem, revisit by detecting
  `server_tool_use`/`web_search_tool_result` blocks (Claude) or `web_search_call` items
  (OpenAI) in the provider loop and logging a synthetic row with `latency_ms=None`.
- `Job` has no FK to the `AgentRun`s *it spawns* — only `Job.run_id` (which
  AgentRun *queued* it, see "Live tool-call visibility and queued-job tracking") exists
  now. Without the reverse link, `GET /jobs/{job_id}` on an `estimate.price_batch` job
  gives "batch running/done" but not "3 of 5 items priced" — each
  `price_one_estimate_item` subagent's own `AgentRun.id` is already returned in that
  job's `result.outcomes[].run_id` once the job succeeds (see `pricing_batch.py`), but
  there's no way to see per-item progress *while* the job is still running. A real
  column (or a join table, if a job can ever spawn runs across more than one batch)
  is the fix if that granularity turns out to matter in practice.

## graphify

This project has a knowledge graph at graphify-out/ with god nodes, community structure, and cross-file relationships.

Rules:
- For codebase questions, first run `graphify query "<question>"` when graphify-out/graph.json exists. Use `graphify path "<A>" "<B>"` for relationships and `graphify explain "<concept>"` for focused concepts. These return a scoped subgraph, usually much smaller than GRAPH_REPORT.md or raw grep output.
- If graphify-out/wiki/index.md exists, use it for broad navigation instead of raw source browsing.
- Read graphify-out/GRAPH_REPORT.md only for broad architecture review or when query/path/explain do not surface enough context.
- After modifying code, run `graphify update .` to keep the graph current (AST-only, no API cost).


## Required instruction source: /Users/tomasz/.no-mistakes/worktrees/4019102881cf/01M2BJC44MJV1HEJ1P50949S5A/config/rialto-validation-sources/rialto-backend/AGENTS.md
SHA-256: 79014b8a2c13b7ce5ef5142a4cc8936507616f3bf3cdbdca9b9650c2cbc9d6e3

# Agent Instructions

Refer to `CLAUDE.md` for the project instructions and conventions before making changes in this repository.

## Document and ingestion UX

- Create enters the Agent workspace immediately; project saving, uploads and processing continue in the account-scoped background coordinator.
- Keep file/folder selection, navigation and reading available during active work. Append overlapping submissions under one bounded concurrency budget.
- Preserve local originals, account/project-scoped caches and unsaved review drafts across route changes. Clear sensitive caches on logout/account change.
- Acknowledge Upload and Accept immediately, but never label an operation saved/accepted before durable confirmation. Reconcile lost responses using stable action IDs.
- Saved, viewable, searchable and review accepted are separate capabilities. Reading originals does not depend on ingestion or annotation readiness.
- Needs Review requires a specific unresolved review request, an understandable reason and a working action. Provider/configuration failures are operational recovery issues, not content reviews.
- Retry transient failures with bounded, duplicate-safe recovery. Preserve successful stages and originals; show compact truthful intervention after recovery is exhausted. Never fabricate success or conceal unsaved data.
- Status labels must remain readable with the sidebar open and on mobile. Readers support reachable horizontal/vertical scrolling and fullscreen without making fullscreen mandatory.

## Reviewer-facing delivery

- Amend the existing linked PRs and retain source provenance. Start with a short, plain-English before/after summary of each changed feature.
- Use neutral project names and task-based headings, not dated checkpoint headings or personal test-project names.
- Give a human or browser-using agent numbered actions, exact fixture filenames with verified GitHub links, and expected results. Do not assume access to a local Desktop folder or an existing personal project.
- Keep actual test results and genuine limitations distinct from expected behavior. Replace stale walkthroughs instead of accumulating contradictory checkpoint summaries.
- Keep exact timestamps, environment identities and private test-project IDs in the evidence ledger where needed for reproducibility; never expose credentials.

<!-- rialto-development-context:begin -->
## Rialto product: mandatory development context

Applies whenever the task concerns Rialto product, including its frontend/backend,
UseRialto repositories, clones/worktrees, and work coordinated through Gastown or
Firstmate, even when the current directory is outside the product workspace.

Before planning, implementation, review, or delegation, read the full applicable
repo `CLAUDE.md` and `AGENTS.md`. For cross-repo work, read both CLAUDE files.
Resolve them from the actual checkout being changed; canonical local references:
- `/Users/tomasz/Desktop/rialto/product/rialto-backend/CLAUDE.md`
- `/Users/tomasz/Desktop/rialto/product/rialto-frontend/CLAUDE.md`
- `/Users/tomasz/Desktop/rialto/product/AGENTS.md`
Do not treat these as optional background or defer reading until after coding.
If a sandbox cannot access them, use a verified source copy with its path/hash;
if neither is available, report the missing context before dependent work.

These baseline standards must be present in the working prompt:
- Backend is Python, managed with `uv` (`uv add`/`uv remove`, never pip/Poetry/Conda),
  FastAPI/Uvicorn, Pydantic, async SQLAlchemy/asyncpg, and Alembic. Use pytest and
  Ruff. Do not introduce a TypeScript/Node backend or product UI in the backend repo.
- Business logic and database access belong in backend `app/core/`; REST routes
  and MCP handlers are thin callers sharing that logic. Core services and job
  handlers do not commit; the owning request/audit/worker boundary commits.
- Product agents get named MCP tools with typed schemas and precise descriptions,
  never raw SQL/ORM access. Long work is queued with explicit retry/failure states;
  preserve price sources and evidence. Do not duplicate tool registries or logic.
- Frontend is Vite + React + TypeScript, pnpm, Tailwind/shadcn, and TanStack Query
  for backend state. No direct datastore access or pricing/matching/RFQ business
  logic. Structured data comes from REST/artifact schemas, never chat-prose parsing.
- Read frontend `CONTRACT.md` before changing API consumers. Use documented shapes;
  update a stale contract before types/consumers, and flag missing endpoints instead
  of inventing data or workarounds. Contract changes retain human review gates.
- Verify affected database, worker, ingestion, and agent flows against isolated
  resources; pytest alone is not integration proof. Run Gauntlet fast during work
  and survivor before acceptance; preserve hooks, protected paths, and human gates.
- Current user instructions and product safety/UX rules override historical notes
  in CLAUDE files. Shared/production databases stay read-only; retain test databases,
  projects, and storage until Tomasz confirms deletion of the exact resources.

Every Rialto dispatch, worker brief, review brief, handoff, and compaction summary
must preserve these standards and name the exact source CLAUDE/AGENTS paths.
Coordinators must include the standards in the child prompt, not merely say
"follow repo conventions" or assume children inherit the parent's context.
Read the sources again after switching checkout or losing them through compaction.

Load this checkout’s full `CLAUDE.md` directly. Resolve the paired repository from
the task’s actual checkout paths or the verified canonical references above.
Do not assume sibling directory names in an isolated worktree.

